            title: `Fundamentals of Constitution & Governance`,
            articles: `Constitutional Concepts · Acts of 1773–1947`,
            summary: `A constitution turns power into rules; India's journey runs from the 1773 Regulating Act through the 1935 Act and the 1947 Independence Act.`,
            story: `Before 1773, the British East India Company operated as a ruthless commercial monopoly until rampant corruption and financial collapse forced the British Parliament to intervene with the Regulating Act of 1773. Over the next 170 years, every major uprising and royal charter chipped away at unchecked power, culminating in the Government of India Act 1935—the administrative bedrock of modern federalism. A constitution was forged not out of academic theory, but out of a colonized people's urgent need to permanently bind arbitrary power with transparent rules.`,
            storyLink: `Realizing that alien masters could never craft fair rules, Indian nationalists began demanding an elected body of their own, sparking the historic struggle for a Constituent Assembly.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Definition &amp; Core Ideals:</strong> A constitution lays out the ideals forming the basis of a nation and distinguishes democratic governance from arbitrary rule; constitutional governance means power is exercised according to established law.`,
              `<strong>Tradition Origins:</strong> The modern written constitution tradition began in the USA with the Constitution of 1787; the USA declared independence on 4 July 1776.`,
              `<strong>Forms of Government:</strong> Communist (means of production controlled by government, e.g., China); Oligarchic (ruled by a few elites, e.g., China, Russia); Monarchic (ruled by a single monarch or king, e.g., Saudi Arabia, UK); Totalitarian (total control over citizens' lives, e.g., North Korea); Democratic (power held by the people and exercised through elected representatives).`,
              `<strong>Regulating Act 1773:</strong> First parliamentary control over the East India Company; created the Governor-General of Bengal (Lord Warren Hastings) and established a Supreme Court at Calcutta in 1774.`,
              `<strong>Charter Acts of 1833 &amp; 1853:</strong> The 1833 Act elevated Lord William Bentinck as Governor-General of India; the 1853 Act separated legislative and executive functions and introduced open civil service competition.`,
