            story: `When British leaders arrogantly mocked Indians as incapable of drafting their own laws, Motilal Nehru answered in 1928 with the historic Nehru Report—India's first constitutional prototype. In 1934, radical thinker M.N. Roy formally conceived the idea of a sovereign Constituent Assembly, which the Congress officially championed in 1935 and Nehru cemented in 1938 by demanding full adult franchise without outside meddling. Cornered by World War II, the desperate British dangled half-hearted offers like the August Offer and Cripps Mission, but Indians refused to settle for second-class dominion status.`,
