        story: `The Constitution then reveals its personality: long, flexible, federal, parliamentary, and borrowing the best of many systems.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `2.1`,
            title: `Salient Structural Features of the Constitution`,
            articles: `System Overview`,
            summary: `India's Constitution is long, flexible yet rigid, federal with a strong unitary tilt, parliamentary in spirit, and built on adult franchise.`,
            story: `Faced with immense continental diversity, caste divides, and communal wounds, the framers rejected a simple, brief constitution like America's and chose to write the world's longest, most detailed constitution. They crafted a unique hybrid: flexible enough to adapt through ordinary amendments, yet rigid enough to protect federalism; federal in structure during calm times, but instantly unitary in national emergencies. They boldly granted universal adult franchise from day one, trusting an illiterate rural population with the vote—a democratic leap that wealthier Western nations took centuries to grant.`,
            storyLink: `To build such an intricate constitutional machine, the framers searched global democracies to borrow the finest battle-tested mechanisms in human history.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Lengthiest Written Constitution:</strong> Expanded over time from 395 Articles / 8 Schedules / 22 Parts (1949) to over 470 Articles / 12 Schedules / 25 Parts today.`,
              `<strong>Blend of Rigidity &amp; Flexibility:</strong> Combines simple majority laws, special parliamentary majority (2/3rd present &amp; voting + majority of total strength), and federal ratification by half the states.`,
              `<strong>Federal System with Unitary Bias:</strong> Features dual polity and written supremacy, yet retains strong Centre traits — single constitution, single citizenship, integrated judiciary, All-India Services, and emergency powers.`,
              `<strong>Parliamentary Government &amp; Adult Franchise:</strong> Executive is collectively responsible to the Lower House; the 61st Amendment Act (1988) lowered voting age from 21 to 18 years.`,
              `<strong>Separation of Powers:</strong> The Constitution distributes powers among the legislature, executive, and judiciary, but India follows a parliamentary fusion of the executive and legislature rather than the strict American separation; judicial independence and review provide the counterbalance.`
            ],
            landmark: `K.C. Wheare described India as "quasi-federal", while Granville Austin called it "cooperative federalism".`,
            takeaway: `India balances Parliamentary Sovereignty (UK) with Judicial Supremacy (USA) through constitutional supremacy.`
          },
          {
            code: `2.2`,
