            summary: `Schedules 7 to 12 carry the three legislative lists, 22 languages, anti-defection, land reform, panchayats, municipalities, and urban local bodies.`,
            story: `As the Indian democracy matured, the remaining Schedules became battlegrounds of federal power, linguistic pride, and democratic integrity. Schedule 7 divides governing authority into Union, State, and Concurrent Lists to prevent endless jurisdictional wars; Schedule 8 protects 22 recognized languages reflecting our civilizational mosaic; and Schedule 9 was born in 1951 to shield revolutionary land reforms from court vetoes. Decades later, the 10th Schedule (52nd CAA 1985) banned opportunistic horse-trading by politicians ('Aaya Ram, Gaya Ram'), while Schedules 11 and 12 (73rd/74th CAAs 1992) granted constitutional dignity to Panchayats and Municipalities.`,
            storyLink: `Before diving into the technical mechanics of territory and governance, the founders wrote a grand opening preface capturing the moral soul of this entire constitutional edifice—the Preamble.`,
