            summary: `Jurists call the Preamble the identity card, preface, and soul of the Constitution, but it is not an independent source of power.`,
            story: `Legal titans across the globe have revered the Indian Preamble for its compressed brilliance: N.A. Palkhivala famously termed it the 'Identity Card of the Constitution,' while Sir Alladi Krishnaswami Iyer hailed it as an expression of 'what we had thought or dreamt so long.' Pandit Thakur Das Bhargava called it the 'soul, key, and jewel of the Constitution,' while Ernest Barker reprinted it on the opening page of his treatise. Yet beneath these poetic accolades lay a strict legal reality: the Preamble grants no independent executive power and cannot override clear statutory text in a courtroom.`,
            storyLink: `To truly understand this constitutional jewel, we must dissect the two monumental halves of its text: the nature of the Indian State and the promises made to its citizens.`,
