            story: `The Preamble is constructed with five pillars describing what India IS, and four pillars describing what India PROMISES. India is declared Sovereign (beholden to no foreign power), Socialist (democratic welfare reducing wealth inequality), Secular (no state religion; equal respect for all faiths), Democratic (ruled by elected representatives), and a Republic (the head of state is an elected citizen, not a hereditary monarch). In return, the State commits to four sacred ideals: Justice (social, economic, political), Liberty of thought and faith, Equality of status and opportunity, and Fraternity assuring individual dignity and national unity.`,
            storyLink: `But if Parliament commands the power to amend laws, could a political regime rewrite or erase these sacred foundational words? That triggered a historic judicial showdown.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Source of Authority:</strong> "We, the people of India" affirms popular sovereignty — the Constitution derives its authority from the people.`,
              `<strong>Nature of the State:</strong> Sovereign (independent internal and external authority); Socialist (added by the 42nd CAA 1976; democratic/mixed socialism leaning towards Gandhian socialism); Secular (positive secularism respecting all religions equally); Democratic (government by consent, associated with John Locke and Lincoln's "of the people, by the people, for the people"); Republic (elected Head of State, not a hereditary monarch).`,
              `<strong>Objectives — Justice:</strong> Social, economic, and political justice, inspired by the Russian Revolution of 1917 and secured through Fundamental Rights and DPSP.`,
              `<strong>Objectives — Liberty, Equality &amp; Fraternity:</strong> Liberty of thought, expression, belief, faith, and worship; equality of status and opportunity; fraternity assuring individual dignity and the unity and integrity of the nation. Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity are inspired by the French Revolution of 1789.`,
              `<strong>42nd Amendment Additions:</strong> The Preamble was amended only once, by the 42nd CAA 1976, which added Socialist, Secular, and Integrity.`
            ],
            landmark: `The Preamble's ideals draw from multiple revolutions: Justice from Russia (1917), and Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity from France (1789).`,
            takeaway: `Use the SSI trick for the three words added in 1976: Socialist, Secular, Integrity.`
