            story: `In the 1960 Berubari Union case, the Supreme Court took a narrow view, ruling that the Preamble was merely a decorative preface and not part of the Constitution at all. That dangerous doctrine was shattered thirteen years later in the landmark Kesavananda Bharati case (1973), where the 13-judge bench reversed Berubari and declared that the Preamble is an integral, inseparable part of the Constitution. Parliament can amend it under Article 368—as it did in the 42nd Amendment of 1976 by adding 'Socialist, Secular, and Integrity'—provided the Basic Structure is never damaged.`,
            storyLink: `With this philosophical vision etched in stone, the Constitution organizes its massive blueprint into a systematic master sequence of thematic Parts.`,
