              `<strong>LIC of India Case (1995):</strong> Reaffirmed that the Preamble is an integral part of the Constitution.`,
              `<strong>Enforceability:</strong> The Preamble is non-justiciable; it is neither a source of legislative power nor a prohibition on legislative power.`,
              `<strong>Amendment History:</strong> The Preamble has been amended only once — by the 42nd CAA 1976, which inserted Socialist, Secular, and Integrity.`
            ],
            landmark: `Berubari (1960: not part) -> Kesavananda Bharati (1973: part and amendable subject to Basic Structure) -> LIC (1995: reaffirmed).`,
            takeaway: `The Preamble can be amended, but not in a way that destroys the Basic Structure.`
          },
          {
            code: `3.5`,
            title: `Master Table: 22 Parts of the Constitution`,
            articles: `Parts I–XXII`,
            summary: `The 22 original Parts, now 25 after deletions and additions, are a map from the Union to emergency, amendments, and temporary provisions.`,
            story: `To govern a subcontinent, the framers organized the Constitution like a logical manual of 22 original Parts, which has expanded to 25 Parts today through key amendments. The logical flow is breathtakingly intuitive: first define the nation's physical boundary (Part I) and who belongs to it (Part II); then grant citizens inalienable rights (Part III) and establish state duties (Part IV & IVA); next build the central government (Part V), the state governments (Part VI), local councils (Part IX & IXA), and judicial umpires; and finally provide emergency brakes (Part XVIII) and amendment tools (Part XX).`,
            storyLink: `The very first practical task of any newborn country is to demarcate its borders and establish its territorial identity—the exact mandate of Part I.`,
            points: [
