              `<strong>Integration of 562 of 565 Princely States:</strong> 562 acceded through the Instrument of Accession via Sardar Patel and V.P. Menon; the three reluctant states were Jammu &amp; Kashmir (Instrument of Accession, 26 October 1947), Junagadh (referendum, February 1948), and Hyderabad (Operation Polo, September 1948).`,
              `<strong>Dhar Commission (1948) &amp; JVP Committee (1948):</strong> Both rejected language as the primary basis for state reorganization, favoring administrative convenience.`,
              `<strong>First Linguistic State (1953):</strong> Following Potti Sriramulu's 56-day fast, Andhra State was carved out of Madras in October 1953.`,
              `<strong>Fazl Ali Commission (1953–1955):</strong> Accepted language broadly (rejecting "one language, one state"); led to the 7th Constitutional Amendment and States Reorganisation Act (1956), creating 14 States and 6 Union Territories.`
