            articles: `Art. 5–11`,
            summary: `Articles 5 to 11 set out citizenship at the commencement of the Constitution and leave Parliament free to regulate citizenship by law.`,
            story: `As millions of shell-shocked refugees crossed burning borders during the blood-soaked Partition of 1947, the Constituent Assembly faced an agonizing humanitarian emergency: who gets citizenship? Articles 5 to 8 set immediate, pragmatic criteria—granting citizenship by domicile, to refugees who migrated from Pakistan before or after July 19, 1948, and to those who briefly migrated to Pakistan but returned under resettlement permits. Crucially, Article 9 upheld single citizenship, ruling that anyone acquiring foreign citizenship automatically forfeits Indian nationality.`,
            storyLink: `Knowing they could not anticipate future geopolitical migrations, the framers deliberately used Article 11 to pass the legislative torch to Parliament, leading to the Citizenship Act of 1955.`,
            points: [
