              `<strong>Articles 5 to 8 (Commencement Scope):</strong> Identified citizens at 26 January 1950 — by domicile (Art. 5), migrants from Pakistan (Art. 6), migrants to Pakistan who returned for permanent resettlement (Art. 7), and persons of Indian origin residing abroad (Art. 8).`,
              `<strong>Article 9 (Foreign Citizenship):</strong> Voluntary acquisition of citizenship of any foreign country automatically terminates Indian citizenship.`,
              `<strong>Articles 10 &amp; 11:</strong> Continuance of citizenship rights (Art. 10) and plenary power of Parliament to regulate acquisition and termination of citizenship by law (Art. 11).`
            ],
            landmark: `Constitutional Articles 5–8 dealt only with who became a citizen on 26 January 1950; ongoing rules rest with Parliament under Article 11.`,
            takeaway: `Voluntarily acquiring a foreign passport triggers automatic loss of Indian citizenship under Article 9.`
          },
          {
            code: `4.4`,
            title: `Citizenship Act, 1955: Acquisition, Loss & OCI`,
            articles: `Act of 1955`,
            summary: `The Citizenship Act, 1955 provides birth, descent, registration, naturalisation, and incorporation, while OCI cards offer limited overseas rights.`,
            story: `Article 11 gave Parliament total authority to govern citizenship after 1950, resulting in the Citizenship Act, 1955. The statute laid down five clear paths to acquire citizenship: Birth, Descent, Registration, Naturalization, and Incorporation of Territory (like Goa or Sikkim). It also established three ways to lose citizenship: Renunciation (giving it up voluntarily), Termination (taking foreign citizenship), and Deprivation (stripped by government for disloyalty or fraud). To reconnect with millions of diaspora Indians who had taken foreign passports, India later introduced the Overseas Citizen of India (OCI) card—granting lifelong travel and economic rights, but strictly withholding voting rights and public office.`,
            storyLink: `Even as citizenship stabilized, regional aspirations and population shifts continuously sparked demands to create new administrative units and reorganise states chronologically.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Five Modes of Acquisition:</strong> (1) By Birth (subject to cut-off rules of 1950, 1987, and 2004), (2) By Descent, (3) By Registration (7 years' ordinary residence for PIOs/spouses), (4) By Naturalisation (12 years' residence, reduced to 5 years for specified categories under CAA 2019), and (5) By Incorporation of Territory (e.g., Pondicherry, Goa, Sikkim).`,
              `<strong>Three Modes of Loss:</strong> (1) Renunciation (voluntary declaration by an adult), (2) Termination (automatic upon acquiring foreign citizenship), and (3) Deprivation (compulsory termination by the Union Government for fraud, disloyalty, or 7 years' continuous absence abroad).`,
              `<strong>Overseas Citizen of India (OCI):</strong> Merged PIO and OCI schemes in 2015; grants lifelong multiple-entry visa and parity in economic/educational fields, but carries no voting rights or constitutional offices.`
            ],
            landmark: `The Citizenship Act, 1955 has been amended several times (1986, 1992, 2003, 2005, 2015, and 2019).`,
            takeaway: `Naturalized citizens hold full constitutional equality for offices such as President of India, unlike in the USA.`
