            story: `India's internal map was never frozen in concrete; it evolved through decades of democratic accommodation and regional pride. Following the 1956 reorganisation, Bombay was split into Gujarat and Maharashtra in 1960; Nagaland was carved out in 1963 to calm tribal insurgency; Punjab, Haryana, and Himachal were bifurcated in 1966; while the Northeast was reconfigured into Meghalaya, Manipur, and Tripura in 1972, and Goa achieved full statehood in 1987. In 2000, three massive states were trimmed to create Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand, and Jharkhand, followed by Telangana in 2014, and the historic reorganization of Jammu & Kashmir into two Union Territories in 2019.`,
