            story: `Having lived through centuries of colonial oppression where the government could confiscate property, ban meetings, and jail dissenters at will, India's framers dedicated Part III as India's 'Magna Carta.' To prevent the government from evading these rights, Article 12 broadly defines the 'State' to include Parliament, state legislatures, local panchayats, and all statutory authorities like LIC and ONGC. Article 13 acts as the watchdog of democracy, boldly declaring that any law that violates Fundamental Rights is void from its inception.`,
            storyLink: `With this powerful judicial shield in place, the Constitution begins its catalog of rights with the foundational prerequisite of all civilized life: absolute equality before the law.`,
