            title: `Right to Equality (Articles 14–18)`,
            articles: `Art. 14–18`,
            summary: `Articles 14 to 18 guarantee equality before law, ban discrimination, open public employment, abolish untouchability, and restrict titles.`,
            story: `In a feudal society plagued by thousands of years of caste hierarchy and princely privilege, the founders used Articles 14 to 18 to level the playing field for every Indian. Article 14 commands that no person is above the law, while permitting reasonable classification to help the underprivileged; Article 15 bans discrimination on race, religion, caste, sex, or place of birth, while enabling special provisions for women and socially backward classes. Article 16 guarantees equal opportunity in public employment, Article 17 permanently outlaws the historical curse of Untouchability as a punishable crime, and Article 18 abolishes monarchical titles like Rai Bahadur.`,
            storyLink: `Equality before the law is meaningless if citizens cannot freely speak their minds, walk the streets, or live without fear of arbitrary arrest—opening the door to the Right to Freedom.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Article 14:</strong> Guarantees "Equality before Law" (negative UK concept, A.V. Dicey's Rule of Law) and "Equal Protection of Laws" (positive USA concept); reasonable classification is permitted, as explained in State of West Bengal v. Anwar Ali Sarkar (1952).`,
              `<strong>Exceptions to Article 14:</strong> The President and Governors (Art. 361), foreign diplomats and monarchs, and parliamentary privileges (Arts. 105 and 194).`,
              `<strong>Article 15:</strong> Prohibits discrimination on grounds only of religion, race, caste, sex, or place of birth; clauses (3)–(6) permit special provisions for women, children, SC/ST/SEBCs, educational institutions, and EWS (103rd CAA 2019).`,
              `<strong>Article 16:</strong> Equality of opportunity in public employment; clauses (3)–(6) cover residence, backward-class reservation, promotion reservation, carry-forward vacancies, religious offices, and EWS; Kaka Kalelkar (1953) was the first Backward Classes Commission, Mandal (1979) recommended 27% OBC reservation, and Indra Sawhney (1992) upheld the 50% ceiling and creamy-layer exclusion.`,
              `<strong>Articles 17 &amp; 18:</strong> Article 17 abolishes untouchability absolutely and is enforceable against private individuals (Protection of Civil Rights Act 1955); Article 18 abolishes hereditary titles except military and academic distinctions, and Balaji Raghavan (1996) upheld national awards as decorations, not titles.`
            ],
            landmark: `Article 17 and Article 24 are enforceable against private individuals as well as the State.`,
            takeaway: `Article 14 permits reasonable classification, not arbitrary discrimination.`
          },
          {
            code: `5.3`,
            title: `Right to Freedom (Articles 19–22)`,
            articles: `Art. 19–22`,
            summary: `Articles 19 to 22 protect speech, assembly, association, movement, residence, and profession, plus life, liberty, and detention safeguards.`,
            story: `During colonial rule, singing a patriotic song or gathering four people in a public square could get you thrown into a dark cell without a trial under draconian laws like the Rowlatt Act. When framing the Republic, our leaders vowed that independent Indians would breathe free: Article 19 gave six wings of liberty, while Article 20 barred police from punishing people retroactively. The real revolution came with Article 21: for decades, courts thought the state could take your liberty as long as a formal law existed (Gopalan 1950), until Maneka Gandhi's passport was arbitrarily seized in 1978. The Supreme Court declared that laws must not just exist—they must be fair, just, and humane.`,
            storyLink: `Yet individual liberty cannot stand alone if the vulnerable can still be bought, sold, or forced into unpaid servitude, connecting directly to the ban on exploitation in Articles 23–24.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Article 19 (Six Freedoms):</strong> Speech and expression, peaceful assembly, associations/cooperatives (97th CAA 2011), free movement, residence and settlement, and profession/trade/business; the right to property under Article 19(1)(f) was omitted by the 44th CAA 1978.`,
              `<strong>Reasonable Restrictions (Art. 19(2)):</strong> Sovereignty and integrity, security of the State, friendly relations with foreign States, public order, decency, morality, contempt of court, defamation, and incitement to an offence.`,
              `<strong>Hidden Rights under Article 19(1)(a):</strong> Freedom of the press (Romesh Thappar 1950), right to information (Raj Narain 1975; PUCL 2004), right to internet access (Anuradha Bhasin 2020), and the striking down of Section 66A of the IT Act (Shreya Singhal 2015).`,
              `<strong>Article 20 (Conviction Safeguards):</strong> Prohibits ex-post-facto criminal laws, double jeopardy, and compelled self-incrimination; thumbprints and blood samples are permitted.`,
              `<strong>Article 21 &amp; 21A:</strong> Article 21 protects life and personal liberty; A.K. Gopalan (1950) gave way to Maneka Gandhi (1978), which introduced fair, just, and reasonable procedure and the golden triangle of Articles 14, 19, and 21; Article 21A (86th CAA 2002) guarantees free and compulsory education for ages 6–14.`,
              `<strong>Article 22 (Arrest &amp; Detention):</strong> Punitive detention safeguards include grounds of arrest, consultation with a lawyer, and production before a magistrate within 24 hours; preventive detention generally requires an Advisory Board after three months.`
            ],
            landmark: `Maneka Gandhi (1978) transformed Article 21 from a narrow procedure-based guarantee into a fair, just, and reasonable due-process protection.`,
            takeaway: `Articles 20 and 21 can never be suspended, even during a National Emergency (44th CAA 1978).`
          },
          {
            code: `5.4`,
            title: `Right Against Exploitation (Articles 23–24)`,
            articles: `Art. 23–24`,
            summary: `Articles 23 and 24 ban human trafficking, forced labour, and child labour in hazardous work.`,
            story: `In traditional rural India, impoverished peasants and marginalized families were trapped for generations in begar (forced unremunerated labor) and debt bondage, while women and children were traded like commodities. The Constituent Assembly struck down this inhumanity with Article 23, making trafficking, begar, and forced labor criminal offenses enforceable against private landlords as well as the State. Alongside this, Article 24 placed an absolute prohibition on employing children under 14 years in hazardous industries, factories, or mines, laying the moral foundation for the Child Labour Prohibition Act.`,
            storyLink: `Once the physical body is liberated from bonded slavery, the Constitution turns immediately to protect the spiritual sanctuary of the soul: the freedom of conscience and faith.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Article 23:</strong> Prohibits traffic in human beings, begar, and forced or bonded labour; the State may impose compulsory public service for public purposes without discrimination.`,
              `<strong>Article 24:</strong> Prohibits employment of children below 14 years in factories, mines, or hazardous employment; the Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986 gives effect to this right.`,
              `<strong>Private Enforcement:</strong> Both Articles 23 and 24 are enforceable against private individuals as well as the State.`
            ],
            landmark: `Article 23 covers human trafficking, begar, and bonded labour; the Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act, 1976 provides statutory enforcement.`,
            takeaway: `Article 24 sets a strict age floor of 14 for hazardous employment.`
          },
          {
            code: `5.5`,
            title: `Right to Freedom of Religion (Articles 25–28)`,
            articles: `Art. 25–28`,
            summary: `Articles 25 to 28 protect conscience, free profession and practice, religious denominations, and limit state-funded religious instruction.`,
            story: `Born amidst the raging fires of communal partition, India made a conscious decision never to become a theocracy, choosing instead Sarva Dharma Sambhava (equal respect for all religions). Article 25 guarantees every individual the freedom of conscience to profess, practice, and propagate their faith peacefully, while recognizing the Sikh practice of wearing Kirpans. Article 26 empowers religious denominations to establish shrines and manage their own internal religious affairs, Article 27 forbids the State from levying taxes to promote any specific religion, and Article 28 prohibits compulsory religious indoctrination in state-funded schools.`,
            storyLink: `In a deeply diverse subcontinent, religious freedom alone was not enough; distinct cultural and linguistic minorities needed specific shields to protect their unique heritage from being erased.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Article 25:</strong> Individual freedom of conscience and the right to profess, practise, and propagate religion, subject to public order, morality, and health; forcible conversion is not protected, and the wearing of the Kirpan is recognised for Sikhs.`,
              `<strong>Article 26:</strong> Collective right of every religious denomination to establish institutions, manage its religious affairs, and own and administer property.`,
              `<strong>Article 27:</strong> The State cannot levy a tax specifically for promoting or maintaining any religion; fees for services are permitted.`,
              `<strong>Article 28:</strong> Religious instruction is completely prohibited in wholly state-funded institutions; it is permitted in institutions established under a trust and is voluntary in state-recognised or state-aided institutions.`
            ],
            landmark: `The Constitution does not define "religion" or "minority"; courts interpret these terms contextually.`,
            takeaway: `Article 25 protects individual freedom; Article 26 protects collective denominational autonomy.`
          },
          {
            code: `5.6`,
            title: `Cultural & Educational Rights (Articles 29–30)`,
            articles: `Art. 29–30`,
            summary: `Articles 29 and 30 protect minority culture, language, and script, and give minorities the right to establish and administer educational institutions.`,
            story: `For minority communities, the greatest fear in a majoritarian democracy is the slow death of their mother tongue, script, and heritage. Article 29 gives any section of citizens with a distinct language, script, or culture the absolute right to conserve it, banning discrimination in state-aided admissions. Article 30 goes even further, earning the title of the 'Charter of Minority Rights,' by giving both religious and linguistic minorities the fundamental right to establish and administer their own educational institutions without discriminatory state funding.`,
            storyLink: `All these grand declarations of equality, liberty, and cultural rights would remain empty ink on paper without an instant, powerful weapon to enforce them when violated—the supreme power of Article 32.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Article 29(1):</strong> Any section of citizens with a distinct language, script, or culture has the right to conserve it.`,
              `<strong>Article 29(2):</strong> No citizen may be denied admission to a state-aided or state-maintained educational institution on grounds only of religion, race, caste, or language.`,
              `<strong>Article 30:</strong> Religious and linguistic minorities have the right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice; the Constitution does not define "minority", and T.M.A. Pai Foundation (2002) shaped the modern interpretation.`
            ],
            landmark: `Article 29 protects any section of citizens, while Article 30 specifically protects religious and linguistic minorities.`,
            takeaway: `Article 29(2) is an individual non-discrimination guarantee; Article 30 is a collective minority-rights guarantee.`
          },
          {
            code: `5.7`,
            title: `Right to Constitutional Remedies & Writs (Articles 32–35)`,
            articles: `Art. 32–35`,
            summary: `Article 32 is the right to constitutional remedies; the five writs are habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, certiorari, and quo warranto.`,
            story: `When asked which single provision is the most critical in the entire Constitution, Dr. B.R. Ambedkar didn't hesitate: he declared Article 32 to be the 'very soul and heart of the Constitution.' Why? Because a right without a remedy is a useless illusion: Article 32 gives every ordinary citizen the direct right to knock on the doors of the Supreme Court if their Fundamental Rights are crushed. The Court can unleash five ancient prerogative writs: Habeas Corpus (produce the detained body), Mandamus (command an official to perform their duty), Prohibition and Certiorari (quash lower tribunal overreach), and Quo Warranto (challenge illegal occupation of public office).`,
            storyLink: `While Fundamental Rights tell the State what it CANNOT do to the individual, the Constitution also needed a visionary moral blueprint telling the State what it MUST do to build a just welfare society—the Directive Principles.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Articles 31, 31A, 31B &amp; 31C:</strong> Article 31 (Right to Property) was repealed by the 44th CAA 1978; Article 31A saves estate-acquisition laws; Article 31B validates Ninth Schedule laws; Article 31C gives precedence to DPSP Articles 39(b) and 39(c) over Articles 14 and 19.`,
              `<strong>Article 32:</strong> Called the "Heart and Soul of the Constitution" by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar; Article 32(1) gives direct access to the Supreme Court, and Article 32(2) empowers it to issue five writs.`,
              `<strong>Supreme Court vs. High Court:</strong> The Supreme Court's writ remedy is itself a Fundamental Right and cannot be refused; High Courts under Article 226 can issue writs for Fundamental Rights and ordinary legal rights, but their jurisdiction is discretionary and territorial.`,
              `<strong>Five Writs:</strong> Habeas Corpus (against unlawful detention), Mandamus (compels a public duty; not against the President, Governor, or private individuals), Prohibition (prevents a lower court from exceeding jurisdiction), Certiorari (quashes an order), and Quo Warranto (tests a claim to public office).`,
              `<strong>Articles 33, 34 &amp; 35:</strong> Article 33 lets Parliament restrict Fundamental Rights for the armed forces, police, and intelligence agencies; Article 34 covers martial law; Article 35 gives Parliament exclusive power to make laws enforcing Fundamental Rights.`
            ],
            landmark: `Prohibition is preventive; Certiorari is both preventive and curative; Mandamus cannot lie against the President or a Governor.`,
            takeaway: `Only Parliament, not State Legislatures, can prescribe punishments under Articles 17, 23, 33, and 34 (Article 35).`
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 6,
        unitId: 1,
        title: `DPSP & Fundamental Duties`,
        articles: `Articles 36–51A (Parts IV & IVA)`,
        summary: `Explores the non-justiciable Directive Principles of State Policy across three ideological categories, FR–DPSP judicial evolution, and the 11 Fundamental Duties under Article 51A.`,
        story: `Part IV adds the conscience: welfare goals the State should pursue, plus the duties citizens owe back to the nation.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `6.1`,
            title: `Classification of DPSP (Part IV, Articles 36–51)`,
            articles: `Art. 36–51`,
            summary: `Part IV's Directives are non-justiciable goals in three families: socialistic, Gandhian, and liberal-intellectual.`,
            story: `When India woke to freedom in 1947, the country was desperately impoverished, ill-equipped, and bankrupt—meaning the state could not immediately afford to guarantee jobs, healthcare, or free housing to every citizen as enforceable court rights. The framers solved this through a stroke of brilliance inspired by the Irish Constitution: they created Part IV (Articles 36–51) as non-justiciable Directive Principles of State Policy—a moral and ideological compass for future governments. These principles naturally split into three families: Socialistic (welfare, fair wealth distribution, living wages), Gandhian (village panchayats, cottage industries, banning alcohol), and Liberal-Intellectual (Uniform Civil Code, free legal aid, protecting monuments and international peace).`,
            storyLink: `Over the decades, tensions flared: what happens when Parliament passes a socialist welfare law that collides head-on with an individual's fundamental rights?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Nature &amp; Source:</strong> Borrowed from the Irish Constitution (1937); Dr. B.R. Ambedkar called them a "Novel Feature"; Article 37 declares them non-justiciable in courts yet fundamental in the governance of the country.`,
              `<strong>Socialistic Principles:</strong> Art. 38 (welfare state), Art. 39 (adequate livelihood, equitable wealth, equal pay for equal work), Art. 39A (free legal aid), Art. 41 (work/education/public assistance), Art. 42 (humane work &amp; maternity relief), Art. 43 (living wage), Art. 43A (worker participation), Art. 47 (nutrition &amp; public health).`,
              `<strong>Gandhian Principles:</strong> Art. 40 (organization of Village Panchayats), Art. 43 (cottage industries), Art. 43B (cooperative societies), Art. 46 (SC/ST/weaker section advancement), Art. 47 (prohibition of intoxicating drinks/drugs), Art. 48 (cow slaughter ban &amp; cattle improvement).`,
              `<strong>Liberal-Intellectual Principles:</strong> Art. 44 (Uniform Civil Code), Art. 45 (early childhood care below age 6), Art. 48 (scientific agriculture), Art. 48A (environment &amp; wildlife), Art. 49 (protecting monuments), Art. 50 (separation of judiciary from executive), Art. 51 (international peace and security).`
            ],
            landmark: `Granville Austin termed Fundamental Rights and DPSP together as the "Conscience of the Constitution".`,
            takeaway: `Notice that Article 43B (Co-operatives) and Article 40 (Panchayats) sit in the Gandhian group, while Article 44 (UCC) and Article 50 (Judicial separation) sit in the Liberal-Intellectual group.`
          },
          {
            code: `6.2`,
            title: `DPSP Amendments & Harmony with Fundamental Rights`,
            articles: `42nd, 44th, 86th, 97th`,
            summary: `The 42nd Amendment pushed DPSPs into education, health, and environment; the 44th trimmed property rights, and the 86th and 97th added education and co-operatives.`,
            story: `For decades, an ideological war raged between Part III (Fundamental Rights) and Part IV (DPSP): governments argued that social welfare for millions outweighed individual private property rights. In landmark cases like Champakam Dorairajan (1951) and Minerva Mills (1980), the Supreme Court settled the fight with poetic wisdom: Part III and Part IV are like two wheels of a chariot, and the Constitution is founded on the bedrock of balance between them. Later amendments strengthened DPSPs: the 42nd CAA added equal justice, workers' participation, and environment protection (Art 48A), while the 86th CAA elevated early childhood care and the 97th CAA promoted cooperative societies.`,
            storyLink: `While the State was being directed to serve the people, leaders during the turbulent 1970s realized that a healthy democracy cannot survive if citizens demand all the rights but accept zero personal responsibility.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>42nd Amendment Act (1976):</strong> Added four directives — Art. 39(f) (healthy child development), Art. 39A (equal justice &amp; free legal aid), Art. 43A (workers in management), and Art. 48A (environment protection).`,
              `<strong>44th, 86th &amp; 97th Amendments:</strong> 44th (1978) added Art. 38(2) to minimize income inequalities; 86th (2002) altered Art. 45 to focus on early childhood care below age 6; 97th (2011) added Art. 43B for cooperative societies.`,
              `<strong>FR vs. DPSP Conflict Trajectory:</strong> Champakam Dorairajan (1951: FRs prevail); Golaknath (1967: FRs unamendable); 25th Amendment (inserted Art. 31C); Kesavananda Bharati (1973: Basic Structure); Minerva Mills (1980: Constitution founded on the bedrock of balance between FRs and DPSP).`
            ],
            landmark: `Currently, Fundamental Rights enjoy supremacy over DPSP except where Articles 39(b) and 39(c) receive limited protection under a valid Article 31C.`,
            takeaway: `In Minerva Mills (1980), the Supreme Court struck down the 42nd Amendment's attempt to give ALL DPSPs primacy over Articles 14 and 19.`
          },
          {
            code: `6.3`,
            title: `Origin & Expansion of Fundamental Duties (Part IVA)`,
            articles: `Art. 51A`,
            summary: `Fundamental Duties arrived through the Swaran Singh Committee, the 42nd Amendment, and the 86th Amendment's education duty.`,
            story: `The original 1950 Constitution contained rights and state principles, but not a single civic duty for citizens—the framers had trusted that a newly freed nation would naturally behave responsibly. During the national emergency in 1976, the Swaran Singh Committee recommended that citizens must balance their rights with civic obligations, inspiring the 42nd Amendment to introduce Part IVA and Article 51A with 10 Fundamental Duties borrowed from the Soviet USSR Constitution. Decades later, the historic 86th Amendment in 2002 added an 11th sacred duty: commanding every parent or guardian to provide educational opportunities to their child between the ages of 6 and 14.`,
            storyLink: `Though non-enforceable by courts on their own, what are these eleven moral duties that every patriotic citizen is expected to uphold?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Origin:</strong> Not present in the original 1950 Constitution; inspired by the Constitution of the former USSR.`,
              `<strong>Swaran Singh Committee (1976):</strong> Recommended a separate chapter of 8 duties along with tax-paying duty and penalty provisions (government rejected penalty/tax ideas and enacted 10 duties).`,
              `<strong>42nd Amendment Act (1976):</strong> Inserted Part IVA and Article 51A with 10 Fundamental Duties (applicable solely to Indian citizens, unlike several Fundamental Rights).`,
              `<strong>86th Amendment Act (2002):</strong> Added the 11th Fundamental Duty — Art. 51A(k) requiring parents or guardians to provide educational opportunities to children aged 6 to 14 years.`
            ],
            landmark: `Verma Committee (1999) identified existing statutes (such as Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act 1971 and Wildlife Protection Act 1972) that enforce specific duties.`,
            takeaway: `Part IVA contains only ONE Article (51A) with 11 clauses (a) through (k).`
          },
          {
            code: `6.4`,
            title: `Complete Summary of the 11 Fundamental Duties`,
            articles: `51A(a)–(k)`,
            summary: `The eleven duties run from respecting the Constitution to protecting the environment, developing scientific temper, and sending children to school.`,
            story: `The 11 Fundamental Duties in Article 51A serve as a constant reminder of our collective pledge to the nation. They command citizens to respect the National Flag, Anthem, and Constitution; cherish the noble ideals of the freedom struggle; defend the sovereignty and integrity of India; promote communal brotherhood transcending religious divides; and renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women. Furthermore, they demand that we preserve our composite heritage, protect natural forests, lakes, and wildlife, foster a scientific temper and spirit of inquiry, safeguard public property, strive for excellence in all spheres, and educate our children.`,
            storyLink: `To fully master how these ideals guide governance on the ground, we must examine the specific, high-yield policy mandates mapped out across Articles 38 to 51.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Civic &amp; National Ideals (a–d):</strong> (a) Abide by Constitution &amp; respect National Flag/Anthem; (b) Cherish noble ideals of freedom struggle; (c) Uphold sovereignty, unity &amp; integrity; (d) Defend the country and render national service.`,
              `<strong>Social &amp; Cultural Harmony (e–f):</strong> (e) Promote harmony and brotherhood transcending divisions &amp; renounce practices derogatory to women's dignity; (f) Value and preserve rich heritage of composite culture.`,
              `<strong>Environment, Science &amp; Public Spirit (g–j):</strong> (g) Protect forests, lakes, rivers, wildlife &amp; show compassion for living creatures; (h) Develop scientific temper, humanism &amp; spirit of inquiry; (i) Safeguard public property &amp; abjure violence; (j) Strive towards excellence in individual and collective spheres.`,
              `<strong>Education Duty (k):</strong> Added in 2002 — parent/guardian duty to ensure schooling opportunities between ages 6 and 14.`
            ],
            landmark: `Fundamental Duties are non-justiciable directly by writs, though courts cite Article 51A when assessing the "reasonableness" of laws under Article 14 and Article 19.`,
            takeaway: `Notice the tri-part pairing from the 86th Amendment (2002): Art. 21A (FR), Art. 45 (DPSP), and Art. 51A(k) (FD).`
          },
          {
            code: `6.5`,
            title: `Detailed Breakdown of Articles 38–51`,
            articles: `Art. 38–51`,
            summary: `Articles 38 to 51 move from welfare and equality through village panchayats, workers' rights, and international peace to the duty to raise the nation.`,
            story: `Articles 38 to 51 are the engine room of Indian social democracy, laying out concrete socio-economic benchmarks that governments are judged against at election time. Article 38 orders a social order promoting welfare and minimizing income inequalities; Article 39 commands fair distribution of material resources and equal pay for equal work; Article 40 sparked India's panchayat revolution; and Article 44 continues to fuel modern national debate by urging a Uniform Civil Code. From maternity relief (Art 42) and living wages (Art 43) to separating the judiciary from the executive (Art 50) and fostering international peace (Art 51), these articles form the conscience of our republic.`,
            storyLink: `Having defined the constitutional soul, citizen rights, and welfare duties in Unit I, the Constitution turns in Unit II to build the formidable governing machinery that wields state power: starting with the Union Executive.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Articles 38–39:</strong> Article 38 directs the State to secure a social order based on justice and to minimise income and status inequalities (38(2) added by 44th CAA 1978); Article 39 covers adequate livelihood, distribution of material resources, prevention of concentration of wealth, equal pay for equal work, protection of workers' and children's health, and healthy child development.`,
              `<strong>Articles 39A–41:</strong> Article 39A provides equal justice and free legal aid (42nd CAA 1976); Article 40 directs organisation of village panchayats; Article 41 covers the right to work, education, and public assistance in unemployment, old age, sickness, and disablement.`,
              `<strong>Articles 42–43B:</strong> Article 42 requires just and humane work conditions and maternity relief; Article 43 promotes a living wage, decent standard of life, and cottage industries; Article 43A provides workers' participation in management (42nd CAA 1976); Article 43B promotes cooperative societies (97th CAA 2011).`,
              `<strong>Articles 44–46:</strong> Article 44 directs a Uniform Civil Code; Article 45 provides early childhood care and education below age 6 (86th CAA 2002); Article 46 promotes the educational and economic interests of SCs, STs, and weaker sections.`,
              `<strong>Articles 47–48A:</strong> Article 47 requires raising nutrition and public health standards and prohibiting intoxicating drinks and drugs; Article 48 organises agriculture and animal husbandry and prohibits cow slaughter; Article 48A protects the environment, forests, and wildlife (42nd CAA 1976).`,
              `<strong>Articles 49–51:</strong> Article 49 protects monuments and objects of national importance; Article 50 separates the judiciary from the executive in public services; Article 51 promotes international peace, just relations, respect for international law and treaties, and arbitration.`
            ],
            landmark: `The 42nd CAA 1976 added Articles 39(f), 39A, 43A, and 48A; the 86th CAA 2002 changed Article 45; the 97th CAA 2011 added Article 43B.`,
            takeaway: `The Article 39 mnemonic is LDC-PHC: Livelihood, Distribution, Concentration, Pay, Health, Children.`
          }        ]

      },
      {
        id: 7,
        unitId: 2,
        title: `President & Vice President`,
        articles: `Articles 52–72 & 123 (Part V)`,
        summary: `Covers the Union Executive's constitutional heads: Presidential election, powers and impeachment, the full chronicle of Presidents and Vice-Presidents, succession rules, and the Vice President's dual role.`,
        story: `The story moves upward to the Union Executive, where the President is the constitutional head and the Vice-President waits in the wings.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `7.1`,
            title: `President: Election, Qualifications & Oath`,
            articles: `Art. 52–60`,
            summary: `The President is elected indirectly by an electoral college, must be 35, and takes oath before the Chief Justice of India.`,
            story: `Having suffered under autocratic British Viceroys, the framers wrestled with a critical choice: should India's President be an executive strongman like the US President, or a dignified constitutional referee? They chose the parliamentary model: the President is the formal head of State (De jure), elected not by a direct public vote, but by an Electoral College combining MPs and MLAs from across India using single transferable votes. At 35 years of age, taking oath before the Chief Justice of India, the President embodies the sovereign unity and constitutional continuity of the entire republic.`,
            storyLink: `Though the President reigns as the supreme ceremonial protector of the Constitution, what checks exist if a President betrays this oath, and what lethal legislative and judicial powers do they hold?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Position (Art. 52–53):</strong> Head of the Indian State, First Citizen, and symbol of national unity; executive power of the Union vests in the President, exercised per Article 74 advice.`,
              `<strong>Electoral College (Art. 54):</strong> Comprises ONLY elected members of both Houses of Parliament, elected MLAs of States, and elected MLAs of Delhi and Puducherry (70th Amendment 1992). Nominated members and State Legislative Council members do NOT vote.`,
              `<strong>Voting Formula (Art. 55):</strong> Proportional representation via single transferable vote by secret ballot; MLA vote value = (State population / Elected MLAs) / 1000; MP vote value = Total value of all MLAs / Total elected MPs.`,
              `<strong>Qualifications &amp; Security (Art. 58):</strong> Citizen of India, 35+ years, qualified for House of the People election, no office of profit; requires 50 proposers and 50 seconders plus Rs. 15,000 RBI deposit. Oath (Art. 60) administered by Chief Justice of India.`
            ],
            landmark: `President holds a 5-year term and is eligible for re-election any number of times (Dr. Rajendra Prasad served two full terms).`,
            takeaway: `Contrast Electoral Colleges: President includes elected state MLAs; Vice President excludes all state MLAs and includes nominated MPs.`
          },
          {
            code: `7.2`,
            title: `Impeachment, Vetoes, Ordinances & Pardoning Powers`,
            articles: `Art. 61, 72, 111, 123`,
            summary: `Impeachment needs a two-thirds special majority; vetoes, ordinances, and pardons show the President's careful, limited power.`,
            story: `To ensure no President acts as an unchecked monarch, Article 61 established the ultimate parliamentary guillotine: impeachment for 'violation of the Constitution', requiring a punishing two-thirds majority of the total membership in both Houses. In daily statecraft, the President exercises vital checks—using Absolute Veto (rejecting private bills), Suspensive Veto (sending bills back for reconsideration), or Pocket Veto (quietly sitting on a bill indefinitely, as Zail Singh did in 1986). Under Article 72, the President holds the sovereign mercy power to pardon death sentences, and under Article 123, the temporary legislative power to promulgate ordinances during recess.`,
            storyLink: `But if an active President suddenly dies, falls ill, or resigns in the middle of a national crisis, who maintains the unbroken pulse of the state?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Impeachment (Art. 61):</strong> Solely for "violation of the Constitution" (phrase undefined); initiated in EITHER House with 1/4th member signatures and 14 days' notice; requires 2/3rd of TOTAL membership in both Houses (includes nominated MPs, excludes state MLAs).`,
              `<strong>Veto Powers (Art. 111):</strong> Absolute Veto (withhold assent), Suspensive Veto (return non-Money Bill once — overridden by simple majority), and Pocket Veto (no constitutional time limit — used by Giani Zail Singh in 1986). No veto over Constitutional Amendment Bills (24th Amendment 1971).`,
              `<strong>Ordinance Power (Art. 123):</strong> Issued when either or both Houses are not in session on ministerial advice; must be approved by Parliament within 6 weeks of reassembly (max theoretical life 6 months + 6 weeks).`,
              `<strong>Pardoning Power (Art. 72):</strong> Five forms — Pardon (complete absolution), Commutation (lighter form), Remission (reduced period without changing character), Respite (lesser sentence for special facts like pregnancy/disability), and Reprieve (temporary stay of execution). Includes death sentence and court-martial.`
            ],
            landmark: `Maru Ram (1980) and Epuru Sudhakar (2006) confirmed that Presidential pardoning decisions under Art. 72 are subject to limited judicial review against arbitrariness.`,
            takeaway: `The President's impeachment requires 2/3rd of TOTAL membership of each House — the strictest parliamentary threshold in the Constitution.`
          },
          {
            code: `7.3`,
            title: `Vice President: Election, Removal & Functions`,
            articles: `Art. 63–69`,
            summary: `The Vice-President is elected by both Houses, is ex-officio Rajya Sabha Chairman, and steps in when the President cannot act.`,
            story: `Borrowing directly from the American constitutional architecture, the founders created the office of the Vice President as the ultimate constitutional spare tire. Elected jointly by both Houses of Parliament through proportional representation, the Vice President holds no executive portfolio; instead, they earn their official salary as the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha, maintaining order and conducting debates. But the moment the presidency falls vacant due to death, resignation, or removal, the Vice President immediately steps up as acting President for up to six months.`,
            storyLink: `What happens if both the President and Vice President pass away or are incapacitated simultaneously, or if a bitter dispute erupts over who won the election?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Electoral College (Art. 66):</strong> Consists of BOTH elected and nominated members of both Houses of Parliament (State Legislatures do NOT participate).`,
              `<strong>Qualifications &amp; Oath:</strong> Citizen, 35+ years, qualified for election to the Council of States (note difference from President's House of the People requirement); 20 proposers &amp; 20 seconders; Oath (Art. 69) administered by President.`,
              `<strong>Ex-Officio Role (Art. 64):</strong> Serves as ex-officio Chairman of the Council of States (Upper House), mirroring the US Vice President.`,
              `<strong>Removal Process (Art. 67):</strong> No formal impeachment needed; resolution introduced ONLY in the Council of States with 14 days' notice, passed by an effective majority (majority of all then members of Upper House) and agreed to by the House of the People by simple majority.`
            ],
            landmark: `Dr. S. Radhakrishnan and Hamid Ansari are the two Vice Presidents to serve two consecutive full terms.`,
            takeaway: `While acting as President (max 6 months during vacancy), the Vice President draws the President's salary and ceases to perform Upper House Chairman duties.`
          },
          {
            code: `7.4`,
            title: `Succession Rules & Election Disputes`,
            articles: `Art. 65, 70, 71`,
            summary: `Succession passes to the Vice-President, then the Chief Justice, or a qualified person; election disputes go to the Supreme Court.`,
            story: `In May 1969, India faced an unprecedented constitutional vacuum when President Zakir Husain died in office, and Vice President V.V. Giri promptly resigned to contest the presidency. Parliament swiftly enacted the President (Discharge of Functions) Act 1969: Chief Justice of India Mohammad Hidayatullah took the presidential oath, ensuring the republic was never left headless. To prevent partisan street brawls over who occupies Rashtrapati Bhavan, Article 71 commands that all election disputes regarding the President and Vice President must be inquired into and exclusively decided by the Supreme Court.`,
            storyLink: `Behind these legal rules lies a living gallery of Indian statesmen, scholars, and trailblazers who shaped the presidency from 1950 to the present day.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Presidential Vacancy (Art. 62 &amp; 65):</strong> Vacancy due to death, resignation, or removal must be filled by fresh election within 6 months; the Vice President acts as President in the interim.`,
              `<strong>Contingency Succession (1969 Act):</strong> If both President and Vice President offices fall vacant simultaneously, the Chief Justice of India (and in absence, senior-most Supreme Court judge) discharges Presidential duties (Justice M. Hidayatullah did so in 1969).`,
              `<strong>Election Disputes (Art. 71):</strong> All doubts and disputes relating to the election of the President or Vice President are inquired into and decided EXCLUSIVELY by the Supreme Court, whose decision is final.`
            ],
            landmark: `Acts done by a President or Vice President before an election is declared void by the Supreme Court remain legally valid.`,
            takeaway: `Presidential resignation is addressed to the Vice President; Vice Presidential resignation is addressed to the President.`
          },
          {
            code: `7.5`,
            title: `Chronicle of Presidents of India`,
            articles: `1950–Present`,
            summary: `From Rajendra Prasad to Droupadi Murmu, the presidential chronicle is a story of firsts, long tenures, and short ones.`,
            story: `Rashtrapati Bhavan's history reflects India's evolving political soul: Dr. Rajendra Prasad served a record twelve years as the anchor of the young republic, followed by philosopher-statesman Dr. S. Radhakrishnan. The office witnessed deep tragedy when Zakir Husain and Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed died in harness, political turbulence during the 1975 Emergency, and a historic uncontested election when Neelam Sanjiva Reddy was chosen in 1977. Later decades brought brilliant milestones—from scientist Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam as the 'People's President', to Pratibha Patil as the first woman President, and Droupadi Murmu as the first tribal woman to lead the republic.`,
            storyLink: `Alongside the presidents, the Vice-Presidents formed a parallel line of distinguished scholars and jurists who steered the Upper House.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>1950–1969:</strong> Dr. Rajendra Prasad (1950–1962; longest serving, two full terms); Dr. Sarvepalli Radhakrishnan (1962–1967); Dr. Zakir Husain (1967–1969; first Muslim President, died in office, shortest tenure); V.V. Giri (1969–1974; first acting President to be elected).`,
              `<strong>1974–1987:</strong> Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed (1974–1977; declared the 1975 Emergency, died in office); Neelam Sanjiva Reddy (1977–1982; only President elected unopposed, first non-Congress President); Giani Zail Singh (1982–1987; first Sikh President, used pocket veto on the Indian Post Office Bill 1986).`,
              `<strong>1987–2002:</strong> R. Venkataraman (1987–1992); Dr. Shankar Dayal Sharma (1992–1997); K.R. Narayanan (1997–2002; first Dalit President).`,
              `<strong>2002–2017:</strong> Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam (2002–2007; "Missile Man" and "People's President"); Pratibha Patil (2007–2012; first woman President); Pranab Mukherjee (2012–2017).`,
              `<strong>2017–Present:</strong> Ram Nath Kovind (2017–2022; second Dalit President); Droupadi Murmu (2022–present; first Tribal and second woman President).`
            ],
            landmark: `Dr. Rajendra Prasad is the longest-serving President; Zakir Husain had the shortest tenure; Pratibha Patil was the first woman President; Droupadi Murmu is the first Tribal President.`,
            takeaway: `Remember the firsts: first Muslim (Zakir Husain), first acting-elected (V.V. Giri), first Sikh (Zail Singh), first Dalit (K.R. Narayanan), first woman (Pratibha Patil), first Tribal (Droupadi Murmu).`
          },
          {
            code: `7.6`,
            title: `Chronicle of Vice-Presidents of India`,
            articles: `1952–Present`,
            summary: `From Radhakrishnan to Dhankhar, the Vice-Presidents show the office's shift from scholar-statesman to parliamentary presiding officer.`,
            story: `The office of the Vice-President began with towering intellectual giants: Dr. S. Radhakrishnan served two full terms as a revered scholar before ascending to the presidency, establishing a tradition followed by Zakir Husain, V.V. Giri, R. Venkataraman, Shankar Dayal Sharma, and K.R. Narayanan. The roll call traces memorable figures like Krishan Kant, the only Vice-President to die in office, alongside two-term chairman Mohammad Hamid Ansari, M. Venkaiah Naidu, and Jagdeep Dhankhar. Over seven decades, the role transitioned from a quiet academic chair into a high-stakes parliamentary command center.`,
            storyLink: `While the President reigns in ceremonial splendor, the real power plant that drives the daily machinery of the Indian State is the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>1952–1969:</strong> Dr. S. Radhakrishnan (1952–1962; two terms); Dr. Zakir Husain (1962–1967); V.V. Giri (1967–1969).`,
              `<strong>1969–1987:</strong> Gopal Swarup Pathak (1969–1974); B.D. Jatti (1974–1979); Mohammad Hidayatullah (1979–1984); R. Venkataraman (1984–1987).`,
              `<strong>1987–2007:</strong> Dr. Shankar Dayal Sharma (1987–1992); K.R. Narayanan (1992–1997); Krishan Kant (1997–2002; died in office); Bhairon Singh Shekhawat (2002–2007).`,
              `<strong>2007–2025:</strong> Mohammad Hamid Ansari (2007–2017; two terms, served under three Presidents); M. Venkaiah Naidu (2017–2022); Jagdeep Dhankhar (2022–2025); C.P. Radhakrishnan (current Vice President).`
            ],
            landmark: `Dr. S. Radhakrishnan and Hamid Ansari each served two full terms; Krishan Kant died in office.`,
            takeaway: `The Vice-President is ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha and can act as President for a maximum of six months.`
          }        ]

      },
      {
        id: 8,
        unitId: 2,
        title: `Prime Minister & Council of Ministers`,
        articles: `Articles 74–78 & 88`,
        summary: `Examines real executive authority under India's parliamentary model: appointment, duties, roles and significance of the Prime Minister, collective responsibility, cabinet committees, and the Attorney General.`,
        story: `The Prime Minister and Council of Ministers supply the real executive, turning constitutional authority into daily government.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `8.1`,
            title: `Prime Minister: Appointment & Constitutional Duties`,
            articles: `Art. 74, 75, 78`,
            summary: `The Prime Minister is appointed by the President, advises on ministers, and communicates Cabinet decisions to the President.`,
            story: `The Indian Constitution creates a classic Westminster engine: the President is the ceremonial head of state (De jure), but the Prime Minister is the real chief executive (De facto). Appointed by the President as the undisputed leader of the Lok Sabha majority, the PM selects the council of ministers and allocates portfolios with absolute authority. Under Article 78, the Prime Minister acts as the sole constitutional communication bridge, duty-bound to keep the President briefed on all cabinet decisions, legislative proposals, and administrative policies.`,
            storyLink: `The Prime Minister does not govern in isolation; executive power is wielded through an organized team of ministers bound by collective survival—the Union Council of Ministers.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>De Facto Executive:</strong> The President is the nominal (de jure) head of state, whereas the Prime Minister is the real (de facto) head of government and leader of the majority coalition/party in the House of the People.`,
              `<strong>Appointment &amp; House Membership:</strong> Appointed by the President under Art. 75; can belong to either House (Indira Gandhi, H.D. Deve Gowda, I.K. Gujral, and Dr. Manmohan Singh were Upper House members), or even be a non-MP provided they enter either House within 6 months.`,
              `<strong>Article 74 (Aid &amp; Advice):</strong> Council of Ministers headed by PM aids and advises the President; 42nd Amendment made advice binding, and 44th Amendment allowed the President to send advice back once for reconsideration.`,
              `<strong>Article 78 (Channel of Communication):</strong> PM must communicate all Council decisions on administration and legislative proposals to the President, furnish requested information, and submit individual ministerial decisions for Council consideration if required.`
            ],
            landmark: `If no party holds a clear majority, the President exercises situational discretion to appoint a leader likely to prove majority on the floor of the House within a stipulated time.`,
            takeaway: `The resignation or death of a sitting Prime Minister automatically dissolves the entire Council of Ministers.`
          },
          {
            code: `8.2`,
            title: `Union Council of Ministers: Structure & Responsibility`,
            articles: `Art. 75 · 91st Amendment`,
            summary: `The Council of Ministers is a three-tier body collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha, capped at 15% of its strength.`,
            story: `Under Article 75, the Union Council of Ministers is bound by the golden rule of parliamentary democracy: Collective Responsibility to the Lok Sabha. They stand or fall together like a swimming team—if a single no-confidence motion passes, the entire ministry must resign, and ministers cannot publicly criticize cabinet decisions. The team operates in a three-tier hierarchy: the inner Cabinet of senior heavyweights, Ministers of State (with or without independent charge), and junior Deputy Ministers. To stop rampant political horse-trading, the 91st Amendment in 2003 capped the council's size at a strict 15% of the Lok Sabha's total strength.`,
            storyLink: `Because a sprawling council of 80 ministers cannot debate complex daily crises efficiently, policy decisions are delegated to smaller, nimble executive strike teams—the Cabinet Committees.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Size Cap (91st Amendment Act, 2003):</strong> Total number of Ministers including the Prime Minister shall NOT exceed 15% of the total strength of the House of the People.`,
              `<strong>Three Ranks:</strong> Cabinet Ministers (head key ministries, attend Cabinet meetings), Ministers of State (either independent charge or attached to Cabinet Ministers), and Deputy Ministers (assist senior ministers, not Cabinet members).`,
              `<strong>Collective Responsibility (Art. 75(3)):</strong> The Council is collectively responsible to the House of the People ("sink and swim together"); a No-Confidence Motion passed in the Lower House forces all ministers (including Upper House ministers) to resign.`,
              `<strong>Individual Responsibility (Art. 75(2)):</strong> Ministers hold office during the pleasure of the President (effectively exercised on the PM's advice).`
            ],
            landmark: `The word "Cabinet" appears only ONCE in the original/amended text of the Constitution — inside Article 352(3) inserted by the 44th Amendment Act (1978).`,
            takeaway: `Distinguish clearly: "Council of Ministers" is the broader 60–70 member constitutional body (Art. 74/75); "Cabinet" is its compact 15–20 member inner decision-making core.`
          },
          {
            code: `8.3`,
            title: `Cabinet Committees & Executive Coordination`,
            articles: `Rules of Business`,
            summary: `Cabinet Committees are the executive's internal wiring; almost all are chaired by the PM, except Parliamentary Affairs.`,
            story: `While the full Council of Ministers rarely meets together, real policy decisions are crafted inside elite Cabinet Committees set up under the Transaction of Business Rules. These extra-constitutional bodies tackle urgent national issues—such as the Appointments Committee, Economic Affairs, and Political Affairs (often nicknamed the 'Super-Cabinet'). The Prime Minister chairs nearly all of these powerful bodies, with one notable exception: the Cabinet Committee on Parliamentary Affairs, traditionally chaired by the Defense Minister or a senior cabinet veteran.`,
            storyLink: `When this executive powerhouse drafts bills or enters legal disputes, who stands up in court as the sovereign legal protector of the Government of India?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Legal Nature:</strong> Extra-constitutional bodies created under the Government of India Transaction of Business Rules (1961) to reduce Cabinet workload and enable deep policy scrutiny.`,
              `<strong>Types:</strong> Standing Committees (permanent) and Ad-hoc Committees (temporary for specific tasks); typically comprise 3 to 8 Cabinet-level ministers.`,
              `<strong>Key Standing Committees:</strong> Appointments Committee of the Cabinet (ACC), Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA), Cabinet Committee on Political Affairs (CCPA — often called "Super-Cabinet"), and Cabinet Committee on Security (CCS) — all normally chaired by the Prime Minister.`,
              `<strong>Exceptions:</strong> Cabinet Committee on Parliamentary Affairs and Cabinet Committee on Accommodation are usually chaired by the Home or Defence Minister.`
            ],
            landmark: `Group of Ministers (GoMs) and Empowered Groups of Ministers (EGoMs) serve alongside Cabinet Committees for cross-ministerial issues.`,
            takeaway: `All major Cabinet Committees except Parliamentary Affairs and Accommodation are chaired by the Prime Minister.`
          },
          {
            code: `8.4`,
            title: `Attorney General of India`,
            articles: `Art. 76 & 88`,
            summary: `The Attorney General is the Union's first law officer, appointed by the President, and can speak in both Houses without voting.`,
            story: `Under Article 76, the President appoints the Attorney General of India—the nation's highest-ranking legal officer, possessing the qualifications of a Supreme Court judge. Holding office during the pleasure of the President, the Attorney General defends the Union Government in all landmark legal showdowns and gives advice on thorny constitutional questions. Uniquely, the AG enjoys the right of audience in all Indian courts and the special privilege to sit and speak in both Houses of Parliament, though they are strictly barred from voting on the floor.`,
            storyLink: `With legal advisors and ministers in place, we can now appreciate the Prime Minister's overarching role as the keystone holding the entire Indian constitutional arch together.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Position (Art. 76):</strong> Highest Law Officer of the country; appointed by the President and holds office during the pleasure of the President (conventionally resigns when the Council changes).`,
              `<strong>Qualification:</strong> Must possess the qualifications required to be appointed a Judge of the Supreme Court (Indian citizen + 5 years HC judge, or 10 years HC advocate, or distinguished jurist).`,
              `<strong>Parliamentary Privileges (Art. 88):</strong> Has the right to speak and participate in proceedings of both Houses of Parliament, joint sittings, and any parliamentary committee of which they are named a member — WITHOUT the right to vote.`,
              `<strong>Private Practice Rules:</strong> Not a full-time government servant and not debarred from private legal practice, provided they do not advise or appear against the Government of India.`
            ],
            landmark: `The Solicitor General and Additional Solicitors General assist the Attorney General, though ONLY the Attorney General is mentioned in Article 76.`,
            takeaway: `Article 88 gives Ministers and the Attorney General speaking rights in both Houses without voting rights in the House they do not belong to.`
          },
          {
            code: `8.5`,
            title: `Roles, Powers & Significance of the Prime Minister`,
            articles: `Art. 74–78`,
            summary: `The PM is the keystone of the parliamentary arch: leader of the House, chief adviser, cabinet chair, and public face of the government.`,
            story: `Constitutional scholars like Sir Ivor Jennings describe the British PM as 'primus inter pares' (first among equals), but in India's political reality, the Prime Minister is the sun around which all executive planets revolve. The PM leads the Lok Sabha, commands the armed forces during crises, represents the republic on the global stage, and directs the Planning and NITI Aayog apparatus. A strong Prime Minister defines the political era, while a fragile coalition PM must balance regional allies to prevent the collapse of government.`,
            storyLink: `Every decision, policy, and budget crafted by the Prime Minister's executive team must ultimately face the sovereign scrutiny of the people's elected temple: the Parliament of India.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Central Position:</strong> Jawaharlal Nehru called the Prime Minister the "Linchpin of the Government"; the office is also described as the "Keystone of the Cabinet arch"; Dr. Ambedkar said collective responsibility can be achieved only through the instrumentality of the Prime Minister.`,
              `<strong>Ex-Officio Chairs:</strong> The Prime Minister chairs NITI Aayog, the National Integration Council, the Inter-State Council, the National Disaster Management Authority, and the National Water Resources Council.`,
              `<strong>Resignation or Death:</strong> The resignation or death of the Prime Minister automatically dissolves the entire Council of Ministers.`,
              `<strong>Rajya Sabha Prime Ministers:</strong> Indira Gandhi (1966), H.D. Deve Gowda (1996), I.K. Gujral (1997), and Dr. Manmohan Singh (2004) served as Prime Ministers while belonging to the Rajya Sabha.`,
              `<strong>Notable Tenures:</strong> Jawaharlal Nehru was the longest-serving Prime Minister (16 years, 286 days); Atal Bihari Vajpayee had the shortest single term (13 days in 1996); Chaudhary Charan Singh served 170 days and never faced Parliament; Chandra Shekhar served seven months.`
            ],
            landmark: `Morarji Desai was the first Prime Minister to resign; Nehru remains the longest-serving Prime Minister.`,
            takeaway: `The Prime Minister is the real executive, the link between the President and the Council of Ministers, and the chair of most major extra-constitutional councils.`
          }        ]

      },
      {
        id: 9,
        unitId: 2,
        title: `Parliament`,
        articles: `Articles 79–122 (Part V)`,
        summary: `Comprehensive breakdown of the Union Parliament: both Houses, presiding officers, bills and budgets, motions, ordinances, parliamentary committees, and state-wise seat distribution.`,
        story: `Parliament then becomes the stage: two Houses, bills, budgets, questions, committees, and ordinances.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `9.1`,
            title: `Composition of Parliament & Both Houses`,
            articles: `Art. 79–84`,
            summary: `Parliament is the President plus the Rajya Sabha and Lok Sabha, with the Lok Sabha directly elected and the Rajya Sabha representing states.`,
            story: `Article 79 commands that the Parliament of India consists of three distinct pillars: the President, the Council of States (Rajya Sabha), and the House of the People (Lok Sabha). The Lok Sabha represents the raw democratic voice of India's citizens through direct adult franchise across single-member constituencies, capped at 550 members after the 104th Amendment abolished Anglo-Indian nominations. The Rajya Sabha represents the federal states, a permanent chamber where one-third of its 250 members retire every second year, preventing populist swings from dismantling federal stability.`,
            storyLink: `To prevent these hundreds of passionate MPs from descending into chaos during fierce debates, both Houses are presided over by powerful constitutional referees.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Three Organs (Art. 79):</strong> Parliament consists of the President, the Council of States (Upper House), and the House of the People (Lower House).`,
              `<strong>Council of States (Art. 80):</strong> Maximum 250 members (238 elected indirectly by State/UT MLAs via proportional representation + 12 nominated by the President for Literature, Science, Art, and Social Service). Current strength: 245. Permanent House; 1/3rd retire every 2 years; member tenure 6 years.`,
              `<strong>House of the People (Art. 81):</strong> Maximum 550 members (530 States + 20 UTs). The 104th Constitutional Amendment (2019) discontinued the 2 nominated Anglo-Indian seats. Current strength: 543. Normal tenure 5 years.`,
              `<strong>Age &amp; Delimitation:</strong> Minimum age 25 years for Lower House, 30 years for Upper House; 84th Amendment froze seat allocation until after the first census post-2026.`,
              `<strong>State Seat Distribution:</strong> Uttar Pradesh has the largest share — 31 Rajya Sabha seats, 80 Lok Sabha seats, and a 403-member Assembly; Maharashtra follows with 19 Rajya Sabha and 48 Lok Sabha seats.`
            ],
            landmark: `Unlike the US Senate (equal 2 seats per state), India's Council of States allocates seats based on state population under the Fourth Schedule (e.g., UP 31, Goa/NE states 1).`,
            takeaway: `The President is NOT a member of either House, yet is an integral part of Parliament because bills require Presidential assent to become law.`
          },
          {
            code: `9.2`,
            title: `Presiding Officers, Quorum & Disqualifications`,
            articles: `Art. 89–104`,
            summary: `The Speaker, Deputy Speaker, Chairman, and Deputy Chairman preside; quorum is one-tenth, and disqualifications have their own rules.`,
            story: `The Lok Sabha is steered by the Speaker (assisted by the Deputy Speaker), who yields vast authority: deciding what gets debated, certifying Money Bills, and maintaining floor discipline. In the Upper House, the Vice President presides as ex-officio Chairman. No parliamentary work can proceed without a Quorum of at least one-tenth of the House's total strength. MPs who take offices of profit, hold foreign allegiance, or violate party whips face swift disqualification under Article 102 or the 10th Schedule Anti-Defection law.`,
            storyLink: `Once the House is in order and quorum is met, Parliament tackles its primary constitutional duty: transforming public aspirations into statutory law through the lawmaking process.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Speaker of Lower House (Art. 93–96):</strong> Elected from among House members; does not vacate office upon dissolution until the first meeting of the new House; decides Money Bill certification (Art. 110 — final), chairs Joint Sittings (Art. 108), exercises casting vote in a tie, and adjudicates Tenth Schedule defection petitions.`,
              `<strong>Chairman of Upper House (Art. 89):</strong> The Vice President is ex-officio Chairman; cannot vote in the first instance (only casting vote) and does NOT preside when their own removal resolution is under consideration.`,
              `<strong>Quorum (Art. 100):</strong> Minimum 1/10th of total House strength (including presiding officer) — 55 in Lower House, 25 in Upper House.`,
              `<strong>Disqualifications (Art. 102):</strong> Office of profit, unsound mind, undischarged insolvent, foreign citizenship, or Representation of the People Act 1951 offences (decided by President on Election Commission binding opinion — Art. 103); Defection under Tenth Schedule decided by Presiding Officer (subject to judicial review per Kihoto Hollohan 1992).`
            ],
            landmark: `A parliamentary seat can be declared vacant if a member remains absent from all meetings for 60 consecutive days without House permission (Art. 101(4)).`,
            takeaway: `The Speaker of the Lower House submits resignation to the Deputy Speaker, and vice versa.`
          },
          {
            code: `9.3`,
            title: `Ordinary, Money & Financial Bills Procedure`,
            articles: `Art. 107–117`,
            summary: `Ordinary bills can start in either House, money bills only in the Lok Sabha, and financial bills have special safeguards.`,
            story: `Passing a law in India depends on the type of bill introduced: Ordinary Bills can start in either House, and if a deadlock occurs, the President can summon a Joint Sitting under Article 108 (presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker). But when public money is involved, the Lok Sabha reigns supreme: Money Bills (Art 110) can only be introduced in the Lok Sabha on the President's recommendation. The Rajya Sabha is given just 14 days to suggest changes; if it fails to act, the Money Bill passes automatically without any provision for a joint sitting.`,
            storyLink: `Beyond passing laws, how does Parliament hold powerful ministers accountable on a day-to-day basis during live legislative sessions?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Ordinary Bill (Art. 107):</strong> Introduced in either House by a minister or private member; undergoes 3 readings; Upper House can amend, reject, or detain for max 6 months; deadlock resolved by Joint Sitting (Art. 108) summoned by President and presided over by Speaker (used only thrice: Dowry Prohibition 1961, Banking Service 1978, POTA 2002).`,
              `<strong>Money Bill (Art. 110):</strong> Covers taxes, borrowing, Consolidated/Contingency Funds; introduced ONLY in Lower House with prior Presidential recommendation; Speaker's certificate is final; Upper House CANNOT amend or reject and must return within 14 days; NO Joint Sitting.`,
              `<strong>Financial Bills Type I &amp; II (Art. 117):</strong> Type I (117(1)) requires Lower House initiation + prior President consent, yet Upper House can amend/reject and Joint Sitting applies; Type II (117(3)) involves Consolidated Fund expenditure, can start in either House, and requires President recommendation at consideration stage.`,
              `<strong>Annual Financial Statement / Budget (Art. 112):</strong> Term "Budget" is not in the Constitution; goes through 6 stages ending with Appropriation Bill (Art. 114) and Finance Bill.`
            ],
            landmark: `No money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund of India except under an Appropriation Act passed by Parliament (Art. 114(3)).`,
            takeaway: `Joint Sitting (Art. 108) applies ONLY to Ordinary Bills and Financial Bills — NEVER to Money Bills or Constitutional Amendment Bills.`
          },
          {
            code: `9.4`,
            title: `Sessions, Question Hour, Zero Hour & Motions`,
            articles: `Art. 85 & Rules`,
            summary: `Sessions, Question Hour, Zero Hour, and motions are the daily grammar of parliamentary accountability.`,
            story: `Parliament meets across three annual sessions—Budget, Monsoon, and Winter—with a constitutional mandate under Article 85 that no more than six months can elapse between two sessions. The daily drama kicks off with Question Hour, where MPs fire Starred (oral answers with follow-ups), Unstarred (written), and Short Notice questions at ministers. This is followed by Zero Hour, a unique Indian parliamentary invention born in 1962, where urgent public crises can be raised without advance notice. To test government survival, the opposition can table a No-Confidence Motion, which requires 50 MPs to introduce and can immediately topple the ministry.`,
            storyLink: `Beyond daily questions and fiery motions, the ultimate test of parliamentary power is total control over the public purse—how taxes are collected and audited.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Sessions &amp; Recess (Art. 85):</strong> Three regular sessions — Budget (Feb–May), Monsoon (Jul–Sep), Winter (Nov–Dec); maximum permissible gap between two sessions is 6 months.`,
              `<strong>Adjournment vs. Prorogation vs. Dissolution:</strong> Adjournment suspends a sitting for hours/days (by Presiding Officer); Adjournment Sine Die suspends indefinitely; Prorogation terminates a session (by President); Dissolution ends the life of Lower House only (Upper House is never dissolved).`,
              `<strong>Question Hour &amp; Zero Hour:</strong> First hour of sitting (11–12 PM): Starred Questions (oral answer + supplementaries), Unstarred (written answer), Short Notice (under 10 days' notice). Zero Hour (12–1 PM) is an Indian innovation (since 1962) not mentioned in Rules of Procedure.`,
              `<strong>Key Motions:</strong> No-Confidence Motion (only in Lower House against entire Council; needs 50 members' support; no reason needed), Adjournment Motion (urgent public matter; 50 members), Censure Motion (specific policy/minister; must state reasons), and Calling Attention Motion (Indian innovation).`
            ],
            landmark: `Passing an Adjournment Motion or Censure Motion does not legally force resignation, whereas passing a No-Confidence Motion mandates immediate resignation of the entire Council of Ministers.`,
            takeaway: `Pending bills lapse rules: A bill pending in Upper House NOT passed by Lower House does NOT lapse when Lower House dissolves; a bill passed by Lower House and pending in Upper House DOES lapse.`
          },
          {
            code: `9.5`,
            title: `Public Funds & Financial Parliamentary Committees`,
            articles: `Art. 266–267`,
            summary: `The Consolidated Fund, Contingency Fund, and Public Account hold public money; the PAC, Estimates Committee, and COPU audit the spending.`,
            story: `Article 266 places all government revenue, loans, and tax receipts into the Consolidated Fund of India, decreeing that not a single rupee can be withdrawn without parliamentary approval via an Appropriation Act. For sudden disasters, the Contingency Fund of India (Art 267) holds emergency reserves under the President's disposal. To ensure ministers do not waste public funds, Parliament deploys three financial bloodhounds: the Public Accounts Committee (PAC, headed by an opposition MP, scrutinizing CAG reports), the 30-member Estimates Committee (examining budget efficiency), and the Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU).`,
            storyLink: `But what happens when an urgent national crisis erupts while Parliament is prorogued or adjourned, and new laws are needed before MPs can reconvene?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Three Public Funds:</strong> Consolidated Fund of India (Art. 266(1) — all revenues, loans, treasury bills; parliamentary appropriation required), Public Account of India (Art. 266(2) — provident funds, small savings; executive action suffices), and Contingency Fund of India (Art. 267 — placed at President's disposal for unforeseen urgent expenditure).`,
              `<strong>Public Accounts Committee (PAC):</strong> Oldest (1921); 22 members (15 Lower House + 7 Upper House); 1-year term; since 1967 chaired by an Opposition member; audits CAG reports.`,
              `<strong>Estimates Committee:</strong> Largest (30 members — ALL from Lower House); suggests economies in public expenditure ("continuous economy committee").`,
              `<strong>Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU):</strong> 22 members (15 Lower House + 7 Upper House); created in 1964 on Krishna Menon Committee recommendation.`
            ],
            landmark: `Ministers cannot be elected or appointed as members of any of the three Financial Committees (PAC, Estimates, COPU).`,
            takeaway: `Estimates Committee is unique among the three financial committees because it has ZERO representation from the Council of States.`
          },
          {
            code: `9.6`,
            title: `Ordinance-Making Power of the President (Article 123)`,
            articles: `Art. 123`,
            summary: `Article 123 lets the President issue ordinances when Parliament is not in session; they expire six weeks after it reassembles.`,
            story: `The founders recognized that natural disasters, epidemics, or financial emergencies could strike when Parliament is not in session. Under Article 123, the President has the exceptional power to promulgate Ordinances that have the exact legal force of an Act of Parliament. However, to prevent authoritarian rule by decree, ordinances face strict expiration clocks: they must be laid before both Houses and cease to operate six weeks after Parliament reassembles, unless approved earlier. In DC Wadhwa (1987), the Supreme Court struck down the fraudulent practice of repeatedly re-promulgating ordinances without legislative voting.`,
            storyLink: `With thousands of complex budget demands and specialized bills flooding each session, Parliament relies on specialized standing committees to conduct granular forensic scrutiny.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>When Issued:</strong> The President may promulgate an ordinance when either House or both Houses are not in session, on the advice of the Council of Ministers; the ordinance has the same force as an Act of Parliament.`,
              `<strong>Duration:</strong> It must be laid before Parliament within six weeks of reassembly; its maximum theoretical life is six months plus six weeks.`,
              `<strong>Judicial Review:</strong> Cooper v. Union of India (1970) and D.C. Wadhwa v. State of Bihar (1986) held that re-promulgation without legislative review is unconstitutional and that the President's satisfaction is subject to judicial review.`,
              `<strong>Limits:</strong> An ordinance cannot amend the Constitution; it may be retrospective; it ceases to operate if Parliament disapproves it or if it is not laid before Parliament in time.`
            ],
            landmark: `D.C. Wadhwa (1986) condemned the practice of re-promulgating ordinances to bypass the legislature.`,
            takeaway: `Article 123 is co-extensive with Parliament's legislative power but cannot be used to amend the Constitution.`
          },
          {
            code: `9.7`,
            title: `Parliamentary Committees`,
            articles: `Standing & Ad Hoc Committees`,
            summary: `Parliamentary committees are mini-parliaments: standing committees scrutinise ministries, while ad hoc committees investigate specific issues.`,
            story: `Because 543 MPs shouting in a chamber cannot read every fine-print clause of complex technical bills, Parliament conducts its deepest, non-partisan work inside Parliamentary Committees—often described as 'mini-parliaments.' Standing Committees (like Departmentally Related Standing Committees, DRSCs) work year-round, examining budget demands and scrutinizing draft bills behind closed doors where party rivalries soften. In contrast, Ad Hoc Committees (like Joint Parliamentary Committees, JPCs) are formed for specific one-time corruption scandals or crises and dissolve as soon as their report is submitted.`,
            storyLink: `The bedrock of parliamentary legitimacy rests on fair representation: how are seats in both Houses distributed among India's diverse states and regions?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Standing vs. Ad Hoc:</strong> Standing committees are permanent and constituted every year; ad hoc committees are temporary and created for a specific task.`,
              `<strong>Departmental Standing Committees:</strong> There are 24 Department-Related Standing Committees (DRSCs), each with 31 members — 21 from the Lok Sabha and 10 from the Rajya Sabha.`,
              `<strong>Public Accounts Committee (PAC):</strong> Established in 1921 under the Government of India Act 1919; 22 members (15 Lok Sabha + 7 Rajya Sabha); the chairman is conventionally from the Opposition; it examines CAG reports.`,
              `<strong>Estimates Committee &amp; COPU:</strong> The Estimates Committee (1950) has 30 members, all from the Lok Sabha, and is the largest committee; the Committee on Public Undertakings (1964) has 22 members (15 + 7) and was created on the Krishna Menon Committee's recommendation.`,
              `<strong>Ethics &amp; Financial Committees:</strong> The Rajya Sabha Ethics Committee was set up in 1997 and the Lok Sabha Ethics Committee in 2000; ministers cannot be members of the PAC, Estimates Committee, or COPU.`
            ],
            landmark: `The PAC is the oldest financial committee; the Estimates Committee is the largest and has no Rajya Sabha members.`,
            takeaway: `Financial committees: PAC 22 (15+7), Estimates 30 (all Lok Sabha), COPU 22 (15+7).`
          },
          {
            code: `9.8`,
            title: `State-wise Seat Distribution`,
            articles: `Rajya Sabha · Lok Sabha · Assembly`,
            summary: `State-wise seat distribution links population to Lok Sabha seats and equality to Rajya Sabha seats, with assembly sizes capped by Article 170.`,
            story: `Seat allocation in Parliament reflects a delicate federal compromise between democratic numbers and regional protection. In the Lok Sabha, seats are allocated to states in proportion to population—making Uttar Pradesh the heavyweight with 80 seats, while smaller states like Sikkim, Mizoram, and Nagaland have 1 seat each. In the Rajya Sabha, seats are also based on population (unlike the US Senate, where every state gets 2 seats), ranging from 31 seats for UP to 1 seat for small northeastern states. To prevent southern states that succeeded in family planning from being penalized with fewer seats, the 84th Amendment in 2001 froze seat delimitation based on the 1971 census until 2026.`,
            storyLink: `Just as the Union relies on Parliament in New Delhi, each of India's 28 States operates its own legislative assembly and executive machinery at the state capital.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Largest States:</strong> Uttar Pradesh — 31 Rajya Sabha, 80 Lok Sabha, 403 Assembly (highest); Maharashtra — 19/48/288; West Bengal — 16/42/294; Bihar — 16/40/243; Tamil Nadu — 18/39/234.`,
              `<strong>Central &amp; Western States:</strong> Madhya Pradesh — 11/29/230; Karnataka — 12/28/224; Gujarat — 11/26/182; Andhra Pradesh — 11/25/175; Rajasthan — 10/25/200.`,
              `<strong>Eastern &amp; Southern States:</strong> Odisha — 10/21/147; Kerala — 9/20/140; Telangana — 7/17/119; Punjab — 7/13/117; Assam — 7/14/126.`,
              `<strong>Smaller States:</strong> Jharkhand — 6/14/81; Haryana — 5/10/90; Chhattisgarh — 5/11/90.`,
              `<strong>Union Territories with Assemblies:</strong> Delhi — 3 Rajya Sabha, 7 Lok Sabha, 70 Assembly; Jammu and Kashmir — 4/5/90; Puducherry — 1/1/30.`
            ],
            landmark: `Uttar Pradesh has the largest representation in both Houses and the largest State Assembly.`,
            takeaway: `Use the ratio pattern: Rajya Sabha seats are roughly half the Lok Sabha seats for most States.`
          }        ]

      },
      {
        id: 10,
        unitId: 2,
        title: `State Legislature`,
        articles: `Articles 153–213 (Part VI)`,
        summary: `Details State Legislative architecture: unicameral vs. bicameral legislatures, Article 169, legislative procedures, the Governor and State Executive, and Part XVII official language.`,
        story: `The same drama repeats in the states, with Assemblies, Councils, Governors, and the official-language thread.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `10.1`,
            title: `Unicameral vs. Bicameral States & Article 169`,
            articles: `Art. 168–169`,
            summary: `Most states are unicameral; six have Legislative Councils, which Parliament can create or abolish under Article 169.`,
            story: `Unlike the Union Parliament which is mandatorily bicameral, India allows its states the flexibility of having either a single chamber (Legislative Assembly/Vidhan Sabha) or two chambers (Assembly plus Legislative Council/Vidhan Parishad). Today, only six states maintain a bicameral legislature: Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, and Telangana. Under Article 169, Parliament holds the sovereign power to create or abolish a state's Legislative Council by a simple law, provided the state's Legislative Assembly first passes a resolution by a special two-thirds majority.`,
            storyLink: `How do the composition, election rules, and tenures of state MLAs and MLCs compare to their counterparts in Parliament?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Structure (Art. 168):</strong> Unicameral states have Governor + Legislative Assembly; Bicameral states have Governor + Legislative Assembly + Legislative Council.`,
              `<strong>Six Bicameral States:</strong> Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Telangana, and Uttar Pradesh.`,
              `<strong>Creation or Abolition (Art. 169):</strong> Parliament can create or abolish a State Legislative Council by a SIMPLE majority, provided the concerned State Legislative Assembly passes a resolution by a SPECIAL majority (majority of total membership + 2/3rd of members present and voting).`,
              `<strong>Constitutional Status:</strong> A law under Article 169 is NOT deemed a constitutional amendment under Article 368.`
            ],
            landmark: `Notice the two-step majority split: Special Majority in the State Assembly to initiate, followed by Simple Majority in Parliament to enact.`,
            takeaway: `Remember the six bicameral states using the pair mnemonic: UP–Bihar, Maharashtra–Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh–Telangana.`
          },
          {
            code: `10.2`,
            title: `Composition & Tenure of Assembly and Council`,
            articles: `Art. 170–172`,
            summary: `Assemblies are directly elected for five years; Councils are partly elected, partly nominated, and permanently in session.`,
            story: `Under Article 170, a state's Legislative Assembly consists of directly elected representatives, with constitutional limits set between 60 and 500 members (though exceptions like Goa, Sikkim, and Mizoram have smaller assemblies due to low population). The Assembly serves a five-year term, subject to earlier dissolution. In contrast, the Legislative Council is a permanent Upper House capped at one-third of the Assembly's strength (minimum 40), where one-third of members retire every two years. Its members are elected through an ingenious quota: one-third by MLAs, one-third by local bodies, one-twelfth each by graduates and teachers, and one-sixth nominated by the Governor for excellence in art, science, or social service.`,
            storyLink: `When a state bill passes through the Assembly, how does it overcome the Council's delays and reach the Governor's desk to become law?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Legislative Assembly (Art. 170):</strong> Directly elected by universal adult franchise; strength ranges from 60 to 500 (constitutional exceptions: Sikkim 32, Goa &amp; Mizoram 40; Puducherry UT 30). Normal tenure is 5 years; extendable by 1 year at a time during National Emergency.`,
              `<strong>Legislative Council (Art. 171):</strong> Permanent continuing chamber (cannot be dissolved); minimum 40 members, maximum 1/3rd of Assembly strength; 1/3rd retire every 2 years; 6-year member term.`,
              `<strong>Council Composition Breakdown:</strong> 1/3rd elected by local bodies (municipalities/district boards), 1/3rd by MLAs, 1/12th by 3-year graduates, 1/12th by 3-year secondary+ teachers, and 1/6th nominated by Governor (Literature, Science, Art, Social Service, and Cooperative Movement).`,
              `<strong>Qualifications:</strong> Citizen of India; minimum age 25 years for Legislative Assembly and 30 years for Legislative Council.`
            ],
            landmark: `Cooperative Movement is an explicit fifth nomination category for State Legislative Councils (not present in the Union Upper House's 4 categories).`,
            takeaway: `Unlike the Union Upper House, 5/6th of a State Legislative Council is indirectly elected across 4 distinct electorates and 1/6th is nominated.`
          },
          {
            code: `10.3`,
            title: `State Legislative Procedure & Governor's Assent`,
            articles: `Art. 196–201`,
            summary: `State bills follow the same ordinary-money-financial logic, but the Governor's assent adds a presidential reference option.`,
            story: `In state lawmaking, the Legislative Council is far weaker than the Rajya Sabha: it cannot veto an ordinary bill passed by the Assembly, but can merely delay it for up to four months (three months in the first round, one month in the second) before the bill passes automatically. Once cleared, the bill arrives on the Governor's desk under Article 200. The Governor can give assent, withhold assent, return it for reconsideration, or utilize a unique federal weapon: reserve the bill for the consideration of the President of India under Article 201.`,
            storyLink: `This reservation power brings us directly to the central, controversial figure in state politics: the Governor of the State.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Ordinary Bills (Art. 196–197):</strong> Can originate in either House; if passed by Assembly and rejected/altered/delayed by Council, Assembly passes it a second time — Council can delay for MAX 4 months total (3 months first instance + 1 month second instance).`,
              `<strong>No Joint Sitting in States:</strong> The Constitution provides NO joint sitting mechanism for resolving deadlocks between a State Assembly and Legislative Council; the Assembly's will always prevails.`,
              `<strong>Money Bills (Art. 198–199):</strong> Can originate ONLY in the Assembly with prior Governor recommendation; Council can hold it for MAX 14 days without amendment authority.`,
              `<strong>Governor's Assent (Art. 200) &amp; President Reservation (Art. 201):</strong> Governor has four options — give assent, withhold assent, return non-Money Bill once for reconsideration, or reserve the bill for the President's consideration (obligatory if the bill endangers High Court position). Under Art. 201, the President faces no time limit and is not bound to assent even if re-passed.`
            ],
            landmark: `In a state, the Legislative Council is strictly an advisory/delaying chamber (max 4 months for ordinary bills, 14 days for money bills).`,
            takeaway: `Compare deadlocks: Union Parliament uses Joint Sitting (Art. 108); State Legislatures have NO Joint Sitting — the Assembly overrides after 4 months.`
          },
          {
            code: `10.4`,
            title: `Governor & State Executive`,
            articles: `Art. 153–167 & 213`,
            summary: `The Governor is the state's constitutional head, appointed by the President, with discretion in reserved bills, President's Rule, and Sixth Schedule matters.`,
            story: `Under Article 153, every State has a Governor appointed directly by the President, serving at the pleasure of the Union Government for a five-year term. The Governor occupies a dual personality: they are the dignified constitutional head of the state executive acting on the advice of the Chief Minister's cabinet, but also the Union's direct federal watchdog. When a hung assembly occurs, when state law and order collapses, or when recommending President's Rule under Article 356, the Governor exercises independent discretion—making the office one of the most fiercely debated battlegrounds in Indian federalism.`,
            storyLink: `While Governors and state assemblies manage local affairs, what common linguistic framework unites this vast multilingual country during official business and governance?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Dual Role of Governor:</strong> Constitutional head of the state and vital link/agent of the Union Government; appointed by the President for a 5-year term (holds office at President's pleasure); 7th Amendment (1956) allowed one Governor for two or more states.`,
              `<strong>Oath &amp; Qualifications:</strong> Citizen of India, 35+ years; Oath (Art. 159) administered by Chief Justice of the state High Court.`,
              `<strong>Discretionary &amp; Pardoning Powers:</strong> Constitutional discretion (reserving bills under Art. 200, recommending President's Rule under Art. 356, Sixth Schedule matters) plus situational discretion; Pardoning power (Art. 161) covers state laws ONLY and CANNOT pardon death sentences or court-martial.`,
              `<strong>Chief Minister &amp; State Council (Art. 163–164):</strong> 91st Amendment capped State Council at 15% of Assembly strength, with a minimum of 12 ministers including the CM.`
            ],
            landmark: `Governor's Ordinance power (Art. 213) mirrors the President's Art. 123, except the Governor needs prior Presidential instructions for subjects requiring Presidential assent.`,
            takeaway: `Although a Governor cannot pardon a death sentence, they can suspend, remit, or commute a death sentence under state law.`
          },
          {
            code: `10.5`,
            title: `Official Language (Part XVII)`,
            articles: `Art. 343–351`,
            summary: `Part XVII makes Hindi the Union's official language, keeps English, protects regional languages, and recognises 22 scheduled and 11 classical languages.`,
            story: `During the Constituent Assembly, passionate arguments erupted over national language: southern leaders resisted Hindi imposition, while northern delegates demanded it. The framers forged the famous Munshi-Ayyangar formula in Part XVII (Articles 343–351): India has no 'National Language'; instead, Hindi in Devanagari script is the 'Official Language of the Union,' with English retained as an associate official language. The 8th Schedule recognizes 22 regional languages (expanding from 14 through the 21st, 71st, and 92nd CAAs), while 11 languages enjoy prestigious 'Classical' status, balancing national communication with linguistic self-respect.`,
            storyLink: `Balancing regional languages and state autonomy with a powerful central government requires an intricate web of federal relations between the Centre and the States.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Language of the Union (Art. 343–344):</strong> Hindi in Devanagari script is the official language of the Union; English was permitted for 15 years and continued indefinitely by the Official Languages Act, 1963; the first Official Language Commission was headed by B.G. Kher (1955).`,
              `<strong>Regional Languages (Art. 345–347):</strong> A State may adopt any language or Hindi; Article 347 empowers the President to recognise a minority language for official purposes.`,
              `<strong>Judicial Language (Art. 348–349):</strong> Authoritative texts of Supreme Court and High Court proceedings and of Bills and Acts are in English.`,
              `<strong>Special Directives (Art. 350A–351):</strong> Article 350A provides for mother-tongue primary education (7th CAA 1956); Article 350B creates the Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities; Article 351 directs the development and spread of Hindi.`,
              `<strong>Eighth Schedule &amp; Classical Languages:</strong> The Eighth Schedule now contains 22 languages; 11 languages are recognised as classical — Tamil, Sanskrit, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam, and Odia, plus Marathi, Pali, Prakrit, Assamese, and Bengali added in October 2024.`,
              `<strong>No National Language:</strong> The Constitution declares Hindi the official language of the Union, not a national language.`
            ],
            landmark: `The Official Languages Act 1963 continued English; the 7th CAA 1956 added Articles 350A and 350B.`,
            takeaway: `22 scheduled languages, 11 classical languages, and Hindi as the official — not national — language.`
          }        ]

