      {
        id: 11,
        unitId: 2,
        title: `Centre-State Relations`,
        articles: `Articles 245–293 (Parts XI & XII)`,
        summary: `Analyzes India's legislative, administrative, and financial federal distribution, Seventh Schedule dynamics, Finance & GST Councils, and landmark federalism commissions.`,
        story: `Centre and states then negotiate the federal bargain through lists, taxes, councils, and commissions.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `11.1`,
            title: `Legislative Relations & Seventh Schedule Lists`,
            articles: `Art. 245–255`,
            summary: `Articles 245 to 255 divide legislative power through the Union, State, and Concurrent Lists, with Union supremacy in conflicts.`,
            story: `To prevent the Indian Union from tearing itself apart like other fragile federations, Articles 245 to 255 establish a strict division of lawmaking authority backed by the 7th Schedule. The Union List covers national priorities (defense, foreign affairs, atomic energy, banking); the State List covers grassroots governance (police, public health, agriculture); while the Concurrent List covers shared domains (criminal law, education, forests). If a conflict arises over a concurrent subject, the doctrine of federal supremacy ensures the Union law prevails, and all residuary legislative powers belong exclusively to Parliament under Article 248.`,
            storyLink: `Sharing legislative boundaries is only the first step; how does New Delhi ensure that 28 state governments actually implement central directives on the ground?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Territorial Extent (Art. 245):</strong> Parliament legislates for whole/part of India and possesses exclusive extra-territorial legislative power; State Legislatures legislate within state borders.`,
              `<strong>Subject Distribution (Art. 246 &amp; 7th Schedule):</strong> Union List (100 subjects — defence, banking, foreign affairs, railways), State List (61 subjects — police, public health, agriculture), Concurrent List (52 subjects — education, forests, marriage, criminal law). Residuary powers vest in Parliament (Art. 248).`,
              `<strong>42nd Amendment Shift (1976):</strong> Transferred 5 subjects from State List to Concurrent List — Education, Forests, Weights &amp; Measures, Protection of Wild Animals &amp; Birds, and Administration of Justice (constitution of courts except SC &amp; HCs).`,
              `<strong>Five Conditions for Parliament to Legislate on State List:</strong> Upper House resolution in national interest by 2/3rd present &amp; voting (Art. 249); National Emergency (Art. 250); Request by 2+ states (Art. 252); Implementing international treaties (Art. 253); During President's Rule (Art. 356).`
            ],
            landmark: `Doctrine of Pith and Substance and Doctrine of Colourable Legislation are used by courts to resolve legislative list overlaps.`,
            takeaway: `In case of conflict on a Concurrent List subject, Union law prevails unless the State law received Presidential assent under Article 254(2).`
          },
          {
            code: `11.2`,
            title: `Administrative Relations & Inter-State Bodies`,
            articles: `Art. 256–263`,
            summary: `Articles 256 to 263 make the Union and states cooperate through governors, inter-state councils, and mutual delegation.`,
            story: `Articles 256 to 263 construct the administrative bridge between the Centre and the States. State governments are constitutionally obligated to exercise their executive power to comply with central laws, and Parliament can issue direct binding directions regarding railways, communication, and border protection. If a state brazenly defies central instructions, Article 365 empowers the President to deem it a breakdown of constitutional machinery and impose President's Rule. To resolve water feuds and coordinate policies peacefully, the Constitution creates Inter-State River Water Tribunals (Art 262) and enables the Inter-State Council under Article 263.`,
            storyLink: `The ultimate test of federal peace is money: who collects the taxes, and how is the national revenue divided between New Delhi and the states?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Executive Power Symmetry:</strong> Union executive power mirrors Union legislative subjects; State executive power covers State List unless limited by Union laws.`,
              `<strong>Centre's Directions to States (Art. 256–257):</strong> Ensures compliance with parliamentary laws and protects national highways, railways, and communication networks; failure invites President's Rule trigger under Article 365.`,
              `<strong>All-India Services (Art. 312):</strong> IAS, IPS, and Indian Forest Service (added 1966) are recruited/trained by the Centre and serve both Centre and States; Parliament creates new All-India Services only after a Council of States resolution by 2/3rd present &amp; voting.`,
              `<strong>Water Disputes &amp; Inter-State Council (Art. 262–263):</strong> Art. 262 enables Parliament to bar SC/court jurisdiction over inter-state river disputes (River Boards Act &amp; Inter-State Water Disputes Act 1956); Art. 263 provides for the Inter-State Council (established 1990, chaired by PM).`
            ],
            landmark: `Zonal Councils (5 + North-Eastern Council) are STATUTORY bodies established under the States Reorganisation Act 1956 (chaired by the Union Home Minister), NOT constitutional bodies.`,
            takeaway: `Inter-State Council (Art. 263) is Constitutional (PM chairs); Zonal Councils are Statutory (Union Home Minister chairs).`
          },
          {
            code: `11.3`,
            title: `Financial Relations, Finance Commission & GST Council`,
            articles: `Art. 268–293`,
            summary: `Articles 268 to 293 divide taxes, create the Finance Commission, and route GST through the GST Council.`,
            story: `In Indian federalism, the Centre possesses the most lucrative tax engines (income tax, customs, GST), while the States shoulder the heaviest public expenditures (healthcare, education, agriculture). To resolve this structural vertical imbalance, Article 280 mandates the appointment of an independent Finance Commission every five years to calculate the fair tax-devolution formula. In 2017, the 101st Amendment revolutionized federal finances by creating the GST Council under Article 279A—a constitutional body where the Centre holds one-third voting weight and the States hold two-thirds, forcing cooperative fiscal consensus.`,
            storyLink: `Decades of political tensions between overbearing Union leaders and regional chief ministers prompted comprehensive inquiry commissions to protect the federal spirit.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Taxation &amp; Grants:</strong> Divided taxation powers; Statutory Grants-in-Aid to needy states on Finance Commission advice (Art. 275); Discretionary Grants by Centre/States for any public purpose (Art. 282).`,
              `<strong>Finance Commission (Art. 280):</strong> Quasi-judicial body constituted by the President every 5 years (Chairman + 4 members); recommends vertical/horizontal tax devolution and grant principles (16th Finance Commission chaired by Dr. Arvind Panagariya).`,
              `<strong>GST &amp; GST Council (101st Amendment 2016):</strong> Inserted Art. 246A (concurrent GST power) and Art. 279A (GST Council); chaired by Union Finance Minister with Union MoS Finance and State Finance Ministers; decisions require 3/4th weighted majority (Centre 1/3rd weight, States 2/3rd weight).`,
              `<strong>Borrowing Powers (Art. 292–293):</strong> Centre can borrow within or outside India; States can borrow ONLY within India and need Central consent if any Central loan remains outstanding.`
            ],
            landmark: `In Mohit Minerals (2022), the Supreme Court held that GST Council recommendations have persuasive value for cooperative federalism rather than binding legislatures unilaterally.`,
            takeaway: `States cannot raise external/foreign loans directly under Article 293.`
          },
          {
            code: `11.4`,
            title: `Federalism Commissions & Cooperative Reform`,
            articles: `1966–2010 Reports`,
            summary: `The Sarkaria and Punchhi Commissions diagnosed centralisation, while the 101st Amendment and GST Council pushed cooperative federalism forward.`,
            story: `The 1970s and 80s witnessed severe federal friction: New Delhi routinely dismissed opposition state governments under Article 356 and delayed developmental funds. In response, the Union appointed the Sarkaria Commission (1983) and later the Punchhi Commission (2007) to diagnose central overreach. Both commissions strongly advocated 'Cooperative Federalism'—demanding that Article 356 be used only as an extreme last resort (later codified by the Supreme Court in the S.R. Bommai case 1994), that Governors be non-partisan figures appointed after consulting Chief Ministers, and that the Inter-State Council meet regularly.`,
            storyLink: `Having established how the executive and legislature govern in Units I and II, who steps in when the government breaks the law or violates human rights? That brings us to Unit III and the Indian Judiciary.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>First ARC (1966) &amp; Rajamannar Committee (1969):</strong> ARC (Morarji Desai / K. Hanumanthaiah) recommended Inter-State Council; Tamil Nadu's Rajamannar Committee sought abolition of IAS/IPS and All-India Services (rejected by Centre).`,
              `<strong>Sarkaria Commission (1983–1988):</strong> Chaired by R.S. Sarkaria; submitted 247 recommendations — favored strong Centre for national unity, permanent Inter-State Council (Art. 263), active consultation with Chief Ministers on Governor appointment, and Article 356 strictly as last resort.`,
              `<strong>Punchhi Commission (2007–2010):</strong> Chaired by former CJI M.M. Punchhi; recommended fixed 5-year Governor tenure, Governor impeachment procedure, localized emergency guidelines, and 6-month limit for Presidential consideration of state bills.`
            ],
            landmark: `Most modern cooperative federalism institutions (Inter-State Council 1990, Bommai guidelines 1994) trace directly to the Sarkaria Commission framework.`,
            takeaway: `Key sequence of Centre-State panels: Rajamannar (1969) -> Sarkaria (1983) -> Punchhi (2007).`
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 12,
        unitId: 3,
        title: `Supreme Court & High Courts`,
        articles: `Articles 124–147 & 214–237`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on India's integrated judiciary: Supreme Court composition and collegium, writ and appellate jurisdiction, High Courts, subordinate courts, PIL, and tribunals.`,
        story: `The judiciary enters as arbiter: one Supreme Court, High Courts below, and writs that let citizens challenge power.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `12.1`,
            title: `Supreme Court Organization, Collegium & Removal`,
            articles: `Art. 124–129`,
            summary: `The Supreme Court's collegium selects judges, the President appoints them, and removal requires a special majority on proved misbehaviour.`,
            story: `When framing India's judicial pinnacle under Article 124, the founders unified all legal appeals under a single integrated Supreme Court, ending colonial appeals to the British Privy Council. To shield judges from political retaliation, the Constitution erected high protective walls: judges serve until 65, their salaries are charged on the Consolidated Fund without parliamentary votes, and removal is virtually impossible—requiring a punishing special majority in both Houses on 'proved misbehavior or incapacity' (a threshold no Indian judge has ever been removed under). After bitter clashes over executive packing of the courts in the 1970s, the landmark Three Judges Cases (1981–1998) birthed the unique Collegium system, where the Chief Justice and senior-most judges select their own peers to fiercely guard judicial independence.`,
            storyLink: `Armed with total independence, what exact constitutional powers and jurisdictions allow the Supreme Court to act as the ultimate umpire of the Indian Republic?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Origins &amp; Status:</strong> India has a single integrated judiciary inherited from the Government of India Act 1935; the Supreme Court replaced the Federal Court (1937) and Privy Council, was inaugurated on 28 January 1950, and is called the Guardian of the Constitution.`,
              `<strong>Strength (Art. 124(1)):</strong> Originally 1 CJI + 7 judges (8 total); presently 1 CJI + 33 judges (34 total).`,
              `<strong>Collegium Evolution:</strong> Sapru Committee (1944–45) advocated an independent judiciary; First Judges Case (S.P. Gupta, 1982) gave executive primacy; Second Judges Case (1993) created the CJI + 2 senior-most collegium; Third Judges Case (1998) expanded it to CJI + 4 senior-most; Fourth Judges Case (2015) struck down the 99th CAA and NJAC Act and restored the collegium.`,
              `<strong>Qualifications &amp; Tenure (Art. 124(3)–(4)):</strong> Citizen of India; 5 years as a High Court judge, or 10 years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President's opinion; no minimum age; retirement at 65.`,
              `<strong>Removal:</strong> President's order after an address by both Houses passed by special majority on proved misbehaviour or incapacity; procedure governed by the Judges Enquiry Act, 1968; Justice V. Ramaswami (1991) was the first judge to face proceedings.`,
              `<strong>Supporting Judges (Art. 126–128):</strong> Acting CJI, ad-hoc judges from High Courts, and attendance of retired Supreme Court judges; Art. 129 makes the Court a court of record with contempt powers, and Art. 130 fixes its seat at Delhi.`
            ],
            landmark: `Fourth Judges Case (2015) struck down the 99th Constitutional Amendment and the NJAC Act, restoring the collegium system.`,
            takeaway: `Supreme Court strength is 34 (CJI + 33); Supreme Court judges retire at 65, High Court judges at 62.`
          },
          {
            code: `12.2`,
            title: `Jurisdiction: Original, Writ, Appellate & Advisory`,
            articles: `Art. 131–143`,
            summary: `The Court hears original disputes, writs, appeals, and presidential references, making it the guardian of the Constitution.`,
            story: `The Supreme Court wields four formidable constitutional jurisdictions: Original Jurisdiction under Article 131 allows it to resolve federal fistfights between the Centre and States; Writ Jurisdiction under Article 32 empowers it to enforce Fundamental Rights directly; and Appellate Jurisdiction under Articles 132–136 allows it to hear civil, criminal, and special leave petitions from across the nation. Finally, under Advisory Jurisdiction (Article 143), the President can refer complex constitutional questions to the Court—though the Court's opinion remains advisory rather than binding. As a Court of Record (Article 129), its judgments become permanent legal precedents, backed by the power to punish for contempt.`,
            storyLink: `While the Supreme Court sits at the apex in New Delhi, the daily shield protecting citizens in every state is the High Court and the subordinate judiciary.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Original Jurisdiction (Art. 131):</strong> Exclusive federal disputes between the Centre and States or between States; excludes pre-Constitution treaties, inter-State river water disputes, and Finance Commission matters.`,
              `<strong>Appellate Jurisdiction (Art. 132–134):</strong> Constitutional, civil, and criminal appeals from High Courts, subject to the thresholds and certificates prescribed in these articles.`,
              `<strong>Special Leave Petition (Art. 136):</strong> Discretionary power to grant leave against any judgment or order of any court or tribunal in India, except military tribunals.`,
              `<strong>Review, Binding Law &amp; Complete Justice (Art. 137, 141, 142):</strong> Power to review its own judgments; law declared by the Supreme Court binds all courts; Art. 142 allows any order necessary for complete justice, used in the Bhopal gas case, Vishakha Guidelines (1997), and the Ayodhya judgment (2019).`,
              `<strong>Advisory &amp; Curative Remedies (Art. 143):</strong> The President may seek the Court's opinion on questions of law or fact of public importance or on pre-Constitution treaties; the advice is not binding; a curative petition was recognized in Rupa Ashok Hurra v. Ashok Hurra (2002).`,
              `<strong>Writ Jurisdiction (Art. 32):</strong> The Supreme Court can issue the five writs for enforcement of Fundamental Rights; this remedy is itself a Fundamental Right and cannot be refused.`
            ],
            landmark: `Art. 142 was used to award compensation in Union Carbide and to frame the Vishakha Guidelines (1997).`,
            takeaway: `Special Leave Petition under Art. 136 is discretionary; advisory opinions under Art. 143 are not binding.`
          },
          {
            code: `12.3`,
            title: `High Courts & Subordinate Judiciary`,
            articles: `Art. 214–237`,
            summary: `High Courts supervise subordinate courts, while Lok Adalats and NALSA widen access to cheap, speedy justice.`,
            story: `Under Article 214, High Courts stand as the constitutional guardians of each state, enjoying powers that are in some ways even broader than the Supreme Court's: under Article 226, a High Court can issue writs not just for Fundamental Rights, but for any legal right whatsoever. High Court judges retire at 62 and are appointed by the President via the Collegium. Below them, District Courts manage civil and criminal trials under the administrative supervision of the High Court. To prevent crushing case backlogs from bankrupting poor litigants, Parliament established statutory Lok Adalats under the Legal Services Authorities Act (NALSA 1987), providing free legal aid and fast-track dispute settlement based on compromise.`,
            storyLink: `The judiciary did not remain a passive spectator waiting for wealthy litigants to file appeals; in the 1980s, judges unlocked a revolutionary legal weapon—Judicial Activism and PIL.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Origins &amp; Number:</strong> The first High Courts were set up in Calcutta, Bombay, and Madras in 1862 under the Indian High Courts Act, 1861; India presently has 25 High Courts.`,
              `<strong>Establishment &amp; Common High Courts (Art. 214–217, 231):</strong> A High Court for each State; the 7th CAA 1956 allows Parliament to create a common High Court for two or more States or Union Territories, such as Bombay (Maharashtra, Goa, DNH &amp; DD), Guwahati (Assam, Nagaland, Mizoram, Arunachal Pradesh), and Punjab &amp; Haryana.`,
              `<strong>Appointment &amp; Qualifications:</strong> Appointed by the President after consulting the CJI, Governor, and Chief Justice of the High Court; citizen of India; 10 years of judicial office or 10 years as a High Court advocate; retirement at 62; there is no distinguished-jurist category.`,
              `<strong>Jurisdiction &amp; Superintendence (Art. 226–227):</strong> Writ jurisdiction is wider than Art. 32 because it covers Fundamental Rights and ordinary legal rights; Art. 227 gives superintendence over all courts and tribunals in the High Court's jurisdiction.`,
              `<strong>Financial Protection (Art. 221):</strong> High Court judges' salaries are charged to the Consolidated Fund of the State, while pensions are charged to the Consolidated Fund of India.`,
              `<strong>Subordinate Courts (Art. 233–237):</strong> District Judges are appointed by the Governor in consultation with the High Court; the Sessions Judge tries serious criminal cases and a death sentence requires High Court confirmation.`,
              `<strong>Lok Adalats &amp; Legal Aid:</strong> Lok Adalats, given statutory status by the Legal Services Authorities Act 1987, settle disputes through conciliation; their awards are final and binding, and they function under the National Legal Services Authority (NALSA).`
            ],
            landmark: `Art. 226 jurisdiction is wider than Art. 32; High Court writ relief is discretionary.`,
            takeaway: `25 High Courts; High Court judges retire at 62; salaries come from the State Fund and pensions from the Union Fund.`
          },
          {
            code: `12.4`,
            title: `Judicial Review, Activism & PIL`,
            articles: `Art. 13, 32, 226 & 323A–323B`,
            summary: `Judicial review, activism, and PIL turned Articles 13, 32, and 226 into tools for citizens against state excess.`,
            story: `In traditional British law, only a direct victim could approach a court; if a bonded laborer was chained to a brick kiln or prisoners were blinded in jail, they could never afford a lawyer. In the late 1970s and 80s, Justices P.N. Bhagwati and V.R. Krishna Iyer broke this colonial barrier by introducing Public Interest Litigation (PIL). The Supreme Court waived technical rules of locus standi, accepting even handwritten postcards or newspaper reports as writ petitions on behalf of voiceless citizens. Coupled with the fierce power of Judicial Review under Article 13, the court transformed itself from a quiet hall of elite disputes into a people's court fighting bonded labor, custodial torture, and environmental destruction.`,
            storyLink: `But what happens when the very existence of the nation is endangered by war, foreign invasion, or total rebellion? That forces the Constitution to deploy its most drastic weapon: Emergency Provisions.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Judicial Review:</strong> Power of courts to examine the constitutionality of legislative and executive action; anchors are Arts. 13, 32, and 226; the doctrine traces to Marbury v. Madison (1803) in the USA.`,
              `<strong>PIL &amp; Locus Standi:</strong> Public Interest Litigation, borrowed from the USA, was pioneered by Justices P.N. Bhagwati and V.R. Krishna Iyer; it relaxes the traditional rule of locus standi; the first reported case was Hussainara Khatoon (1979), and the principle was formalized in S.P. Gupta (1981).`,
              `<strong>Judicial Activism:</strong> The term was coined by Arthur Schlesinger Jr.; Indian courts have used PIL and Art. 142 to protect rights, environmental interests, and constitutional governance.`,
              `<strong>Tribunals (Part XIVA, Art. 323A–323B):</strong> Added by the 42nd CAA 1976 on the Swaran Singh Committee's recommendation; Art. 323A covers administrative tribunals (CAT and SAT) created only by Parliament, while Art. 323B covers tribunals for taxation, foreign exchange, industrial disputes, and land reforms created by Parliament or State legislatures.`,
              `<strong>Judicial Review of Tribunals:</strong> In L. Chandra Kumar v. UOI (1997), the Supreme Court held that tribunals remain subject to the writ jurisdiction of High Courts under Arts. 226 and 227; judicial review cannot be excluded.`,
              `<strong>Judiciary Firsts:</strong> H.J. Kania was the first CJI; Justice Fathima Beevi was the first female Supreme Court judge (1989); Anna Chandy was the first female High Court judge; Leila Seth was the first female High Court Chief Justice; Justice B.V. Nagarathna is projected to be India's first female CJI in September 2027.`
            ],
            landmark: `L. Chandra Kumar (1997) protected High Court writ jurisdiction over tribunals; PIL was formalized in S.P. Gupta (1981).`,
            takeaway: `Tribunals cannot exclude judicial review under Arts. 226–227; Hussainara Khatoon (1979) was the first reported PIL.`
          },
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 13,
        unitId: 3,
        title: `Emergency Provisions`,
        articles: `Articles 352–360 (Part XVIII)`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on the three constitutional emergencies under Part XVIII: National Emergency, President's Rule, and Financial Emergency, including approval thresholds, effects, judicial review, and historical invocations.`,
        story: `When the nation faces war, breakdown, or financial strain, the emergency provisions tighten the Union's grip.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `13.1`,
            title: `National Emergency: Grounds, Approval & Impact`,
            articles: `Art. 352, 358, 359`,
            summary: `A National Emergency under Article 352 can be declared on war, external aggression, or armed rebellion, and it alters federal and rights equations.`,
            story: `When the state faces existential peril, Article 352 allows the President to proclaim a National Emergency on grounds of War, External Aggression, or Armed Rebellion. The world watched in shock during the 1975 Emergency when Prime Minister Indira Gandhi invoked the vague term 'internal disturbance' to declare emergency without cabinet consultation, jailing opposition leaders and censoring the press. The historic 44th Amendment in 1978 repaired these cracks: it eliminated 'internal disturbance', required written advice from the Union Cabinet, mandated special parliamentary approval within one month, and declared that Articles 20 and 21 can never be suspended, preserving the right to life even in the darkest hours.`,
            storyLink: `Beyond nationwide war emergencies, how does the Union step in when a single state government collapses into political chaos or constitutional defiance?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Grounds (Art. 352):</strong> War, external aggression, or armed rebellion; the 44th CAA 1978 replaced the phrase internal disturbance with armed rebellion.`,
              `<strong>Proclamation &amp; Approval:</strong> The President may proclaim only on the written advice of the Cabinet (the word Cabinet was inserted by the 44th CAA); both Houses must approve within one month by special majority; it continues for six months at a time and can be extended indefinitely with fresh six-month approvals.`,
              `<strong>Revocation:</strong> The President may revoke it at any time; the Lok Sabha can disapprove it by simple majority on a resolution signed by at least one-tenth of its members.`,
              `<strong>Judicial Review:</strong> The 38th CAA 1975 made the proclamation non-justiciable, but Minerva Mills (1980) restored judicial review.`,
              `<strong>Effect on Fundamental Rights (Art. 358 vs 359):</strong> Art. 358 automatically suspends Art. 19 only for war or external aggression, not armed rebellion; Art. 359 lets the President suspend the right to enforce other Fundamental Rights, but Arts. 20 and 21 can never be suspended (44th CAA).`,
              `<strong>Invocations:</strong> 1962 (Indo-China War), 1971 (Indo-Pak War), and 1975 (internal disturbance under Indira Gandhi and President Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed); 25 June is observed as Samvidhan Hatya Diwas.`
            ],
            landmark: `Minerva Mills (1980) restored judicial review of a National Emergency; the 44th CAA 1978 replaced internal disturbance with armed rebellion.`,
            takeaway: `National Emergency approval is one month, versus two months for President's Rule and Financial Emergency.`
          },
          {
            code: `13.2`,
            title: `President's Rule / State Emergency`,
            articles: `Art. 355, 356, 365`,
            summary: `President's Rule under Article 356 suspends state autonomy when constitutional machinery fails, but it needs parliamentary approval and is judicially reviewable.`,
            story: `Article 356 was designed by Dr. Ambedkar as a 'dead letter' to be used only in rarest emergencies, but in post-independence politics, it became the most abused political weapon—used over 100 times by ruling central parties to unseat inconvenient state governments. Imposed when the Governor reports that constitutional machinery has failed (or under Article 365 when a state defies central orders), it dissolves or suspends the state assembly, transferring legislative power to Parliament. The abuse was finally chained in 1994 by the landmark S.R. Bommai judgment: the Supreme Court ruled that Article 356 is subject to judicial review, floor tests must prove majority, and assemblies cannot be dissolved until Parliament formally approves the proclamation.`,
            storyLink: `Having covered external war and state political breakdown, the Constitution includes one third, specialized emergency brake for fiscal catastrophe—the Financial Emergency.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Grounds (Art. 356 &amp; 365):</strong> Breakdown of constitutional machinery in a State, or failure of a State to comply with Union directions under Art. 365.`,
              `<strong>Approval &amp; Duration:</strong> Both Houses must approve within two months by simple majority; valid for six months at a time; maximum three years, and continuation beyond one year requires an Election Commission certificate and a National Emergency in force.`,
              `<strong>Consequences:</strong> The State legislature is dissolved or suspended; the President assumes executive powers; Parliament can legislate for the State; Fundamental Rights are not affected.`,
              `<strong>Judicial Review (S.R. Bommai, 1994):</strong> The Supreme Court held that President's Rule is subject to judicial review, secularism is part of the basic structure, and the Assembly cannot be dissolved before Parliament approves the proclamation.`,
              `<strong>Historical Facts:</strong> First imposed in Punjab (1951); most frequently in Manipur (11 times) and Uttar Pradesh (10 times); never imposed in Chhattisgarh or Telangana.`,
              `<strong>Distinction from Art. 352:</strong> President's Rule does not suspend Fundamental Rights; it changes the State's executive and legislative machinery, whereas a National Emergency affects the federal distribution of power.`
            ],
            landmark: `S.R. Bommai v. UOI (1994) made President's Rule judicially reviewable and protected the Assembly until parliamentary approval.`,
            takeaway: `Art. 356 runs for six months at a time, maximum three years; beyond one year needs an Election Commission certificate and a National Emergency.`
          },
          {
            code: `13.3`,
            title: `Financial Emergency`,
            articles: `Art. 360`,
            summary: `A Financial Emergency under Article 360 has never been used; it would let the Union cut salaries and direct state finances.`,
            story: `Article 360 holds the ultimate economic lever: if the President is satisfied that the financial stability or credit of India or any of its territories is threatened, a Financial Emergency can be declared. During such an emergency, the Union gains total authority to dictate financial propriety to states, reserve all state money bills for presidential approval, and reduce the salaries and allowances of all public servants—including the judges of the Supreme Court and High Courts. India has stood through severe balance of payments crises, including the 1991 foreign reserve crunch, but through fiscal resilience, Article 360 has never been invoked in the history of the Republic.`,
            storyLink: `Having centralized power at the national and state capitals, the Constitution underwent a historic grassroots revolution in 1992—taking democracy straight down to village panchayats and municipal wards.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Ground (Art. 360):</strong> A threat to the financial stability or credit of India; the President may proclaim a Financial Emergency on ministerial advice.`,
              `<strong>Approval:</strong> Both Houses must approve within two months by simple majority; once approved, it continues indefinitely without periodic renewal.`,
              `<strong>Effects:</strong> The Union can direct States to reduce salaries and allowances of public officials, including Supreme Court and High Court judges; all Money Bills may be reserved for the President's consideration.`,
              `<strong>Unique Position:</strong> India has never declared a Financial Emergency since the Constitution came into force in 1950.`,
              `<strong>Comparison:</strong> Approval is two months, like President's Rule, but unlike Art. 352 there is no six-month renewal cycle and no maximum duration.`
            ],
            landmark: `Art. 360 is the only emergency provision never invoked in India.`,
            takeaway: `Financial Emergency has no periodic reapproval and no maximum duration; salaries of judges can be reduced.`
          },
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 14,
        unitId: 3,
        title: `Panchayati Raj & Municipalities`,
        articles: `Articles 243–243ZG (Parts IX & IXA)`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on democratic decentralisation: Panchayati Raj committees, the 73rd and 74th Amendments, State Finance and Election Commissions, PESA, and Part IXB co-operatives.`,
        story: `From the capital, the story returns to the village and the city through panchayats, municipalities, and PESA.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `14.1`,
            title: `Committees & Evolution of Local Self-Government`,
            articles: `1957–1992`,
            summary: `From Balwant Rai Mehta to Ashok Mehta and L.M. Singhvi, committees built the case for democratic decentralisation before 1992.`,
            story: `Mahatma Gandhi passionately dreamed of Gram Swaraj—village self-rule where villages functioned as self-sustaining, autonomous little republics. Although Article 40 included village panchayats as a non-binding DPSP, decades passed with rural councils starved of funds and regular elections. A succession of expert committees built the architectural case: the Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957) proposed the classic 3-tier Panchayati Raj (Gram, Block, District, first launched in Nagaur, Rajasthan); the Ashok Mehta Committee (1977) recommended a 2-tier model with political party participation; and the L.M. Singhvi Committee (1986) demanded constitutional status to make local democracy permanent and non-negotiable.`,
            storyLink: `Armed with Singhvi's recommendations, Parliament passed the historic 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act in 1992, adding Part IX and the 11th Schedule to the Constitution.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Basis (Art. 40):</strong> The DPSP directs the State to organise village panchayats as units of self-government.`,
              `<strong>Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957):</strong> Recommended a three-tier system — Gram Panchayat (village), Panchayat Samiti (block), and Zila Parishad (district) — and democratic decentralisation; Rajasthan (Nagaur) implemented it first on 2 October 1959, followed by Andhra Pradesh.`,
              `<strong>Ashok Mehta Committee (1977):</strong> Proposed a two-tier system — Zila Parishad and Mandal Panchayat — and official participation of political parties.`,
              `<strong>G.V.K. Rao Committee (1985):</strong> Recommended the Zila Parishad as the primary body and criticized bureaucracy as a system of grass without roots.`,
              `<strong>L.M. Singhvi Committee (1986):</strong> First committee to recommend constitutional recognition for Panchayats and Nyaya Panchayats.`,
              `<strong>Thungon &amp; Gadgil Committees (1988):</strong> Recommended constitutional backing, direct elections, a five-year term, and reservation for SC/ST/women.`
            ],
            landmark: `Rajasthan's Nagaur district implemented Panchayati Raj first on 2 October 1959; 24 April is National Panchayati Raj Day.`,
            takeaway: `Balwant Rai Mehta = three tiers; Ashok Mehta = two tiers; L.M. Singhvi = first constitutional recognition.`
          },
          {
            code: `14.2`,
            title: `73rd Amendment Act & Rural Panchayats (Part IX)`,
            articles: `Art. 243–243O`,
            summary: `The 73rd Amendment created a three-tier rural panchayat system, five-year terms, reservations, State Finance Commissions, and the Eleventh Schedule of 29 subjects.`,
            story: `The 73rd Amendment Act of 1992 turned local self-government into an enforceable constitutional right by inserting Part IX (Articles 243–243O) and the 11th Schedule containing 29 developmental subjects. It mandated a uniform 3-tier Panchayati Raj system across states with over 2 million population: Gram Panchayat at the village level, Panchayat Samiti at the block, and Zila Parishad at the district. It fixed 5-year terms, created independent State Election Commissions and State Finance Commissions, and introduced a historic social revolution: mandating at least one-third reservation for women, empowering millions of rural women into elected leadership.`,
            storyLink: `While the 73rd Amendment transformed India's rural villages, a twin amendment was enacted simultaneously to modernize governance across India's rapidly growing towns and cities—the 74th Amendment.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Status:</strong> The 73rd CAA 1992 inserted Part IX (Arts. 243–243O) and the Eleventh Schedule with 29 functional subjects; it came into force on 24 April 1993.`,
              `<strong>Three-Tier Structure (Art. 243B):</strong> Village, intermediate, and district levels; a two-tier structure is allowed for States with a population below 20 lakh; the Gram Sabha (Art. 243A) consists of registered village voters.`,
              `<strong>Elections &amp; Reservation (Art. 243C–243D):</strong> Direct elections for all Panchayat members; chairpersons at intermediate and district levels are elected indirectly; SC/ST reservation is proportional to population, with at least one-third seats for women; the minimum contesting age is 21.`,
              `<strong>Term &amp; Reconstitution (Art. 243E):</strong> Five-year term; fresh elections within six months of dissolution; a member elected to a casual vacancy serves only the remainder of the term.`,
              `<strong>Commissions &amp; Audits (Art. 243I–243K):</strong> State Finance Commission every five years, audit of Panchayat accounts, and a State Election Commission appointed by the Governor.`,
              `<strong>Application &amp; Protection (Art. 243L–243O):</strong> Special provisions for Union Territories, exemptions for Scheduled and tribal areas, one-year transition for existing laws, and a bar on court interference in electoral matters.`
            ],
            landmark: `73rd CAA 1992 inserted Part IX and the Eleventh Schedule with 29 subjects; it came into force on 24 April 1993.`,
            takeaway: `Minimum age 21; one-third seats for women; five-year term; State Election Commission under Art. 243K.`
          },
          {
            code: `14.3`,
            title: `74th Amendment Act & Municipalities`,
            articles: `Art. 243P–243ZG`,
            summary: `The 74th Amendment created nagar panchayats, municipal councils, and corporations, with District and Metropolitan Planning Committees.`,
            story: `Recognizing that unchecked urbanization required democratic urban governance, Parliament enacted the 74th Amendment Act of 1992, inserting Part IXA (Articles 243P–243ZG) and the 12th Schedule with 18 municipal functions. It structured urban local bodies into three tiers: Nagar Panchayats for transitional rural-urban areas, Municipal Councils for smaller towns, and Municipal Corporations for large metropolises (led by a Mayor). To coordinate regional development between bustling cities and surrounding rural belts, the Act created District Planning Committees (DPCs) and Metropolitan Planning Committees (MPCs).`,
            storyLink: `While Panchayati Raj transformed standard rural India, indigenous tribal communities required special protection to prevent external modern laws from destroying their ancient customary traditions.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Status:</strong> The 74th CAA 1992 inserted Part IXA (Arts. 243P–243ZG) and the Twelfth Schedule with 18 functional subjects.`,
              `<strong>Types of Municipalities (Art. 243Q):</strong> Nagar Panchayat for a transitional rural-urban area, Municipal Council for a smaller urban area, and Municipal Corporation for a larger urban area.`,
              `<strong>Planning Committees:</strong> District Planning Committee (Art. 243ZD) with four-fifths of members elected by Zila Parishad and municipal elected members; Metropolitan Planning Committee (Art. 243ZE) with two-thirds elected by municipal members and Panchayat chairpersons.`,
              `<strong>Historical Evolution:</strong> Madras (1687–88) had India's first Municipal Corporation; Bombay and Calcutta followed in 1726; Lord Mayo's Resolution (1870) introduced financial decentralisation; Lord Ripon's Resolution (1882) is called the Magna Carta of Local Self-Government.`,
              `<strong>Part IXB Co-operatives:</strong> Arts. 243ZH–243ZT were added by the 97th CAA 2011; the right to form co-operatives flows from Art. 19(1)(c) and Art. 43B.`
            ],
            landmark: `The 74th CAA 1992 inserted Part IXA and the Twelfth Schedule; the 97th CAA 2011 inserted Part IXB on co-operatives.`,
            takeaway: `18 municipal subjects versus 29 Panchayat subjects; the DPC has four-fifths elected members and the MPC two-thirds.`
          },
          {
            code: `14.4`,
            title: `PESA Act, 1996`,
            articles: `Panchayats (Extension to Scheduled Areas) Act`,
            summary: `PESA extends panchayats to Fifth Schedule areas and makes the Gram Sabha the guardian of tribal land, forests, and minor minerals.`,
            story: `Traditional tribal communities have governed their forests, water bodies, and community land through customary elders for thousands of years, meaning standard bureaucratic panchayats could alienate tribal identity. Based on the recommendations of the Dilip Singh Bhuria Committee, Parliament enacted the Panchayats (Extension to Scheduled Areas) Act (PESA) in 1996, extending Part IX into Fifth Schedule tribal areas across ten states. PESA made the village Gram Sabha the supreme sovereign guardian: empowering tribal elders to preserve customs, manage minor forest produce, regulate local markets, and mandate prior consultation before any land acquisition or mining lease.`,
            storyLink: `Beyond local councils and tribal panchayats, how does the Constitution directly administer strategic federal territories and autonomous tribal homelands across India?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Purpose:</strong> The Panchayats (Extension to the Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996 extends Part IX to Fifth Schedule areas in 10 States, where the ordinary Panchayati Raj provisions would otherwise not apply in full.`,
              `<strong>Gram Sabha Powers:</strong> The Gram Sabha is empowered over minor forest produce, approval of development plans, recommendations for mining leases, and protection of tribal land.`,
              `<strong>Reservation:</strong> Chairpersons at all levels are reserved for Scheduled Tribes, and the Gram Sabha is the primary decision-making body in Scheduled Areas.`,
              `<strong>Scope:</strong> PESA applies to Fifth Schedule areas; Sixth Schedule areas are governed by Autonomous District Councils instead.`
            ],
            landmark: `PESA 1996 extends Part IX to Fifth Schedule areas and makes the Gram Sabha the central institution in those areas.`,
            takeaway: `PESA = Fifth Schedule + Gram Sabha empowerment + ST chairpersons; Sixth Schedule areas use ADCs, not PESA.`
          }        ]

      },
      {
        id: 15,
        unitId: 3,
        title: `Union Territories & Scheduled Areas`,
        articles: `Articles 239–244A (Parts VIII & X)`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on Union Territories and the Fifth and Sixth Schedules: Delhi's special status, J&K reorganisation, Scheduled Areas, Tribes Advisory Councils, and Autonomous District Councils.`,
        story: `The Union Territories and Scheduled Areas show how the Constitution handles difference with tailored maps.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `15.1`,
            title: `Union Territories & Special Status of Delhi`,
            articles: `Art. 239–241`,
            summary: `Union Territories are centrally administered, but Delhi and Puducherry have legislatures; Delhi's 69th Amendment created a hybrid federal arrangement.`,
            story: `Under Part VIII (Articles 239–242), Union Territories are administered directly by the President through appointed Administrators or Lieutenant Governors. While most UTs are managed from New Delhi due to small size, strategic borders, or cultural uniqueness (like Chandigarh, Lakshadweep, and Ladakh), Puducherry, Jammu & Kashmir, and Delhi were granted elected legislative assemblies. The 69th Amendment in 1991 gave Delhi special status as the National Capital Territory (Article 239AA), creating an elected assembly and council of ministers, but deliberately excluding three vital sovereign subjects—Public Order, Police, and Land—which remain under central control through the Lieutenant Governor.`,
            storyLink: `For indigenous tribal populations spread across mainland India, the Constitution created a specialized protective administrative framework—the Fifth Schedule.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Administration (Art. 239–241):</strong> Union Territories are administered by the President through a Lieutenant Governor or Administrator; Delhi, Puducherry, and Jammu &amp; Kashmir have legislatures.`,
              `<strong>Delhi's Special Status (69th CAA 1991, Art. 239AA):</strong> The National Capital Territory has an elected Assembly and Council of Ministers; the ministerial cap is 10% of Assembly strength (seven ministers); police, public order, and land remain with the Centre.`,
              `<strong>Jammu &amp; Kashmir Reorganisation (31 October 2019):</strong> The State was bifurcated into the Union Territory of Jammu &amp; Kashmir (with a legislature) and the Union Territory of Ladakh (without a legislature); the J&amp;K Legislative Council was abolished.`,
              `<strong>Assembly Seats:</strong> 24 seats in the J&amp;K Assembly remain vacant for areas in Pakistan-occupied Kashmir.`,
              `<strong>Judicial Position:</strong> The GNCTD judgments (2018 and 2023) clarified the balance between the elected Delhi government and the Lieutenant Governor; the Lieutenant Governor remains the administrative head.`
            ],
            landmark: `69th CAA 1991 created Art. 239AA for Delhi; the J&amp;K Reorganisation Act 2019 created the Union Territories of J&amp;K and Ladakh.`,
            takeaway: `Delhi's Council of Ministers is capped at 10% (seven), not 15%; Ladakh is a Union Territory without a legislature.`
          },
          {
            code: `15.2`,
            title: `Fifth Schedule: Scheduled Areas & Tribes`,
            articles: `Art. 244(1)`,
            summary: `The Fifth Schedule governs Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes through governors' reports, Tribes Advisory Councils, and special powers.`,
            story: `Article 244(1) and the Fifth Schedule provide a unique constitutional umbrella over tribal areas in 10 mainland states (including Madhya Pradesh, Odisha, Jharkhand, and Andhra Pradesh). Recognizing that vulnerable tribals could easily be cheated out of ancestral lands by moneylenders and mining conglomerates, the Constitution grants the Governor exceptional legislative powers: the Governor can suspend or modify any Act of Parliament or State Assembly in scheduled areas. Every Fifth Schedule state must establish a Tribes Advisory Council (consisting of up to 20 members, three-fourths of whom are tribal MLAs) to advise on welfare and land protection.`,
            storyLink: `While Fifth Schedule tribes operate with gubernatorial oversight, four northeastern border states were granted complete legislative autonomy under their own elected tribal councils—the Sixth Schedule.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Basis (Art. 244(1)):</strong> The Fifth Schedule governs the administration of Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes in States other than Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, and Mizoram.`,
              `<strong>Scheduled Areas:</strong> The President declares Scheduled Areas; 10 States currently have them — Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Chhattisgarh, Gujarat, Himachal Pradesh, Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Odisha, and Rajasthan.`,
              `<strong>Governor's Role:</strong> The Governor has special responsibility for Scheduled Areas, submits an annual report to the President, and may direct that a law of Parliament or the State legislature does not apply, or applies with modifications.`,
              `<strong>Tribes Advisory Council:</strong> A 20-member council advises the Governor; three-fourths of its members must be ST MLAs.`,
              `<strong>Land &amp; PESA:</strong> The Governor can make regulations for the allotment of land, the prevention of land alienation, and the protection of tribal rights; the PESA Act, 1996 extends Panchayati Raj to Fifth Schedule areas.`
            ],
            landmark: `Art. 244(1) and the Fifth Schedule; the PESA Act 1996 extends Part IX to Scheduled Areas.`,
            takeaway: `10 Fifth Schedule States; the Tribes Advisory Council has 20 members, three-fourths ST MLAs.`
          },
          {
            code: `15.3`,
            title: `Sixth Schedule: Autonomous District Councils`,
            articles: `Art. 244(2) & 275(1)`,
            summary: `The Sixth Schedule creates Autonomous District Councils in Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, and Mizoram to protect tribal land and custom.`,
            story: `In the rugged hills of the Northeast, tribal communities have fierce traditions of clan autonomy that rejected both British and colonial bureaucracy. The framers devised the Sixth Schedule under Articles 244(2) and 275(1) specifically for four border states—remembered by the popular acronym 'AMTM' (Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, and Mizoram). It creates Autonomous District Councils (ADCs) that function virtually as 'mini-states within a state'—wielding independent powers to make laws on land, forests, village inheritance, and customary marriages, collect local taxes, and run traditional courts without state legislative interference.`,
            storyLink: `Having mastered the administrative, judicial, and local machinery of the Republic, we enter Unit IV to explore the permanent institutional referees that guard democracy: Constitutional and Statutory Bodies.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Basis (Art. 244(2)):</strong> The Sixth Schedule governs tribal areas in four northeastern States — Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, and Mizoram; Manipur is not included (the ATM without Money mnemonic).`,
              `<strong>Autonomous District Councils:</strong> ADCs and Regional Councils exercise legislative, executive, and judicial powers over land, forests, and local affairs; an ADC has 30 members — 26 elected and 4 nominated by the Governor.`,
              `<strong>Law-Making &amp; Assent:</strong> ADC laws require the Governor's assent; parliamentary and State laws apply to Sixth Schedule areas only as directed by the Governor (or the President for certain Assam laws).`,
              `<strong>Financial Support:</strong> Art. 275(1) provides grants-in-aid to the Sixth Schedule States for tribal administration.`,
              `<strong>Comparison with Fifth Schedule:</strong> The Fifth Schedule uses a Tribes Advisory Council and Governor's regulations, while the Sixth Schedule creates elected Autonomous District Councils with substantial self-government.`
            ],
            landmark: `The Sixth Schedule covers Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, and Mizoram; ADCs have 26 elected and 4 nominated members.`,
            takeaway: `Four Sixth Schedule States; Manipur is excluded; ADCs enjoy legislative, executive, and judicial powers.`
          },
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 16,
        unitId: 4,
        title: `Constitutional Bodies`,
        articles: `Articles 148, 280, 315, 324, 338`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on constitutional bodies: Election Commission, Finance Commission, UPSC/SPSC, CAG, National Commissions for SCs/STs/BCs, and the GST Council.`,
        story: `Constitutional bodies then guard the system: elections, finance, recruitment, audit, social justice, and GST.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `16.1`,
            title: `Election Commission & Finance Commission`,
            articles: `Art. 324 & 280`,
            summary: `The Election Commission runs elections, while the Finance Commission distributes revenues between the Centre and states every five years.`,
            story: `In many fragile nations, ruling parties steal elections and hijack the national treasury; India's framers anticipated this danger and created independent constitutional referees. Under Article 324, the Election Commission of India (ECI) was placed outside executive control, equipped with plenary powers to conduct free and fair elections for Parliament, State Assemblies, and the President, with the Chief Election Commissioner shielded from removal like a Supreme Court judge. Alongside it, Article 280 established the Finance Commission, a quasi-judicial body appointed every five years by the President to act as a fair financial referee, deciding how much tax revenue the Centre must share with the States to prevent fiscal starvation.`,
            storyLink: `Just as elections and finances require impartial guardians, recruiting the nation's permanent administrative leadership demands a totally merit-based, non-partisan institution—the Public Service Commission.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Election Commission (Art. 324):</strong> Formed on 25 January 1950 (National Voters' Day); conducts elections to the President, Vice President, Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha, State Assemblies, and State Legislative Councils, but not Panchayat or Municipal elections.`,
              `<strong>Composition &amp; Tenure:</strong> CEC plus two Election Commissioners; multi-member since 1989 and permanent since October 1993; tenure six years or 65 years; salary equal to a Supreme Court judge.`,
              `<strong>Removal:</strong> The CEC is removed in the same manner and on the same grounds as a Supreme Court judge; other Election Commissioners are removed by the President on the CEC's recommendation.`,
              `<strong>Electoral Facts:</strong> First CEC Sukumar Sen; current CEC Gyanesh Kumar (26th); voting age reduced from 21 to 18 by the 61st CAA 1988; EPIC voter ID cards introduced in 1993.`,
              `<strong>Finance Commission (Art. 280):</strong> Quasi-judicial body constituted every five years; Chairman plus four members; called the balancing wheel of fiscal federalism; recommendations are advisory and non-binding.`,
              `<strong>Finance Commission Chronology:</strong> First FC (1951) K.C. Neogy; 14th FC Y.V. Reddy; 15th FC N.K. Singh; 16th FC Dr. Arvind Panagariya.`
            ],
            landmark: `61st CAA 1988 lowered the voting age to 18; the 16th Finance Commission is chaired by Dr. Arvind Panagariya.`,
            takeaway: `The ECI does not conduct local body elections; Finance Commission recommendations are advisory.`
          },
          {
            code: `16.2`,
            title: `Union & State Public Service Commissions`,
            articles: `Art. 315–323`,
            summary: `UPSC and State PSCs recruit civil servants; their members are appointed by the President or Governor and protected from arbitrary removal.`,
            story: `Under British rule, civil service appointments were plagued by racial nepotism; in independent India, Sardar Patel insisted that an honest, fearless 'Steel Frame' of civil servants was essential to hold the diverse nation together. Articles 315 to 323 establish the Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) and State PSCs as the supreme recruitment watchdogs. Appointed by the President (or Governor for SPSCs) for a strict term of 6 years or until 65 (62 for states), members are shielded from arbitrary dismissal: they can only be removed after an inquiry by the Supreme Court on grounds of misbehavior, and are strictly banned from taking future government jobs to prevent backroom bribes.`,
            storyLink: `While civil servants execute government policies, who watches where the thousands of crores of taxpayer money actually go? That brings us to the supreme auditor of the Republic—the CAG.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Basis (Art. 315–323):</strong> Public Service Commissions for the Union and States; UPSC is called the watchdog of the merit system.`,
              `<strong>Composition &amp; Tenure:</strong> Members appointed by the President; at least half must have 10 years of government experience; UPSC tenure six years or 65 years; SPSC/JPSC six years or 62 years.`,
              `<strong>Removal:</strong> By the President on grounds of insolvency, infirmity, or misbehaviour after a Supreme Court inquiry under Art. 317; the Court's advice is binding.`,
              `<strong>Joint Commission:</strong> A Joint State Public Service Commission is a statutory body created by Parliament, not a constitutional body.`,
              `<strong>All-India Services (Art. 312):</strong> IAS, IPS, and Indian Forest Service; a new All-India Service can be created only by a Rajya Sabha resolution passed by a two-thirds majority of members present and voting.`,
              `<strong>Post-Retirement Restrictions:</strong> Art. 319 imposes restrictions on the subsequent employment of PSC members; the rules differ for UPSC and SPSC members.`
            ],
            landmark: `Art. 317 requires a Supreme Court inquiry before removal; its advice is binding on the President.`,
            takeaway: `UPSC retirement is 65, SPSC 62; All-India Services need a Rajya Sabha two-thirds resolution.`
          },
          {
            code: `16.3`,
            title: `Comptroller and Auditor General of India (CAG)`,
            articles: `Art. 148–151`,
            summary: `The CAG audits all public accounts, reports to Parliament, and is called the guardian of the public purse.`,
            story: `Dr. B.R. Ambedkar declared that the Comptroller and Auditor General of India (Article 148) is arguably the single most important officer in the entire Constitution of India. Serving as the fearless 'Guardian of the Public Purse', the CAG audits every single rupee spent by both the Union and State governments, scrutinizing state-run corporations and government departments. The CAG is insulated like a Supreme Court judge—ineligible for further office and protected by an oath to act without fear or favor. Their scathing audit reports are submitted directly to the President and Governor, who lay them before Parliament to be dissected by the Public Accounts Committee.`,
            storyLink: `To ensure the Republic's commitment to social justice and economic harmony remains equally watchful, the Constitution established specialized commissions for marginalized classes and federal taxation.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Basis (Art. 148–151):</strong> CAG appointed by the President; tenure six years or 65 years; removed like a Supreme Court judge; ineligible for further office under the Union or a State.`,
              `<strong>Role:</strong> Guardian of the public purse; Dr. B.R. Ambedkar called the CAG arguably the most important officer under the Constitution.`,
              `<strong>Audit Function:</strong> Audits the accounts of the Union and States; submits three reports — appropriation, finance, and commercial — to the President; these are examined by the Public Accounts Committee.`,
              `<strong>Accounting Role:</strong> Compiles and maintains State government accounts; relieved of Union accounting in 1976.`,
              `<strong>Comparison with the UK:</strong> The Indian CAG performs audit only and does not control the issue of money from the Consolidated Fund, unlike the British Comptroller and Auditor-General.`,
              `<strong>Current Office:</strong> K. Sanjay Murthy is the current CAG.`
            ],
            landmark: `CAG reports are examined by the Public Accounts Committee; the CAG has no power to control withdrawals from the Consolidated Fund.`,
            takeaway: `CAG tenure is six years or 65; removal is like an SC judge; three audit reports go to the President.`
          },
          {
            code: `16.4`,
            title: `National Commissions & GST Council`,
            articles: `Art. 338, 338A, 338B, 350B & 279A`,
            summary: `National Commissions for SCs, STs, and BCs watchdog constitutional safeguards, while the GST Council harmonises indirect taxes.`,
            story: `To protect historically oppressed communities from exploitation and systemic bias, the Constitution established high-powered permanent watchdogs: Article 338 created the National Commission for Scheduled Castes (NCSC), while the 89th Amendment (2003) bifurcated it to birth the National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST, Art 338A), and the 102nd Amendment (2018) constitutionalized the National Commission for Backward Classes (NCBC, Art 338B). These commissions wield the full investigative powers of a civil court to summon witnesses and probe atrocities. Working in parallel on the economic frontier, Article 279A creates the GST Council, a joint constitutional forum uniting Union and State finance ministers to harmonize indirect taxes nationwide.`,
            storyLink: `While these bodies derive their direct authority from constitutional text, Parliament and the Cabinet created powerful statutory and executive agencies to tackle modern specialized challenges.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Evolution:</strong> Art. 338 originally provided for a Special Officer; the 65th CAA 1990 created a multi-member National Commission for SCs and STs; the 89th CAA 2003 bifurcated it into NCSC (Art. 338) and NCST (Art. 338A).`,
              `<strong>NCBC:</strong> The 102nd CAA 2018 gave constitutional status to the National Commission for Backward Classes (Art. 338B); it was a statutory body created in 1993 after Indra Sawhney.`,
              `<strong>Composition &amp; Powers:</strong> Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and three members appointed by the President for three years; the commissions have the powers of a civil court while investigating complaints.`,
              `<strong>Linguistic Minorities:</strong> The Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities (Art. 350B) was added by the 7th CAA 1956.`,
              `<strong>GST Council (Art. 279A):</strong> Inserted by the 101st CAA 2016; chaired by the Union Finance Minister; members include the Union Minister of State for Finance and State Finance/Taxation Ministers; decisions require a three-fourths weighted majority, with the Centre holding one-third and States two-thirds.`
            ],
            landmark: `89th CAA 2003 created NCSC and NCST; 102nd CAA 2018 gave NCBC constitutional status; 101st CAA 2016 created the GST Council.`,
            takeaway: `National commissions have three-year terms and civil court powers; GST Council decisions need a three-fourths weighted majority.`
          },
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 17,
        unitId: 4,
        title: `Non-Constitutional & Statutory Bodies`,
        articles: `Statutory & Executive Acts`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on statutory and executive bodies: NITI Aayog, NHRC, CIC, CVC, Lokpal and Lokayuktas, CBI, Law Commission, NCW, UGC, and NIA.`,
        story: `Around them stand statutory and executive bodies, from NITI Aayog to NHRC, Lokpal, CBI, and the Law Commission.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `17.1`,
            title: `NITI Aayog & National Development Council`,
            articles: `Executive Resolutions`,
            summary: `NITI Aayog and the National Development Council are extra-constitutional think tanks that advise but do not allocate funds.`,
            story: `For over six decades, India's economy was steered by the Soviet-style Planning Commission (established in 1950 by executive resolution), which allocated central funds through centralized Five-Year Plans—a rigid 'one-size-fits-all' model that treated diverse states like subordinate departments. On 1 January 2015, the Union Government dissolved the Planning Commission and replaced it with NITI Aayog (National Institution for Transforming India). Functioning strictly as a non-statutory, visionary think tank, NITI Aayog fosters 'Competitive and Cooperative Federalism' by seating all Chief Ministers on its Governing Council, promoting cooperative policy innovation rather than dispensing government funds.`,
            storyLink: `While economic think tanks guide macroeconomic growth, who steps in to protect citizens against police atrocities, custodial deaths, and bureaucratic secrecy?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>NITI Aayog:</strong> Established on 1 January 2015, replacing the Planning Commission (set up on 15 March 1950); a non-statutory, extra-constitutional think tank based on a bottom-up approach and cooperative federalism.`,
              `<strong>NITI Structure:</strong> Prime Minister is Chairperson; Vice-Chairperson Suman K. Bery; CEO B.V.R. Subrahmanyam; Governing Council includes all Chief Ministers and Lieutenant Governors; publishes the SDG India Index, Multidimensional Poverty Index, and India Innovation Index.`,
              `<strong>National Development Council:</strong> An extra-constitutional advisory body chaired by the Prime Minister; like NITI Aayog, it is neither constitutional nor statutory.`,
              `<strong>Comparison:</strong> NITI Aayog is an advisory think tank and does not allocate funds like the erstwhile Planning Commission.`
            ],
            landmark: `NITI Aayog was established on 1 January 2015; neither NITI Aayog nor the National Development Council is a constitutional or statutory body.`,
            takeaway: `NITI Aayog is extra-constitutional and advisory; the Prime Minister chairs both NITI Aayog and the National Development Council.`
          },
          {
            code: `17.2`,
            title: `NHRC, Central Information Commission & CVC`,
            articles: `Acts of 1993, 2003, 2005`,
            summary: `NHRC, CIC, and CVC are statutory watchdogs for human rights, information, and corruption, with different selection committees and tenures.`,
            story: `In the 1990s and 2000s, India modernized its governance by enacting specialized statutory watchdogs to defend fundamental human dignity and transparency. The Protection of Human Rights Act 1993 created the National Human Rights Commission (NHRC), headed by a former Chief Justice or SC judge, investigating custodial violence and abuses with civil court powers. In 2005, the revolutionary Right to Information (RTI) Act established the Central Information Commission (CIC), turning secrecy on its head by empowering ordinary citizens to question official files. To root out corruption in central government offices, the Central Vigilance Commission (CVC) was granted statutory teeth in 2003, acting as an apex integrity monitor.`,
            storyLink: `When corruption reaches senior politicians, high ministers, or involves complex financial crimes, what specialized anti-graft agencies take the field?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>NHRC:</strong> Established under the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993; Chairperson is a retired CJI or Supreme Court judge, plus five full-time members; the selection committee includes the PM, Lok Sabha Speaker, Rajya Sabha Deputy Chairman, Home Minister, and Leaders of Opposition in both Houses.`,
              `<strong>NHRC Tenure &amp; Limits:</strong> Three years or 70 years (2019 amendment); recommendations are advisory and non-binding; it cannot inquire into matters older than one year; current Chairperson Justice V. Ramasubramanian.`,
              `<strong>Central Information Commission:</strong> Established in 2005 under the Right to Information Act, 2005; the selection committee includes the PM, Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha, and a Union Cabinet Minister; tenure three years or 65 years (2019 amendment).`,
              `<strong>Central Vigilance Commission:</strong> Set up in 1964 by executive resolution on the Santhanam Committee's recommendation; statutory status conferred by the CVC Act, 2003; selection committee includes the PM, Union Home Minister, and Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha; tenure four years or 65 years.`,
              `<strong>Comparison:</strong> NHRC and CIC have three-year tenures, while CVC has a four-year tenure; all three are statutory bodies.`
            ],
            landmark: `NHRC Act 1993; CVC Act 2003 gave statutory status to the CVC; RTI Act 2005 created the CIC.`,
            takeaway: `NHRC and CIC are 3 years/upper age limits; CVC is 4 years/65; NHRC recommendations are advisory.`
          },
          {
            code: `17.3`,
            title: `Ombudsman (Lokpal & Lokayuktas) & CBI`,
            articles: `Act of 2013 & DSPE 1946`,
            summary: `Lokpal and Lokayuktas are statutory ombudsmen, while the CBI is an executive agency deriving power from the DSPE Act.`,
            story: `Following decades of civil society movements and the historic Anna Hazare anti-corruption crusade of 2011, Parliament enacted the Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act 2013, establishing a statutory national ombudsman with the power to investigate corruption allegations against public servants—including Union Ministers and even the Prime Minister (with specific national security safeguards). Alongside this statutory referee operates the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI), India's premier criminal and anti-corruption investigative agency. Though widely feared, the CBI is not a statutory body created by a dedicated Act; it derives its police search and arrest powers from the Delhi Special Police Establishment (DSPE) Act of 1946.`,
            storyLink: `Beyond anti-corruption and human rights, statutory and advisory bodies protect women, higher education standards, and national security against terrorism.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Lokpal:</strong> The Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013; anti-corruption ombudsman concept from Sweden; the term was coined by L.M. Singhvi in 1963.`,
              `<strong>Composition &amp; Jurisdiction:</strong> Chairperson is a retired CJI/Supreme Court judge or eminent person, plus up to eight members (50% judicial, 50% from SC/ST/OBC/minority/women); tenure five years or 70 years; covers the PM with safeguards, Ministers, MPs, and Group A–D officials.`,
              `<strong>Lokpal Firsts:</strong> First Lokpal Pinaki Chandra Ghose; current Lokpal Ajay Manikrao Khanwilkar; Maharashtra was the first State to establish a Lokayukta in 1971, while Odisha passed the first Act in 1970.`,
              `<strong>CBI:</strong> Established in 1963 by a Ministry of Home Affairs resolution on the Santhanam Committee's recommendation; derives investigation powers from the Delhi Special Police Establishment Act, 1946; it is not a constitutional body or a statutory body under its own Act.`
            ],
            landmark: `Lokpal Act 2013; the CBI derives its power from the DSPE Act 1946; Maharashtra created the first Lokayukta in 1971.`,
            takeaway: `Lokpal is 5 years/70; the CBI is neither constitutional nor a standalone statutory body.`
          },
          {
            code: `17.4`,
            title: `NCW, UGC, NIA & Law Commission`,
            articles: `Statutory & Executive Bodies`,
            summary: `NCW, UGC, NIA, and the Law Commission show the many forms of statutory and executive bodies outside the Constitution.`,
            story: `Modern governance requires targeted statutory shields: the National Commission for Women (NCW, 1992) serves as a statutory watchdog reviewing gender laws and investigating dowry and harassment complaints; the University Grants Commission (UGC, 1956) coordinates higher education standards and university funding; and following the horrific 26/11 Mumbai terror attacks, Parliament created the National Investigation Agency (NIA, 2008) as a specialized federal anti-terror agency with nationwide jurisdiction across state borders. Meanwhile, the Law Commission of India operates as an executive advisory body reconstituted every three years to recommend progressive legal reforms and repeal obsolete colonial statutes.`,
            storyLink: `No matter how well institutions are constructed, an evolving society requires the Constitution itself to grow and change—opening the door to Article 368 and the Amendment process.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>National Commission for Women (NCW):</strong> Established on 31 January 1992 under the NCW Act, 1990; it advises on women's rights and reviews related laws; the current Chairperson is Vijaya Kishore Rahatkar.`,
              `<strong>University Grants Commission (UGC):</strong> Established in 1956 under the Ministry of Education; headquartered in New Delhi with six regional centres; it coordinates and maintains standards in higher education.`,
              `<strong>National Investigation Agency (NIA):</strong> Established in 2008 after the Mumbai 26/11 attacks under the NIA Act, 2008; it functions under the Ministry of Home Affairs and investigates terrorism, counterfeit currency, and cyber-terrorism.`,
              `<strong>Law Commission of India:</strong> An executive advisory body for legal reform; the first pre-independence Commission was set up in 1834 under Lord Macaulay, and the first post-independence Commission in 1955 under M.C. Setalvad.`
            ],
            landmark: `NCW 1992; UGC 1956; NIA 2008; Law Commission 1834 (pre-independence) and 1955 (post-independence).`,
            takeaway: `NCW, UGC, and NIA are statutory; the Law Commission is an executive body.`
          }        ]

      },
      {
        id: 18,
        unitId: 4,
        title: `Constitutional Amendments & Basic Structure`,
        articles: `Article 368 (Part XX)`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on Article 368 amendment procedures, the evolution of the Basic Structure doctrine, and the landmark amendments from the 1st to the 106th.`,
        story: `The Constitution stays alive through amendments, while the Basic Structure doctrine draws the line it cannot cross.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `18.1`,
            title: `Amendment Procedures Under Article 368`,
            articles: `Art. 368`,
            summary: `Article 368 provides three amendment routes: simple majority, special majority, and special majority plus state ratification.`,
            story: `The framers recognized that a rigid constitution that cannot bend will inevitably break during national revolutions. Under Article 368 in Part XX, they forged a flexible yet robust amendment procedure: amendments can be initiated only in Parliament, not in state assemblies. Most provisions require a Special Majority (a majority of total membership plus two-thirds of members present and voting). However, if an amendment touches federal architecture—such as the election of the President, the Supreme Court, or legislative lists—it requires a Double Lock: a Special Majority in Parliament plus formal ratification by at least half of the State Legislatures by simple majority.`,
            storyLink: `But if Parliament possesses this awesome power to amend, could an authoritarian parliamentary majority rewrite the Constitution to declare India a dictatorship?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Source &amp; Character:</strong> The amendment procedure is borrowed from South Africa; K.C. Wheare called India's variety of amending processes wise but rarely found; Granville Austin said it strikes a balance between rigidity and flexibility.`,
              `<strong>Simple Majority (Outside Art. 368):</strong> Creation or reorganisation of States (Arts. 2–4), abolition or creation of Legislative Councils (Art. 169), Second Schedule emoluments, quorum, delimitation, citizenship, official language, and Fifth/Sixth Schedule matters.`,
              `<strong>Special Majority (Under Art. 368):</strong> Majority of the total membership of each House plus two-thirds of members present and voting; covers Fundamental Rights, DPSPs, and Fundamental Duties.`,
              `<strong>Special Majority + State Ratification:</strong> At least half the State legislatures must ratify amendments on federal provisions such as the election of the President, executive powers, Supreme Court and High Courts, the Seventh Schedule, State representation in Parliament, and Art. 368 itself.`,
              `<strong>Procedural Rules:</strong> A Bill can originate in either House; no prior recommendation of the President is needed; there is no joint sitting; the President must give assent after the 24th CAA 1971.`,
              `<strong>Basic Structure:</strong> Established in Kesavananda Bharati (1973); core elements include the supremacy of the Constitution, judicial review, separation of powers, federalism, secularism, and democracy.`
            ],
            landmark: `Kesavananda Bharati (1973) established the Basic Structure doctrine; the 24th CAA 1971 made Presidential assent mandatory.`,
            takeaway: `State legislatures cannot initiate constitutional amendments; there is no joint sitting; there are three amendment tracks under Art. 368.`
          },
          {
            code: `18.2`,
            title: `Evolution of the Basic Structure Doctrine`,
            articles: `1951–2007 Jurisprudence`,
            summary: `The Basic Structure doctrine grew from Shankari Prasad and Sajjan Singh through Golaknath and Kesavananda to Minerva Mills and I.R. Coelho.`,
            story: `In the 1960s and 70s, a titanic constitutional war exploded between Parliament and the Judiciary: in Shankari Prasad (1951), the Court held Parliament could amend any part of the Constitution, but in Golaknath (1967), an 11-judge bench dramatically reversed course, declaring Fundamental Rights could never be diluted. An enraged Parliament passed the 24th Amendment to assert unlimited amending power. The climax arrived on 24 April 1973 in Kesavananda Bharati: a record 13-judge bench ruled 7–6 that while Parliament can amend any article under 368, it cannot alter or destroy the 'Basic Structure' of the Constitution—such as democracy, secularism, the rule of law, and judicial review—saving the republic's soul forever.`,
            storyLink: `Armed with this protective judicial boundary, how has the Indian Constitution evolved through its historic diary of landmark amendments from 1951 to the present?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Early Cases:</strong> Shankari Prasad (1951) and Sajjan Singh (1965) upheld the power of Parliament to amend Fundamental Rights; Golaknath (1967) held that Fundamental Rights cannot be taken away or abridged.`,
              `<strong>24th CAA 1971:</strong> Inserted Art. 13(4) and Art. 368(3), asserting Parliament's constituent power to amend any part of the Constitution.`,
              `<strong>Kesavananda Bharati (1973):</strong> A 13-judge bench, by 7:6, upheld the 24th CAA but held that Parliament cannot destroy the Basic Structure; this remains the foundation of constitutional limits on amendments.`,
              `<strong>Post-Kesavananda:</strong> Indira Nehru Gandhi (1975) struck down the 39th CAA's attempt to immunize the PM's election; Minerva Mills (1980) protected judicial review and the balance between FRs and DPSPs; Waman Rao (1981) applied the doctrine to post-1973 amendments.`,
              `<strong>Ninth Schedule Review:</strong> I.R. Coelho (2007) held that laws added to the Ninth Schedule after 24 April 1973 are subject to Basic Structure review.`,
              `<strong>Undefined Doctrine:</strong> The Constitution does not define Basic Structure; the courts identify its elements case by case.`
            ],
            landmark: `Kesavananda Bharati (1973); I.R. Coelho (2007) brought post-1973 Ninth Schedule laws under Basic Structure review.`,
            takeaway: `The sequence is Golaknath (1967), 24th CAA (1971), Kesavananda (1973), Minerva Mills (1980), and I.R. Coelho (2007).`
          },
          {
            code: `18.3`,
            title: `Landmark Amendments Checklist (1st to 106th)`,
            articles: `1951–2023`,
            summary: `From the 1st Amendment's land reforms to the 106th's women's reservation, the amendment checklist is India's constitutional diary.`,
            story: `The 106 constitutional amendments enacted since 1950 read like a living diary of India's social and political evolution: the 1st Amendment (1951) created the 9th Schedule to protect land reforms; the 7th (1956) reorganized states on linguistic lines; the 42nd (1976)—the infamous 'Mini-Constitution'—added Socialist, Secular, Integrity, and Fundamental Duties; while the 44th (1978) restored democratic safeguards and defanged emergency powers. Modern milestones include the 86th (2002) for Right to Education, the 101st (2016) for GST, the 103rd (2019) for 10% EWS quota, and the historic 106th Amendment (2023)—the Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam—reserving one-third of Lok Sabha and Assembly seats for women.`,
            storyLink: `To complete our constitutional journey, we examine the final specialized operational rules governing official languages, anti-defection safeguards, and the historic criminal law overhaul of 2023.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Early Amendments:</strong> 1st CAA 1951 added the Ninth Schedule; 7th CAA 1956 reorganised States; 24th/25th CAA 1971 addressed amendment power and Art. 31C.`,
              `<strong>42nd CAA 1976:</strong> Called the Mini-Constitution; added Socialist, Secular, and Integrity to the Preamble, transferred subjects to the Concurrent List, and expanded DPSPs.`,
              `<strong>44th CAA 1978:</strong> Removed the right to property as a Fundamental Right, restored the five-year Lok Sabha term, and introduced safeguards against emergencies.`,
              `<strong>Democratic &amp; Social Amendments:</strong> 52nd CAA 1985 (anti-defection), 61st CAA 1988 (voting age 18), 69th CAA 1991 (Delhi), 73rd/74th CAA 1992 (local government), 86th CAA 2002 (right to education), 91st CAA 2003 (15% ministry cap), and 97th CAA 2011 (co-operatives).`,
              `<strong>Recent Amendments:</strong> 99th CAA 2014 (NJAC, struck down), 100th CAA 2015 (Bangladesh land boundary), 101st CAA 2016 (GST), 102nd CAA 2018 (NCBC), 103rd CAA 2019 (EWS), 104th CAA 2019 (SC/ST extension, end of Anglo-Indian nomination), 105th CAA 2021 (State OBC lists), and 106th CAA 2023 (women's reservation).`,
              `<strong>106th CAA 2023:</strong> The Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam reserves one-third of seats in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies for women.`
            ],
            landmark: `42nd CAA 1976 expanded central power; 44th CAA 1978 rolled back emergency and property provisions; 106th CAA 2023 introduced women's reservation.`,
            takeaway: `Distinguish the 42nd Amendment's expansions from the 44th Amendment's rollbacks; the 106th CAA is the women's reservation amendment.`
          },
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 19,
        unitId: 4,
        title: `Official Language, Anti-Defection & Special Provisions`,
        articles: `Articles 343–351, 371 & Tenth Schedule`,
        summary: `Complete study notes on Part XVII official language, the Tenth Schedule anti-defection law, special provisions under Articles 371–371J, the 2023 criminal-law overhaul, and high-yield constitutional trivia.`,
        story: `The story closes with language, anti-defection, special state provisions, new criminal laws, and the trivia that ties it all together.`,
        subtopics: [
          {
            code: `19.1`,
            title: `Official Language & Classical Languages`,
            articles: `Art. 343–351`,
            summary: `Hindi is the official language, not the national language; English continues, 22 languages are scheduled, and 11 are classical.`,
            story: `India's linguistic diversity is one of the most vibrant in human history, requiring the Constitution to carefully balance national unity with regional pride. Under Part XVII (Articles 343–351), Hindi in Devanagari script is the Official Language of the Union, with English continuing as an associate language for official communication and higher court proceedings. The 8th Schedule protects 22 recognized languages, while the central government has bestowed the prestigious status of 'Classical Language' upon 11 ancient literary languages (including Tamil, Sanskrit, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam, Odia, Marathi, Bengali, Assamese, Pali, and Prakrit), honoring their extraordinary antiquity and independent heritage.`,
            storyLink: `While language unites cultures, what constitutional mechanism stops corrupt politicians from auctioning their loyalty to the highest bidder after winning elections?`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Official Language (Art. 343):</strong> Hindi in Devanagari script is the official language of the Union; English was permitted for 15 years and continued indefinitely by the Official Languages Act, 1963; the first Official Language Commission was headed by B.G. Kher (1955).`,
              `<strong>Regional &amp; Judicial Languages (Art. 345–349):</strong> States may adopt any language or Hindi; Art. 347 empowers the President to recognize a minority language; authoritative texts of Supreme Court/High Court proceedings and Bills/Acts are in English.`,
              `<strong>Special Directives (Art. 350A–351):</strong> Art. 350A provides for primary education in the mother tongue (7th CAA 1956); Art. 350B creates the Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities; Art. 351 directs the development and spread of Hindi.`,
              `<strong>Eighth Schedule:</strong> Originally 14 languages, presently 22; additions were Sindhi (21st CAA 1967), Konkani/Manipuri/Nepali (71st CAA 1992), Bodo/Dogri/Maithili/Santhali (92nd CAA 2003), and Odia renaming (96th CAA 2011).`,
              `<strong>Classical Languages:</strong> Eleven languages are recognized — the original six are Tamil (2004), Sanskrit (2005), Telugu (2008), Kannada (2008), Malayalam (2013), and Odia (2014); five were added in October 2024 — Marathi, Pali, Prakrit, Assamese, and Bengali.`,
              `<strong>No National Language:</strong> The Constitution declares Hindi the official language of the Union, not a national language.`
            ],
            landmark: `Official Languages Act 1963 continued English; the 7th CAA 1956 added Arts. 350A and 350B.`,
            takeaway: `22 scheduled languages; 11 classical languages; Hindi is the official language, not the national language.`
          },
          {
            code: `19.2`,
            title: `Anti-Defection Law & 91st Amendment`,
            articles: `Tenth Schedule`,
            summary: `The Tenth Schedule disqualifies defectors; the 91st Amendment ended the one-third split and retained only the two-thirds merger exception.`,
            story: `In the late 1960s, Indian democracy was mocked by the infamous political slogan 'Aaya Ram, Gaya Ram', as opportunistic legislators switched parties multiple times in a single day to grab cabinet posts. To cure this democratic rot, the 52nd Amendment in 1985 added the Tenth Schedule, disqualifying any MP or MLA who voluntarily gives up party membership or defies the party whip during floor votes, with the Speaker acting as the tribunal (subject to judicial review under Kihoto Hollohan 1992). When politicians began engineering mass 'splits' of one-third to escape disqualification, the 91st Amendment in 2003 closed the loophole—deleting split exemptions entirely and allowing only full party mergers of at least two-thirds members.`,
            storyLink: `While anti-defection protects legislative stability, the Constitution maintains tailored administrative exceptions for historically fragile and border states under Articles 371 to 371J.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Constitutional Basis:</strong> The Tenth Schedule was added by the 52nd CAA 1985; it disqualifies members who voluntarily give up party membership or vote/abstain against the party whip.`,
              `<strong>Grounds:</strong> A nominated member who joins a party after six months, and an independent member who joins a party, are also disqualified.`,
              `<strong>Merger Exception:</strong> The 91st CAA 2003 deleted the one-third split exemption and retained only the two-thirds merger exemption; a defector is also barred from becoming a minister.`,
              `<strong>Decision-Making:</strong> The Presiding Officer decides disqualification petitions; the decision is subject to judicial review under Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992).`,
              `<strong>No Time Limit:</strong> The Constitution prescribes no fixed time limit for the Speaker or Chairman to decide a defection petition.`,
              `<strong>Related Reform:</strong> The 91st CAA 2003 also capped the Council of Ministers at 15% of the Lok Sabha strength.`
            ],
            landmark: `Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992) subjected the Speaker's anti-defection decision to judicial review.`,
            takeaway: `Only the two-thirds merger exception remains; there is no fixed timeline for the Presiding Officer.`
          },
          {
            code: `19.3`,
            title: `Special Provisions for States (Art. 371–371J)`,
            articles: `Art. 371–371J`,
            summary: `Articles 371 to 371J are state-specific exceptions protecting land, custom, and regional development.`,
            story: `India's federalism is built on the pragmatic wisdom that uniform rules cannot be blindly forced upon regions with unique historical wounds, tribal treaties, or economic disparities. Part XXI provides customized constitutional umbrellas: Article 371A shields Nagaland's customary land rights; 371B and 371C protect Assam and Manipur's tribal and hill areas; 371F preserves Sikkim's pre-merger laws; and 371G shields Mizoram. Articles 371D and 371J empower the President to create specialized developmental boards and public employment quotas for neglected regions in Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, and Karnataka, ensuring no community is left behind in national progress.`,
            storyLink: `Just as the Constitution adapts its federal provisions, India undertook a revolutionary modernization of its core criminal justice system in 2023.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Special Provisions (Art. 371–371J):</strong> Art. 371 Maharashtra/Gujarat; 371A Nagaland (13th CAA 1962); 371B Assam; 371C Manipur; 371D/371E Andhra Pradesh/Telangana; 371F Sikkim; 371G Mizoram; 371H Arunachal Pradesh; 371-I Goa; 371J Karnataka's Hyderabad-Karnataka region (98th CAA 2012).`,
              `<strong>Purpose &amp; Scope:</strong> These provisions preserve local customs, land, and resource rights and provide regional development or administrative safeguards; they are exceptions to the uniform application of Union and State laws.`,
              `<strong>Key Safeguards:</strong> Article 371A protects Naga customary law and land; Article 371F contains Sikkim's accession safeguards; Article 371J provides special development measures for the Hyderabad-Karnataka region.`
            ],
            landmark: `Article 371A (Nagaland) came via the 13th CAA 1962; Article 371J (Karnataka) came via the 98th CAA 2012; Article 371F contains Sikkim's safeguards.`,
            takeaway: `Special provisions are State-specific exceptions; do not confuse them with the Sixth Schedule's autonomous district councils.`
          },
          {
            code: `19.4`,
            title: `New Criminal Laws 2023`,
            articles: `BNS · BNSS · BSA`,
            summary: `The BNS, BNSS, and BSA replaced the IPC, CrPC, and Evidence Act on 1 July 2024, adding digital evidence and faster procedures.`,
            story: `For over 160 years, India's criminal justice system remained shackled to Victorian colonial statutes designed by Lord Macaulay in 1860 to subjugate Indian subjects. On 1 July 2024, India completed a historic legal decolonization: the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) replaced the Indian Penal Code (IPC); the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS) replaced the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC); and the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA) replaced the Indian Evidence Act. These revolutionary codes modernized Indian law: introducing community service for minor crimes, mandating videography during police searches, incorporating electronic and digital evidence, adding zero-FIRs, and repealing the archaic colonial offense of sedition.`,
            storyLink: `With modern criminal statutes in place, we conclude with a high-yield treasury of noteworthy constitutional trivia, historical firsts, and essential exam anchors.`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Legislative Transition:</strong> The Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) replaces the Indian Penal Code 1860 (358 sections); the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS) replaces the Code of Criminal Procedure 1973 (531 sections); the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA) replaces the Indian Evidence Act 1872 (170 sections); all came into force on 1 July 2024.`,
              `<strong>BNS Innovations:</strong> Sedition under Section 124A IPC was repealed and replaced by Section 152 (acts endangering sovereignty, unity, and integrity); adultery and consensual Section 377 offences were omitted; new offences cover mob lynching, organised crime, terrorism, deceitful sexual intercourse, and hit-and-run; community service was introduced as a statutory punishment.`,
              `<strong>BSA Innovations:</strong> Electronic and digital records get legal parity with primary documentary evidence; secondary evidence includes oral admissions and certified machine copies; police confessions remain inadmissible, while voluntary confessions before a Magistrate are admissible.`,
              `<strong>BNSS Innovations:</strong> Zero FIR and e-FIR, compulsory videography of searches and seizures, forensic investigation for offences punishable by seven years or more, trial in absentia, strict timelines for charges and judgments, and flexible police custody.`
            ],
            landmark: `The BNS, BNSS, and BSA replaced the IPC, CrPC, and Evidence Act on 1 July 2024.`,
            takeaway: `Remember the trio: BNS = offences, BNSS = procedure, BSA = evidence.`
          },
          {
            code: `19.5`,
            title: `Noteworthy Constitutional & Legal Facts`,
            articles: `High-Yield Trivia`,
            summary: `The final trivia layer covers firsts, institutions, pay commissions, Cabinet exceptions, and Article 341's SC notification power.`,
            story: `Master the hidden gems and trivia that examiners love to test: Prem Behari Narain Raizada was the master calligrapher who penned the original Constitution by hand without charging a single rupee except writing his name alongside his grandfather's on each page; while Nand Lal Bose and his Shantiniketan team illuminated the borders with motifs of India's civilizational journey from Mohenjo-Daro to Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose. The word 'Cabinet' appears only once in the entire Constitution under Article 352 (added by 44th CAA), while the power to notify Scheduled Castes and Tribes rests exclusively with the President under Articles 341 and 342.`,
            storyLink: `From the first 1773 charter to the 2023 criminal codes, you have now completed the entire living, breathing narrative of Indian Polity—turning hundreds of articles into one unforgettable mental story!`,
            points: [
              `<strong>Historical Firsts:</strong> The Age of Consent Act 1891 raised the marital age for girls from 10 to 12; Manipur held independent India's first universal adult franchise election in June 1948; Sikkim has the smallest Assembly with 32 members; the 40th CAA 1976 amended Article 297 to bring maritime zones under the Union; the 106th CAA 2023 introduced 33% women's reservation in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies.`,
              `<strong>Legal Theory &amp; Institutions:</strong> Legal positivism was formulated by John Austin; the National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission functions under the Consumer Protection Act 2019; the LLP Amendment Act 2021 decriminalised minor procedural offences.`,
              `<strong>Cabinet Committees:</strong> Almost all Cabinet Committees are chaired by the Prime Minister, except the Cabinet Committee on Parliamentary Affairs, which is headed by the Minister of Defence.`,
              `<strong>Pay Commissions:</strong> The 1st Central Pay Commission was constituted in January 1946 under Srinivasa Varadachariar; the 2nd was appointed in August 1957 under Jagannath Das.`,
              `<strong>Article 341(1):</strong> The President, after consulting the Governor, may notify the castes, races, or tribes deemed to be Scheduled Castes in a State or Union Territory; Article 371A for Nagaland was inserted by the 13th CAA 1962.`
            ],
            landmark: `Manipur's 1948 election, Sikkim's 32-member Assembly, and the 40th CAA's maritime-zones amendment are recurring trivia questions.`,
            takeaway: `Keep the exceptions separate: Parliamentary Affairs is the Cabinet Committee not chaired by the PM, and Article 341(1) is the SC notification power.`
          }        ]

      }
    ];

    const MY_NOTES_STORAGE_KEY = `indianPolityMyNotes`;

    let appState = {
      completedIds: BASELINE_COMPLETED.slice(),
      expandedChapters: [1],
      expandedSubtopics: [`1-0`],
      unitFilter: `all`,
      statusFilter: `all`,
      searchQuery: ``,
      myNotes: loadMyNotesFromStorage()
    };

    function loadMyNotesFromStorage() {
      try {
        const raw = window.localStorage.getItem(MY_NOTES_STORAGE_KEY);
        const parsed = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
        if (parsed && typeof parsed === `object`) return parsed;
      } catch (err) {
        // Storage unavailable: notes still work for this session
      }
      return {};
    }

    function saveMyNotesToStorage() {
      try {
        window.localStorage.setItem(MY_NOTES_STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(appState.myNotes));
      } catch (err) {
        // Storage unavailable: notes still work for this session
      }
    }

    function escapeHtml(text) {
      return String(text)
        .replace(/&/g, `&amp;`)
        .replace(/</g, `&lt;`)
        .replace(/>/g, `&gt;`)
        .replace(/"/g, `&quot;`)
        .replace(/'/g, `&#39;`);
    }

    function padTwo(num) {
      return num < 10 ? `0${num}` : `${num}`;
    }

    function myNoteMarkup(noteKey) {
      const noteHtml = appState.myNotes[noteKey] || ``;
      const filled = noteHtml.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, ``).trim() ? ` my-note-wrap--filled` : ``;
      return `<div class="my-note-wrap${filled}" data-note-wrap="${escapeHtml(noteKey)}">
                        <button type="button" class="my-note-trigger" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" title="Hover to preview · click to pin" onclick="toggleMyNote(this)">My Note</button>
                        <div class="my-note-popup" role="dialog" aria-label="My Note editor">
                          <div class="my-note-popup-head">
                            <span>My Note · paste keeps source format</span>
                            <button type="button" class="my-note-copy" onclick="copyMyNote(this)">Copy</button>
                          </div>
                          <div class="my-note-editor" contenteditable="true" role="textbox" aria-multiline="true" data-note-key="${escapeHtml(noteKey)}" data-placeholder="Type or paste your note here…">${noteHtml}</div>
                        </div>
                      </div>`;
    }

    function getTotalSubtopics() {
      let total = 0;
      let mastered = 0;
      for (let i = 0; i < CHAPTERS.length; i++) {
        const ch = CHAPTERS[i];
        const count = ch.subtopics.length;
        total += count;
        if (appState.completedIds.indexOf(ch.id) !== -1) {
          mastered += count;
        }
      }
      return { total: total, mastered: mastered, pending: total - mastered };
    }

    async function persistState() {
      try {
        if (window.notebookAppApi && typeof window.notebookAppApi.setAppState === `function`) {
          await window.notebookAppApi.setAppState({
            completedIds: appState.completedIds,
            expandedChapters: appState.expandedChapters,
            expandedSubtopics: appState.expandedSubtopics,
            unitFilter: appState.unitFilter,
            statusFilter: appState.statusFilter,
            searchQuery: appState.searchQuery,
            myNotes: appState.myNotes
          });
        }
      } catch (err) {
        // Non-fatal in preview mode
      }
    }

    function setFeedback(message) {
      const el = document.getElementById(`action-feedback`);
      if (el) el.textContent = message;
    }

    function renderKpis() {
      const totalChapters = CHAPTERS.length;
      const completedCount = appState.completedIds.length;
      const pendingCount = totalChapters - completedCount;
      const pct = Math.round((completedCount / totalChapters) * 100);
      const subCounts = getTotalSubtopics();

      const pctEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-progress-pct`);
      const barEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-progress-bar`);
      const detailEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-progress-detail`);
      const compEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-completed-count`);
      const compRangeEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-completed-range`);
      const pendEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-pending-count`);
      const pendRangeEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-pending-range`);
      const subEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-subtopics-count`);
      const subDetailEl = document.getElementById(`kpi-subtopics-detail`);
      const syncEl = document.getElementById(`sync-status-label`);

      if (pctEl) pctEl.textContent = `${pct}%`;
      if (barEl) barEl.style.width = `${pct}%`;
      if (detailEl) detailEl.textContent = `${completedCount} of ${totalChapters} chapters completed`;
      if (compEl) compEl.textContent = `${completedCount}`;
      if (compRangeEl) {
        compRangeEl.textContent = completedCount === 11 && isBaselineState()
          ? `Chapters 01–11 ready for revision`
          : `${completedCount} chapters marked mastered`;
      }
      if (pendEl) pendEl.textContent = `${pendingCount}`;
      if (pendRangeEl) {
        pendRangeEl.textContent = completedCount === 11 && isBaselineState()
          ? `Chapters 12–19 scheduled`
          : `${pendingCount} chapters remaining`;
      }
      if (subEl) subEl.textContent = `${subCounts.total}`;
      if (subDetailEl) subDetailEl.textContent = `${subCounts.mastered} mastered · ${subCounts.pending} upcoming`;
      if (syncEl) syncEl.textContent = `Progress: ${completedCount}/19 Chapters (${pct}%)`;

      const allBtn = document.getElementById(`filter-status-all`);
      const compBtn = document.getElementById(`filter-status-completed`);
      const pendBtn = document.getElementById(`filter-status-pending`);
      if (allBtn) allBtn.textContent = `All (${totalChapters})`;
      if (compBtn) compBtn.textContent = `Completed (${completedCount})`;
      if (pendBtn) pendBtn.textContent = `Pending (${pendingCount})`;
    }

    function isBaselineState() {
      if (appState.completedIds.length !== BASELINE_COMPLETED.length) return false;
      for (let i = 0; i < BASELINE_COMPLETED.length; i++) {
        if (appState.completedIds.indexOf(BASELINE_COMPLETED[i]) === -1) return false;
      }
      return true;
    }

    function getUnitStats(unitId) {
      const unitChapters = CHAPTERS.filter(function (c) { return c.unitId === unitId; });
      const done = unitChapters.filter(function (c) { return appState.completedIds.indexOf(c.id) !== -1; }).length;
      const total = unitChapters.length;
      const pct = total > 0 ? Math.round((done / total) * 100) : 0;
      return { done: done, total: total, pending: total - done, pct: pct };
    }

    function renderUnitBreakdown() {
      const container = document.getElementById(`unit-breakdown-list`);
      if (!container) return;

      let html = ``;
      for (let i = 0; i < UNITS.length; i++) {
        const u = UNITS[i];
        const stats = getUnitStats(u.id);
        const isActive = appState.unitFilter === String(u.id);
        html += `
          <button type="button" class="unit-row-btn" aria-pressed="${isActive ? `true` : `false`}" onclick="toggleUnitRowFilter('${u.id}')">
            <span class="index-num">U${padTwo(u.id)}</span>
            <span>
              <span class="unit-row-title">${escapeHtml(u.title)}</span>
              <span class="unit-row-range">${escapeHtml(u.range)} &middot; ${stats.done} of ${stats.total} completed</span>
            </span>
            <span class="unit-row-stat">
              <strong>${stats.pct}%</strong>
              <span>${stats.done}/${stats.total}</span>
            </span>
          </button>
        `;
      }
      container.innerHTML = html;
    }

    function renderUnitChart() {
      const svg = document.getElementById(`unit-chart-svg`);
      if (!svg) return;

      const barX = 76;
      const maxW = 280;
      const barH = 18;
      const rowGap = 34;
      let svgHtml = ``;

      for (let i = 0; i < UNITS.length; i++) {
        const u = UNITS[i];
        const stats = getUnitStats(u.id);
        const y = 14 + i * rowGap;
        const doneW = stats.total > 0 ? Math.round((stats.done / 6) * maxW) : 0;
        const totalW = Math.round((stats.total / 6) * maxW);
        const pendW = Math.max(0, totalW - doneW);

        svgHtml += `
          <text x="4" y="${y + 13}" fill="var(--on-surface)" font-family="Geist Mono, monospace" font-size="11" font-weight="600">${u.numeral}</text>
          <rect x="${barX}" y="${y}" width="${totalW}" height="${barH}" rx="2" fill="var(--data-context)"></rect>
          ${doneW > 0 ? `<rect x="${barX}" y="${y}" width="${doneW}" height="${barH}" rx="2" fill="var(--data-3)"></rect>` : ``}
          ${pendW > 0 && doneW > 0 ? `<line x1="${barX + doneW}" y1="${y}" x2="${barX + doneW}" y2="${y + barH}" stroke="var(--surface-container-low)" stroke-width="1.5"></line>` : ``}
          <text x="${barX + totalW + 10}" y="${y + 13}" fill="var(--on-surface-variant)" font-family="Geist Mono, monospace" font-size="11">${stats.done}/${stats.total} (${stats.pct}%)</text>
        `;
      }
      svg.innerHTML = svgHtml;
    }

    function matchesFilters(ch) {
      if (appState.unitFilter !== `all` && String(ch.unitId) !== appState.unitFilter) {
        return false;
      }
      const isDone = appState.completedIds.indexOf(ch.id) !== -1;
      if (appState.statusFilter === `completed` && !isDone) return false;
      if (appState.statusFilter === `pending` && isDone) return false;

      const q = appState.searchQuery.trim().toLowerCase();
      if (!q) return true;

      if (ch.title.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
      if (ch.articles.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
      if (ch.summary.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
      for (let i = 0; i < ch.subtopics.length; i++) {
        const st = ch.subtopics[i];
        if (st.title.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
        if (st.articles.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
        if (st.summary && st.summary.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
        if (st.landmark.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
        if (st.takeaway.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
        for (let j = 0; j < st.points.length; j++) {
          if (st.points[j].toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1) return true;
        }
      }
      return false;
    }

    function getNextSubtopic(chapterId, subIdx) {
      const ch = CHAPTERS.find(function (c) { return c.id === chapterId; });
      if (!ch) return null;
      if (subIdx + 1 < ch.subtopics.length) {
        return { chapterId: ch.id, index: subIdx + 1, st: ch.subtopics[subIdx + 1] };
      }
      const chIdx = CHAPTERS.indexOf(ch);
      const nextCh = CHAPTERS[chIdx + 1];
      if (nextCh && nextCh.subtopics.length > 0) {
        return { chapterId: nextCh.id, index: 0, st: nextCh.subtopics[0] };
      }
      return null;
    }

    function renderSyllabus() {
      const container = document.getElementById(`syllabus-container`);
      if (!container) return;

      const filtered = CHAPTERS.filter(matchesFilters);
      if (filtered.length === 0) {
        container.innerHTML = `
          <div class="empty-state">
            <h2 class="panel-title">No Matching Chapters Found</h2>
            <p>Your active search query or unit/status filter returned zero syllabus chapters.</p>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" onclick="clearAllFilters()">Clear All Filters</button>
          </div>
        `;
        return;
      }

      let html = ``;
      for (let uIdx = 0; uIdx < UNITS.length; uIdx++) {
        const unit = UNITS[uIdx];
        const unitChapters = filtered.filter(function (c) { return c.unitId === unit.id; });
        if (unitChapters.length === 0) continue;

        const unitStats = getUnitStats(unit.id);
        html += `
          <section class="unit-group" aria-label="${escapeHtml(unit.numeral)}: ${escapeHtml(unit.title)}">
            <div class="unit-header-bar">
              <div class="unit-header-left">
                <span class="dossier-pill">${escapeHtml(unit.numeral)}</span>
                <h2 class="unit-heading">${escapeHtml(unit.title)}</h2>
              </div>
              <span class="unit-count-meta">${unitStats.done} of ${unitStats.total} Completed &middot; ${escapeHtml(unit.range)}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="chapters-stack">
        `;

        for (let cIdx = 0; cIdx < unitChapters.length; cIdx++) {
          const ch = unitChapters[cIdx];
          const isCompleted = appState.completedIds.indexOf(ch.id) !== -1;
          const isExpanded = appState.expandedChapters.indexOf(ch.id) !== -1;
          const statusClass = isCompleted ? `completed` : `pending`;
          const statusLabel = isCompleted ? `Completed` : `Pending`;
          const toggleLabel = isCompleted ? `Mark Pending` : `Mark Completed`;

          html += `
            <article class="chapter-card chapter-card--${statusClass}" id="chapter-card-${ch.id}">
              <div class="chapter-header">
                <button type="button" class="chapter-toggle-main" aria-expanded="${isExpanded ? `true` : `false`}" aria-controls="chapter-body-${ch.id}" onclick="toggleChapterExpand(${ch.id})">
                  <span class="chapter-num-badge">${padTwo(ch.id)}</span>
                  <span class="chapter-title-wrap">
                    <span class="chapter-title">${escapeHtml(ch.title)}</span>
                    <span class="chapter-meta-row">
                      <span>${escapeHtml(ch.articles)}</span>
                      <span>&middot;</span>
                      <span>${ch.subtopics.length} ${isCompleted ? `Detailed Sub-Topic Notes` : `Planned Sub-Topics`}</span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                </button>
                <div class="chapter-actions">
                  <span class="status-badge status-badge--${statusClass}">
                    ${isCompleted
                      ? `<svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"></polyline></svg>`
                      : `<svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"></circle><polyline points="12 7 12 12 15 14"></polyline></svg>`
                    }
                    ${statusLabel}
                  </span>
                  <button type="button" class="status-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleChapterStatus(${ch.id})">${toggleLabel}</button>
                  <button type="button" class="expand-chevron-btn" aria-label="${isExpanded ? `Collapse` : `Expand`} study notes for ${escapeHtml(ch.title)}" aria-expanded="${isExpanded ? `true` : `false`}" onclick="toggleChapterExpand(${ch.id})">
                    <svg class="chevron-icon ${isExpanded ? `chevron-icon--open` : ``}" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
                  </button>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="chapter-body" id="chapter-body-${ch.id}" ${isExpanded ? `` : `hidden`}>
                <div class="chapter-summary-strip">
                  <div class="chapter-summary-text">${escapeHtml(ch.summary)}</div>
                  <div class="subtopic-controls">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm" onclick="expandChapterSubtopics(${ch.id})">Expand All Sub-Topics</button>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm" onclick="collapseChapterSubtopics(${ch.id})">Collapse Sub-Topics</button>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="subtopic-list">
          `;

          for (let sIdx = 0; sIdx < ch.subtopics.length; sIdx++) {
            const st = ch.subtopics[sIdx];
            const stKey = `${ch.id}-${sIdx}`;
            const stOpen = appState.expandedSubtopics.indexOf(stKey) !== -1;
            const nextTarget = getNextSubtopic(ch.id, sIdx);

            html += `
                  <div class="subtopic-card">
                    <button type="button" class="subtopic-trigger" aria-expanded="${stOpen ? `true` : `false`}" aria-controls="subtopic-content-${stKey}" onclick="toggleSubtopic('${stKey}')">
                      <span class="subtopic-trigger-left">
                        <span class="subtopic-code">${escapeHtml(st.code)}</span>
                        <span class="subtopic-name">${escapeHtml(st.title)}</span>
                        <span class="subtopic-articles">${escapeHtml(st.articles)}</span>
                      </span>
                      <svg class="chevron-icon ${stOpen ? `chevron-icon--open` : ``}" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
                    </button>
                    <div class="subtopic-content" id="subtopic-content-${stKey}" ${stOpen ? `` : `hidden`}>
                      ${st.story ? `<div class="story-memory">
                        <div class="story-memory-head">
                          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 5.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 3H20v18H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 18.5z"></path><path d="M8 7h8M8 11h8"></path></svg>
                          Story Memory Thread
                          ${myNoteMarkup(stKey)}
                        </div>
                        <p class="story-memory-text">${escapeHtml(st.story)}</p>
                        ${st.storyLink ? `<p class="story-next-topic">
                          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 6h9a3 3 0 0 1 3 3v5"></path><path d="M17 18H8a3 3 0 0 1-3-3V7"></path><path d="M3 13l4 4 4-4"></path></svg>
                          <span class="story-next-topic-body">
                            <span><strong>Link to Next Topic:</strong> ${nextTarget ? `<button type="button" class="story-next-topic-link" onclick="jumpToSubtopic(${nextTarget.chapterId}, ${nextTarget.index})">${escapeHtml(nextTarget.st.code)} &middot; ${escapeHtml(nextTarget.st.title)}</button>` : `End of the thread`}</span>
                            <span class="story-next-topic-text">${escapeHtml(st.storyLink)}</span>
                          </span>
                        </p>` : ``}
                        <div class="story-anchor">
                          <div class="story-anchor-head">
                            <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18h6"></path><path d="M10 21h4"></path><path d="M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-3.5 10.9c.6.4 1 1.1 1 1.9V16h5v-.2c0-.8.4-1.5 1-1.9A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"></path></svg>
                            Key Exam Anchor
                          </div>
                          <div class="story-anchor-row">
                            <span class="story-anchor-label">Landmark:</span>
                            <span class="story-anchor-text">${st.landmark}</span>
                          </div>
                          <div class="story-anchor-row">
                            <span class="story-anchor-label">High-Yield Takeaway:</span>
                            <span class="story-anchor-text">${st.takeaway}</span>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                      </div>` : st.summary ? `<div class="story-memory"><div class="story-memory-head">Story Memory Thread${myNoteMarkup(stKey)}</div><p class="story-memory-text">${escapeHtml(st.summary)}</p></div>` : ``}
                      <ul class="notes-points">
                        ${st.points.map(function (pt) { return `<li>${pt}</li>`; }).join(``)}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>
            `;
          }

          html += `
                </div>
              </div>
            </article>
          `;
        }

        html += `
            </div>
          </section>
        `;
      }

      container.innerHTML = html;
    }

    function syncFilterControls() {
      const unitSelect = document.getElementById(`unit-select`);
      if (unitSelect) unitSelect.value = appState.unitFilter;

      const searchEl = document.getElementById(`search-input`);
      if (searchEl && searchEl.value !== appState.searchQuery) {
        searchEl.value = appState.searchQuery;
      }

      const statuses = [`all`, `completed`, `pending`];
      for (let i = 0; i < statuses.length; i++) {
        const s = statuses[i];
        const btn = document.getElementById(`filter-status-${s}`);
        if (btn) btn.setAttribute(`aria-pressed`, appState.statusFilter === s ? `true` : `false`);
      }
    }

    function renderStoryMap() {
      const container = document.getElementById(`story-map-body`);
      if (!container) return;

      let html = `<p class="story-intro">The whole Constitution is one journey: a people frame a constitution, define who belongs and what rights they hold, build an executive and parliament, share power with the states, protect justice, govern locally, and keep the system alive through amendments, emergencies, and special provisions.</p>
        <p class="story-chain"><strong>Memory chain:</strong> Making &rarr; Features &rarr; Preamble &rarr; Union &amp; Citizenship &rarr; Rights &rarr; Duties &rarr; President &rarr; Prime Minister &rarr; Parliament &rarr; State Legislature &rarr; Federalism &rarr; Judiciary &rarr; Emergency &rarr; Local Government &rarr; UTs &rarr; Constitutional Bodies &rarr; Statutory Bodies &rarr; Amendments &rarr; Special Provisions.</p>`;
      for (let uIdx = 0; uIdx < UNITS.length; uIdx++) {
        const unit = UNITS[uIdx];
        const unitChapters = CHAPTERS.filter(function (c) { return c.unitId === unit.id; });
        if (unitChapters.length === 0) continue;

        html += `
          <section class="story-act" aria-label="${escapeHtml(unit.numeral)}: ${escapeHtml(unit.title)}">
            <div class="story-act-header">
              <span class="story-act-title">${escapeHtml(unit.numeral)} &middot; ${escapeHtml(unit.title)}</span>
              <span class="story-act-range">${escapeHtml(unit.range)}</span>
            </div>
        `;

        for (let cIdx = 0; cIdx < unitChapters.length; cIdx++) {
          const ch = unitChapters[cIdx];
          html += `
            <article class="story-node">
              <div class="story-node-head">
                <span class="story-node-index">${padTwo(ch.id)}</span>
                <span class="story-node-title">${escapeHtml(ch.title)}</span>
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm" onclick="openChapterFromStory(${ch.id})">Open notes</button>
              </div>
              <p class="story-node-text">${escapeHtml(ch.story || ch.summary)}</p>
              <div class="story-thread-list">
          `;

          for (let sIdx = 0; sIdx < ch.subtopics.length; sIdx++) {
            const st = ch.subtopics[sIdx];
            html += `
                <div class="story-thread">
                  <span class="story-thread-code">${escapeHtml(st.code)}</span>
                  <span class="story-thread-body">
                    <span class="story-thread-title">${escapeHtml(st.title)}</span>
                    <span class="story-thread-summary">${escapeHtml(st.summary || ``)}</span>
                  </span>
                </div>
            `;
          }

          html += `
              </div>
            </article>
          `;
        }

        html += `
          </section>
        `;
      }

      container.innerHTML = html;
    }

    function openChapterFromStory(chapterId) {
      appState.unitFilter = `all`;
      appState.statusFilter = `all`;
      appState.searchQuery = ``;
      if (appState.expandedChapters.indexOf(chapterId) === -1) {
        appState.expandedChapters.push(chapterId);
      }
      const firstKey = `${chapterId}-0`;
      if (appState.expandedSubtopics.indexOf(firstKey) === -1) {
        appState.expandedSubtopics.push(firstKey);
      }
      renderAll();
      persistState();
      setTimeout(function () {
        const card = document.getElementById(`chapter-card-${chapterId}`);
        if (card) card.scrollIntoView({ behavior: `smooth`, block: `start` });
      }, 60);
    }

    function renderAll() {
      syncFilterControls();
      renderKpis();
      renderUnitBreakdown();
      renderUnitChart();
      renderSyllabus();
    }

    // Hoisted interactive handlers
    function toggleChapterExpand(chapterId) {
      const idx = appState.expandedChapters.indexOf(chapterId);
      if (idx === -1) {
        appState.expandedChapters.push(chapterId);
        const firstKey = `${chapterId}-0`;
        if (appState.expandedSubtopics.indexOf(firstKey) === -1) {
          appState.expandedSubtopics.push(firstKey);
        }
      } else {
        appState.expandedChapters.splice(idx, 1);
      }
      renderSyllabus();
      persistState();
    }

    function toggleSubtopic(subtopicKey) {
      const idx = appState.expandedSubtopics.indexOf(subtopicKey);
      if (idx === -1) {
        appState.expandedSubtopics.push(subtopicKey);
      } else {
        appState.expandedSubtopics.splice(idx, 1);
      }
      renderSyllabus();
      persistState();
    }

    function jumpToSubtopic(chapterId, subIdx) {
      appState.unitFilter = `all`;
      appState.statusFilter = `all`;
      appState.searchQuery = ``;
      if (appState.expandedChapters.indexOf(chapterId) === -1) {
        appState.expandedChapters.push(chapterId);
      }
      const key = `${chapterId}-${subIdx}`;
      if (appState.expandedSubtopics.indexOf(key) === -1) {
        appState.expandedSubtopics.push(key);
      }
      renderAll();
      persistState();
      setTimeout(function () {
        const target = document.getElementById(`subtopic-content-${key}`);
        if (!target) return;
        target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: `smooth`, block: `center` });
        const card = target.closest(`.subtopic-card`);
        if (card) card.classList.add(`subtopic-card--jump`);
        setTimeout(function () { if (card) card.classList.remove(`subtopic-card--jump`); }, 1400);
      }, 60);
    }

    let myNoteActiveWrap = null;
    let myNotePinned = false;

    function openMyNote(wrap) {
      const popup = wrap.querySelector(`.my-note-popup`);
      const trigger = wrap.querySelector(`.my-note-trigger`);
      if (!popup || !trigger) return;

      if (myNoteActiveWrap && myNoteActiveWrap !== wrap) hideMyNote();
      myNoteActiveWrap = wrap;
      popup.classList.add(`is-open`);
      trigger.setAttribute(`aria-expanded`, `true`);

      popup.style.visibility = `hidden`;
      const rect = trigger.getBoundingClientRect();
      const popupWidth = popup.offsetWidth;
      const popupHeight = popup.offsetHeight;
      const margin = 12;
      let left = Math.min(rect.left, window.innerWidth - popupWidth - margin);
      left = Math.max(margin, left);
      let top = rect.bottom + 8;
      if (top + popupHeight > window.innerHeight - margin) {
        top = rect.top - popupHeight - 8;
      }
      top = Math.max(margin, Math.min(top, window.innerHeight - popupHeight - margin));
      popup.style.left = `${left}px`;
      popup.style.top = `${top}px`;
      popup.style.visibility = ``;
    }

    function hideMyNote() {
      if (!myNoteActiveWrap) return;
      const wrap = myNoteActiveWrap;
      myNoteActiveWrap = null;
      const popup = wrap.querySelector(`.my-note-popup`);
      const trigger = wrap.querySelector(`.my-note-trigger`);
      if (popup) popup.classList.remove(`is-open`);
      if (trigger) trigger.setAttribute(`aria-expanded`, `false`);
    }

    function toggleMyNote(button) {
      const wrap = button.closest(`.my-note-wrap`);
      if (!wrap) return;
      if (myNotePinned && myNoteActiveWrap === wrap) {
        myNotePinned = false;
        hideMyNote();
        return;
      }
      myNotePinned = true;
      openMyNote(wrap);
    }

    function copyTextToClipboard(text) {
      if (navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText) {
        return navigator.clipboard.writeText(text).catch(function () {
          return legacyCopyToClipboard(text);
        });
      }
      return legacyCopyToClipboard(text);
    }

    function legacyCopyToClipboard(text) {
      return new Promise(function (resolve, reject) {
        const area = document.createElement(`textarea`);
        area.value = text;
        area.setAttribute(`readonly`, ``);
        area.style.position = `fixed`;
        area.style.top = `-9999px`;
        document.body.appendChild(area);
        area.select();
        let ok = false;
        try {
          ok = document.execCommand(`copy`);
        } catch (err) {
          ok = false;
        }
        document.body.removeChild(area);
        if (ok) resolve();
        else reject(new Error(`copy failed`));
      });
    }

    function copyRichToClipboard(html, text) {
      if (navigator.clipboard && window.ClipboardItem && window.isSecureContext !== false) {
        const item = new ClipboardItem({
          'text/html': new Blob([html], { type: `text/html` }),
          'text/plain': new Blob([text], { type: `text/plain` })
        });
        return navigator.clipboard.write([item]).catch(function () {
          return copyTextToClipboard(text);
        });
      }
      return copyTextToClipboard(text);
    }

    function copyMyNote(button) {
      const popup = button.closest(`.my-note-popup`);
      const editor = popup ? popup.querySelector(`.my-note-editor`) : null;
      if (!editor) return;
      const text = editor.innerText || editor.textContent || ``;
      if (!text.trim()) {
        setFeedback(`My Note is empty - add or paste something first.`);
        return;
      }
      copyRichToClipboard(editor.innerHTML, text).then(function () {
        const original = button.textContent;
        button.textContent = `Copied ✓`;
        setFeedback(`My Note copied with formatting - paste it into Google Chat.`);
        setTimeout(function () { button.textContent = original; }, 1600);
      }).catch(function () {
        setFeedback(`Copy failed - select the text in My Note and copy manually.`);
      });
    }

    function sanitizeNoteHtml(html) {
      const holder = document.createElement(`div`);
      holder.innerHTML = String(html);
      const blocked = holder.querySelectorAll(`script, style, iframe, object, embed, link, meta, form, input, button`);
      for (let i = 0; i < blocked.length; i++) blocked[i].remove();
      const all = holder.querySelectorAll(`*`);
      for (let i = 0; i < all.length; i++) {
        const attrs = Array.prototype.slice.call(all[i].attributes);
        for (let a = 0; a < attrs.length; a++) {
          const name = attrs[a].name.toLowerCase();
          if (name.indexOf(`on`) === 0) all[i].removeAttribute(attrs[a].name);
          if ((name === `href` || name === `src`) && /^\s*javascript:/i.test(attrs[a].value)) {
            all[i].removeAttribute(attrs[a].name);
          }
        }
      }
      return holder.innerHTML;
    }

    function insertNoteHtml(editor, html) {
      editor.focus();
      const selection = window.getSelection();
      const range = selection && selection.rangeCount ? selection.getRangeAt(0) : null;
      const template = document.createElement(`template`);
      template.innerHTML = html;
      const fragment = template.content;
      const lastNode = fragment.lastChild;

      if (!range || !editor.contains(range.commonAncestorContainer)) {
        editor.appendChild(fragment);
        return;
      }

      range.deleteContents();
      range.insertNode(fragment);
      if (lastNode) {
        const after = document.createRange();
        after.setStartAfter(lastNode);
        after.collapse(true);
        selection.removeAllRanges();
        selection.addRange(after);
      }
    }

    let myNoteSaveTimer = null;

    function scheduleMyNoteSave() {
      if (myNoteSaveTimer) clearTimeout(myNoteSaveTimer);
      myNoteSaveTimer = setTimeout(function () {
        myNoteSaveTimer = null;
        persistState();
      }, 500);
    }

    function readMyNoteEditor(editor) {
      const key = editor.getAttribute(`data-note-key`);
      if (!key) return;
      appState.myNotes[key] = editor.innerHTML;
      saveMyNotesToStorage();
      const wrap = editor.closest(`.my-note-wrap`);
      if (wrap) {
        wrap.classList.toggle(`my-note-wrap--filled`, !!editor.innerText.trim());
      }
      scheduleMyNoteSave();
    }

    document.addEventListener(`input`, function (event) {
      const editor = event.target.closest ? event.target.closest(`.my-note-editor`) : null;
      if (!editor) return;
      readMyNoteEditor(editor);
    });

    document.addEventListener(`paste`, function (event) {
      const editor = event.target.closest ? event.target.closest(`.my-note-editor`) : null;
      if (!editor || !event.clipboardData) return;
      const html = event.clipboardData.getData(`text/html`);
      const text = event.clipboardData.getData(`text/plain`);
      if (!html && !text) return;
      event.preventDefault();
      if (html) {
        insertNoteHtml(editor, sanitizeNoteHtml(html));
      } else {
        insertNoteHtml(editor, escapeHtml(text).replace(/\r?\n/g, `<br>`));
      }
      readMyNoteEditor(editor);
    });

    document.addEventListener(`mouseover`, function (event) {
      const wrap = event.target.closest ? event.target.closest(`.my-note-wrap`) : null;
      if (!wrap || wrap === myNoteActiveWrap) return;
      if (!myNotePinned) hideMyNote();
      openMyNote(wrap);
    });

    document.addEventListener(`mouseout`, function (event) {
      const wrap = event.target.closest ? event.target.closest(`.my-note-wrap`) : null;
      if (!wrap || wrap !== myNoteActiveWrap || myNotePinned) return;
      const to = event.relatedTarget;
      if (to && wrap.contains(to)) return;
      if (document.activeElement && wrap.contains(document.activeElement)) return;
      hideMyNote();
    });

    document.addEventListener(`focusin`, function (event) {
      const wrap = event.target.closest ? event.target.closest(`.my-note-wrap`) : null;
      if (wrap) openMyNote(wrap);
    });

    document.addEventListener(`focusout`, function (event) {
      if (!myNoteActiveWrap || myNotePinned) return;
      const wrap = myNoteActiveWrap;
      const to = event.relatedTarget;
      if (to && wrap.contains(to)) return;
      hideMyNote();
    });

    document.addEventListener(`click`, function (event) {
      if (!myNoteActiveWrap) return;
      if (event.target.closest && event.target.closest(`.my-note-wrap`)) return;
      myNotePinned = false;
      hideMyNote();
    });

    window.addEventListener(`scroll`, function (event) {
      if (myNoteActiveWrap && event.target && event.target.nodeType === 1 && myNoteActiveWrap.contains(event.target)) return;
      hideMyNote();
    }, true);
    window.addEventListener(`resize`, function () { hideMyNote(); });

    function expandChapterSubtopics(chapterId) {
      const ch = CHAPTERS.find(function (c) { return c.id === chapterId; });
      if (!ch) return;
      for (let i = 0; i < ch.subtopics.length; i++) {
        const key = `${chapterId}-${i}`;
        if (appState.expandedSubtopics.indexOf(key) === -1) {
          appState.expandedSubtopics.push(key);
        }
      }
      renderSyllabus();
      persistState();
    }

    function collapseChapterSubtopics(chapterId) {
      const prefix = `${chapterId}-`;
      appState.expandedSubtopics = appState.expandedSubtopics.filter(function (k) {
        return k.indexOf(prefix) !== 0;
      });
      renderSyllabus();
      persistState();
    }

    function toggleChapterStatus(chapterId) {
      const idx = appState.completedIds.indexOf(chapterId);
      if (idx === -1) {
        appState.completedIds.push(chapterId);
        appState.completedIds.sort(function (a, b) { return a - b; });
        setFeedback(`Marked Chapter ${padTwo(chapterId)} as Completed.`);
      } else {
        appState.completedIds.splice(idx, 1);
        setFeedback(`Marked Chapter ${padTwo(chapterId)} as Pending.`);
      }
      renderAll();
      persistState();
    }

    function expandCompletedChapters() {
      for (let i = 0; i < appState.completedIds.length; i++) {
        const id = appState.completedIds[i];
        if (appState.expandedChapters.indexOf(id) === -1) {
          appState.expandedChapters.push(id);
        }
        const firstKey = `${id}-0`;
        if (appState.expandedSubtopics.indexOf(firstKey) === -1) {
          appState.expandedSubtopics.push(firstKey);
        }
      }
      setFeedback(`Expanded all ${appState.completedIds.length} completed chapters.`);
      renderSyllabus();
      persistState();
    }

    function collapseAllChapters() {
      appState.expandedChapters = [];
      appState.expandedSubtopics = [];
      setFeedback(`Collapsed all chapter and sub-topic panels.`);
      renderSyllabus();
      persistState();
    }

    function handleSearchInput(val) {
      appState.searchQuery = val;
      if (val.trim().length > 1) {
        const matches = CHAPTERS.filter(matchesFilters);
        for (let i = 0; i < matches.length; i++) {
          const ch = matches[i];
          if (appState.expandedChapters.indexOf(ch.id) === -1) {
            appState.expandedChapters.push(ch.id);
          }
        }
      }
      renderSyllabus();
      persistState();
    }

    function handleUnitSelect(val) {
      appState.unitFilter = val;
      renderAll();
      persistState();
    }

    function toggleUnitRowFilter(unitId) {
      appState.unitFilter = appState.unitFilter === unitId ? `all` : unitId;
      renderAll();
      persistState();
    }

    function handleStatusFilter(status) {
      appState.statusFilter = status;
      renderAll();
      persistState();
    }

    function clearAllFilters() {
      appState.unitFilter = `all`;
      appState.statusFilter = `all`;
      appState.searchQuery = ``;
      setFeedback(`Cleared search and syllabus filters.`);
      renderAll();
      persistState();
    }

    function handleResetBaseline(btn) {
      if (!btn) return;
      if (btn.dataset.armed !== `true`) {
        btn.dataset.armed = `true`;
        btn.classList.add(`btn-warning-armed`);
        btn.textContent = `Confirm Reset to 58%?`;
        setFeedback(`Click again to restore Chapters 01–11 Completed and 12–19 Pending.`);
        return;
      }
      btn.dataset.armed = `false`;
      btn.classList.remove(`btn-warning-armed`);
      btn.textContent = `Reset to Baseline (58%)`;
      appState.completedIds = BASELINE_COMPLETED.slice();
      appState.expandedChapters = [1];
      appState.expandedSubtopics = [`1-0`];
      appState.unitFilter = `all`;
      appState.statusFilter = `all`;
      appState.searchQuery = ``;
      setFeedback(`Restored baseline progress: 11 of 19 chapters completed (58%).`);
      renderAll();
      persistState();
    }

    function downloadTextFile(filename, content, contentType) {
      if (window.notebookAppApi && typeof window.notebookAppApi.triggerFileDownload === `function`) {
        try {
          window.notebookAppApi.triggerFileDownload({
            content: content,
            contentType: contentType,
            encoding: `UTF-8`,
            filename: filename
          });
          return;
        } catch (err) {
          // Fall through to a direct browser download
        }
      }
      const blob = new Blob([content], { type: contentType });
      const objectUrl = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      const link = document.createElement(`a`);
      link.href = objectUrl;
      link.download = filename;
      document.body.appendChild(link);
      link.click();
      link.remove();
      setTimeout(function () { URL.revokeObjectURL(objectUrl); }, 1000);
    }

    function exportNotesJson() {
      const noteKeys = Object.keys(appState.myNotes);
      const payload = {
        app: `indian-polity-dashboard`,
        type: `my-notes`,
        version: 1,
        exportedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
        notes: appState.myNotes
      };
      const filename = `Polity My Notes.json`;
      downloadTextFile(filename, JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2), `application/json;charset=utf-8`);
      setFeedback(noteKeys.length
        ? `Exported ${noteKeys.length} note(s) to ${filename}.`
        : `No notes to export yet - downloaded an empty notes file.`);
    }

    function triggerNotesImport() {
      const input = document.getElementById(`notes-import-input`);
      if (!input) return;
      input.value = ``;
      input.click();
    }

    async function handleNotesImport(input) {
      const file = input.files && input.files[0];
      input.value = ``;
      if (!file) return;
      try {
        const parsed = JSON.parse(await file.text());
        const notes = parsed && typeof parsed === `object` && parsed.notes && typeof parsed.notes === `object`
          ? parsed.notes
          : (parsed && typeof parsed === `object` && !Array.isArray(parsed) ? parsed : null);
        if (!notes) throw new Error(`no notes`);

        let imported = 0;
        const keys = Object.keys(notes);
        for (let i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
          if (/^\d+-\d+$/.test(keys[i]) && typeof notes[keys[i]] === `string`) {
            appState.myNotes[keys[i]] = notes[keys[i]];
            imported++;
          }
        }
        saveMyNotesToStorage();
        persistState();
        renderSyllabus();
        setFeedback(imported
          ? `Imported ${imported} note(s) from ${file.name}.`
          : `No valid notes found in ${file.name}.`);
      } catch (err) {
        setFeedback(`Import failed - choose a JSON file exported from My Notes.`);
      }
    }

    function exportProgressCsv() {
      const rows = [`Chapter Number,Unit,Chapter Title,Articles,Sub-Topics Count,Status`];
      for (let i = 0; i < CHAPTERS.length; i++) {
        const ch = CHAPTERS[i];
        const unit = UNITS.find(function (u) { return u.id === ch.unitId; });
        const status = appState.completedIds.indexOf(ch.id) !== -1 ? `Completed` : `Pending`;
        const unitName = unit ? `${unit.numeral}: ${unit.title}` : `Unit ${ch.unitId}`;
        rows.push(`${padTwo(ch.id)},"${unitName}","${ch.title}","${ch.articles}",${ch.subtopics.length},${status}`);
      }
      const csv = rows.join(`\n`);
      downloadTextFile(`Polity Progress.csv`, csv, `text/csv;charset=utf-8`);
      setFeedback(`Exported 19-chapter progress report as Polity Progress.csv.`);
    }

    async function initDashboard() {
      try {
        if (window.notebookAppApi && typeof window.notebookAppApi.getAppState === `function`) {
          const saved = await window.notebookAppApi.getAppState();
          if (saved && Array.isArray(saved.completedIds)) {
            appState.completedIds = saved.completedIds;
            if (Array.isArray(saved.expandedChapters)) appState.expandedChapters = saved.expandedChapters;
            if (Array.isArray(saved.expandedSubtopics)) appState.expandedSubtopics = saved.expandedSubtopics;
            if (typeof saved.unitFilter === `string`) appState.unitFilter = saved.unitFilter;
            if (typeof saved.statusFilter === `string`) appState.statusFilter = saved.statusFilter;
            if (typeof saved.searchQuery === `string`) appState.searchQuery = saved.searchQuery;
            if (saved.myNotes && typeof saved.myNotes === `object`) appState.myNotes = saved.myNotes;
          }
        }
      } catch (err) {
        // Fall back to baseline state cleanly
      }

      const storedNotes = loadMyNotesFromStorage();
      appState.myNotes = Object.assign(storedNotes, appState.myNotes);

      renderAll();
      renderStoryMap();

      if (window.notebookAppApi && typeof window.notebookAppApi.notifyAppReady === `function`) {
        window.notebookAppApi.notifyAppReady();
      }
    }

    document.addEventListener(`DOMContentLoaded`, initDashboard);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
